window.__CONTENT_EN__=window.__CONTENT_EN__||{};window.__CONTENT_EN__[912]={"91200":{"example:91200:exzh":"There is no spare capacity for additional support.","example:91200:exzh2":"Companies now have less capacity left for investment."},"91201":{"example:91201:exzh":"Let me slip in a short digression here.","example:91201:exzh2":"A new idea came out of a digression."},"91202":{"example:91202:exzh":"They are spending their remaining years in their hometown.","example:91202:exzh2":"They devoted their remaining years to community activities."},"91203":{"example:91203:exzh":"Fill in the required information on the application form.","example:91203:exzh2":"There was an error in what the report stated."},"91204":{"example:91204:exzh":"I eat breakfast every morning.","example:91204:exzh2":"Today I had bread for breakfast.","grammar:91204:ex:trans":"I eat breakfast every morning.","grammar:91204:ex:structure":"毎朝、〔noun〕を＋食べます","grammar:91204:ex:explain":"「朝ご飯」 means breakfast; it is the most common everyday word (the more formal written word is 「朝食」). The sentence structure is simple: 毎朝 (every morning) + 朝ご飯を (breakfast) + 食べます (eat). 「食べます」 is the polite non-past form; with 「毎朝」 it describes a daily habit.","grammar:91204:ex:words:0:zh":"every morning","grammar:91204:ex:words:0:note":"まいあさ","grammar:91204:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91204:ex:words:1:zh":"Breakfast","grammar:91204:ex:words:1:note":"あさごはん","grammar:91204:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91204:ex:words:2:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91204:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91204:ex:words:3:zh":"eat","grammar:91204:ex:words:3:note":"たべます; masu-form (polite form) of 「食べる」","grammar:91204:ex:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91204:ex:points:0":"朝ご飯 = breakfast (朝 = morning + ご飯 = rice/meal); the same set includes 昼ご飯 (lunch) and 晩ご飯 (dinner)","grammar:91204:ex:points:1":"「を」 follows the thing being eaten; 食べます is the polite form (masu-form) of 食べる","grammar:91204:ex:points:2":"Time words meaning every ~, like 毎朝 (every morning), do not take 「に」 after them","grammar:91204:ex2:trans":"I had bread for breakfast today.","grammar:91204:ex2:structure":"〔noun〕に＋〔food〕を食べました","grammar:91204:ex2:explain":"The key here is 「朝ご飯に」: 「に」 means as breakfast, so the whole sentence is I had bread for breakfast today. If you said 「朝ご飯を食べました」, it would only mean I ate breakfast.","grammar:91204:ex2:words:0:zh":"today","grammar:91204:ex2:words:0:note":"きょう","grammar:91204:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91204:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91204:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91204:ex2:words:2:zh":"Breakfast","grammar:91204:ex2:words:2:note":"あさごはん","grammar:91204:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91204:ex2:words:3:zh":"(as ~)","grammar:91204:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91204:ex2:words:4:zh":"bread","grammar:91204:ex2:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91204:ex2:words:5:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91204:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91204:ex2:words:6:zh":"ate","grammar:91204:ex2:words:6:note":"たべました; the past form of 食べる","grammar:91204:ex2:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91204:ex2:points:0":"「朝ご飯にパンを食べる」 = to eat bread for breakfast; here 「に」 means as ~","grammar:91204:ex2:points:1":"食べました = the past form of 食べます (ます → ました)","exam:91204:zh":"Every morning at seven I eat bread and eggs for breakfast, and then I go to school.","exam:91204:why":"The proper meal you eat in the morning is 「朝ご飯」. 「昼ご飯」 is lunch, which doesn't fit 「毎朝七時」."},"91205":{"example:91205:exzh":"It's warm today, isn't it?","example:91205:exzh2":"This room is warm.","grammar:91205:ex:trans":"It's warm today, isn't it?","grammar:91205:ex:structure":"〔noun〕は＋〔い-adjective〕です＋ね","grammar:91205:ex:explain":"「暖かい」 describes weather or a room that feels pleasantly warm, like spring weather. Note that when talking about hot soup or food, you write it 「温かい」. The sentence-final 「ね」 shares a feeling with the other person and invites a reply such as 「そうですね」 (that's right).","grammar:91205:ex:words:0:zh":"today","grammar:91205:ex:words:0:note":"きょう","grammar:91205:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91205:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91205:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91205:ex:words:2:zh":"Warm","grammar:91205:ex:words:2:note":"あたたかい","grammar:91205:ex:words:2:pos":"adjective","grammar:91205:ex:words:3:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91205:ex:words:3:pos":"copula","grammar:91205:ex:words:4:zh":"right? (seeking agreement)","grammar:91205:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91205:ex:points:0":"暖かい = (of weather or air temperature) warm; for something that feels warm to the touch, use the other character 「温かい」 (same pronunciation)","grammar:91205:ex:points:1":"The sentence-final 「ね」 asks for the listener's agreement, like right? or isn't it? in English","grammar:91205:ex2:trans":"This room is warm.","grammar:91205:ex2:structure":"この＋〔noun〕は＋〔い-adjective〕です","grammar:91205:ex2:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91205:ex2:words:0:note":"; it must always be followed by a noun","grammar:91205:ex2:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91205:ex2:words:1:zh":"room","grammar:91205:ex2:words:1:note":"へや","grammar:91205:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91205:ex2:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91205:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91205:ex2:words:3:zh":"Warm","grammar:91205:ex2:words:3:note":"あたたかい","grammar:91205:ex2:words:3:pos":"adjective","grammar:91205:ex2:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91205:ex2:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91205:ex2:points:0":"部屋が暖かい = the room is warm; 暖かい can describe both weather and spaces","grammar:91205:ex2:points:1":"Adding 「です」 directly to an i-adjective makes it polite; you don't need 「な」","exam:91205:zh":"The heater is on, so even though it's cold outside, it's warm in this room.","exam:91205:why":"「暖かい」 fits the comfortable indoor temperature after turning on the heater. 「涼しい」 means cool, which isn't the result of heating the room here."},"91206":{"example:91206:exzh":"Who is that person?","example:91206:exzh2":"Um, excuse me. Where is the station?","grammar:91206:ex:trans":"Who is that person?","grammar:91206:ex:structure":"あの＋〔noun〕は＋誰ですか","grammar:91206:ex:explain":"Here 「あの」 is a demonstrative referring to a person or thing some distance from both speaker and listener. Japanese has three sets: 「この」 (this), 「その」 (that, near the listener), and 「あの」 (that, over there); 「あの」 is the farthest one. The sentence uses 「〜は誰ですか」 to ask who someone is.","grammar:91206:ex:words:0:zh":"that","grammar:91206:ex:words:0:note":"; refers to something far from both the speaker and the listener","grammar:91206:ex:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91206:ex:words:1:zh":"person","grammar:91206:ex:words:1:note":"ひと","grammar:91206:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91206:ex:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91206:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91206:ex:words:3:zh":"who","grammar:91206:ex:words:3:note":"だれ","grammar:91206:ex:words:3:pos":"question word","grammar:91206:ex:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91206:ex:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91206:ex:words:5:zh":"(question)","grammar:91206:ex:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91206:ex:points:0":"「あの」 must be followed by a noun: あの人 (that person), あの店 (that shop)","grammar:91206:ex:points:1":"誰ですか = who is it? Use 「誰」 for people; the more polite option is 「どなた」","grammar:91206:ex2:trans":"Um, excuse me — where is the station?","grammar:91206:ex2:structure":"あの、すみません。〔place〕はどこですか","grammar:91206:ex2:words:0:zh":"um, uh…","grammar:91206:ex2:words:0:note":"; a filler used when starting to address someone","grammar:91206:ex2:words:0:pos":"interjection","grammar:91206:ex2:words:1:zh":"embarrassed","grammar:91206:ex2:words:1:pos":"set phrase","grammar:91206:ex2:words:2:zh":"station","grammar:91206:ex2:words:2:note":"えき","grammar:91206:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91206:ex2:words:3:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91206:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91206:ex2:words:4:zh":"where","grammar:91206:ex2:words:4:pos":"question word","grammar:91206:ex2:words:5:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91206:ex2:words:5:pos":"copula","grammar:91206:ex2:words:6:zh":"(question)","grammar:91206:ex2:words:6:pos":"particle","grammar:91206:ex2:points:0":"Here 「あの」 is not 「あの」 + noun meaning that ~; it is the um, uh… you say before addressing someone.","grammar:91206:ex2:points:1":"「〜はどこですか」 = where is ~? The most basic pattern for asking directions","exam:91206:zh":"That building you can see on the mountain, far from both me and you, is the hotel.","exam:91206:why":"「あの」 refers to something far from both the speaker and the listener. 「その」 usually refers to something close to the listener."},"91207":{"example:91207:exzh":"It's raining today.","example:91207:exzh2":"On rainy days I stay at home.","grammar:91207:ex:trans":"It's raining today.","grammar:91207:ex:structure":"雨が＋降っています","grammar:91207:ex:explain":"「雨」 is a noun; to say it rains you say 「雨が降る」, and since the rain is the subject it takes 「が」. 「降っています」 is the te-form of 「降る」 plus 「います」, showing that it is raining right now.","grammar:91207:ex:words:0:zh":"today","grammar:91207:ex:words:0:note":"きょう","grammar:91207:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91207:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91207:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91207:ex:words:2:zh":"rain","grammar:91207:ex:words:2:note":"あめ","grammar:91207:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91207:ex:words:3:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91207:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91207:ex:words:4:zh":"is falling (right now)","grammar:91207:ex:words:4:note":"ふっています; 降る + ています","grammar:91207:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91207:ex:points:0":"雨が降る = to rain (the verb 「降る」 literally means to fall); for the rain stopping, use 「雨がやむ」","grammar:91207:ex:points:1":"「〜ています」 expresses something in progress right now","grammar:91207:ex2:trans":"On rainy days I stay at home.","grammar:91207:ex2:structure":"〔place〕に＋います","grammar:91207:ex2:words:0:zh":"rain","grammar:91207:ex2:words:0:note":"あめ","grammar:91207:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91207:ex2:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91207:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91207:ex2:words:2:zh":"day","grammar:91207:ex2:words:2:note":"ひ","grammar:91207:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91207:ex2:words:3:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91207:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91207:ex2:words:4:zh":"house","grammar:91207:ex2:words:4:note":"いえ","grammar:91207:ex2:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91207:ex2:words:5:zh":"（place of existence）","grammar:91207:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91207:ex2:words:6:zh":"to be (there); to stay","grammar:91207:ex2:words:6:note":"; the masu-form of いる","grammar:91207:ex2:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91207:ex2:points:0":"雨の日 = a rainy day; the same pattern gives 晴れの日 (a sunny day)","grammar:91207:ex2:points:1":"For a person being somewhere, use 「に＋います」; for a thing being somewhere, use 「に＋あります」","exam:91207:zh":"Drops of water were falling from the sky, so I opened an umbrella so I wouldn't get wet in the rain.","exam:91207:why":"What falls from the sky as drops of water is 「雨」. 「雪」 also falls from the sky, but it isn't liquid drops."},"91208":{"example:91208:exzh":"How many apples are there?","example:91208:exzh2":"How old is your child?","grammar:91208:ex:trans":"How many apples are there?","grammar:91208:ex:structure":"〔thing〕は＋いくつ＋ありますか","grammar:91208:ex:explain":"「いくつ」 is the question word for quantity; you answer with the ひとつ、ふたつ、みっつ counting style. This sentence uses 「〜はいくつありますか」 to ask how many ~ there are, a very common pattern when shopping or counting items.","grammar:91208:ex:words:0:zh":"apple","grammar:91208:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91208:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91208:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91208:ex:words:2:zh":"a few","grammar:91208:ex:words:2:pos":"question word","grammar:91208:ex:words:3:zh":"there is / are","grammar:91208:ex:words:3:note":"; the masu-form of ある","grammar:91208:ex:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91208:ex:words:4:zh":"(question)","grammar:91208:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91208:ex:points:0":"いくつ = how many; used to ask the number of things, answered with 一つ・二つ・三つ…","grammar:91208:ex:points:1":"For the existence of things use 「あります」; for people and animals use 「います」","grammar:91208:ex2:trans":"How old is your child?","grammar:91208:ex2:structure":"〔person〕は＋いくつですか","grammar:91208:ex2:words:0:zh":"(your) child","grammar:91208:ex2:words:0:note":"おこさん","grammar:91208:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91208:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91208:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91208:ex2:words:2:zh":"how old","grammar:91208:ex2:words:2:pos":"question word","grammar:91208:ex2:words:3:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91208:ex2:words:3:pos":"copula","grammar:91208:ex2:words:4:zh":"(question)","grammar:91208:ex2:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91208:ex2:points:0":"「いくつ」 can also ask age; 「おいくつですか」 is more polite","grammar:91208:ex2:points:1":"お子さん = someone else's child (respectful); for your own child, say 「子ども」","exam:91208:zh":"Please count the apples in the basket and tell me how many there are in total.","exam:91208:why":"「いくつ」 asks about a quantity. 「いくら」 usually asks about a price, which doesn't fit the request to 「数えて」."},"91209":{"example:91209:exzh":"I take medicine three times a day.","example:91209:exzh2":"It rained all day today.","grammar:91209:ex:trans":"I take medicine three times a day.","grammar:91209:ex:structure":"〔period〕に＋〔number of times〕＋〔action〕","grammar:91209:ex:explain":"When 「一日」 is read as 「いちにち」, it means one day, and it often appears in 「一日に〜回」 (～ times a day) to express frequency. Note that when 「一日」 is read as 「ついたち」, it means the first day of the month. Also, Japanese uses 「飲む」 for taking medicine, because medicine is usually swallowed with water.","grammar:91209:ex:words:0:zh":"one day","grammar:91209:ex:words:0:note":"いちにち","grammar:91209:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91209:ex:words:1:zh":"(within ～, expressing frequency)","grammar:91209:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91209:ex:words:2:zh":"three times","grammar:91209:ex:words:2:note":"さんかい","grammar:91209:ex:words:2:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91209:ex:words:3:zh":"medicine","grammar:91209:ex:words:3:note":"くすり","grammar:91209:ex:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91209:ex:words:4:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91209:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91209:ex:words:5:zh":"take (medicine)","grammar:91209:ex:words:5:note":"のみます: the masu-form of 飲む.","grammar:91209:ex:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91209:ex:points:0":"一日＝one day (read いちにち); 「一日に三回」＝three times a day","grammar:91209:ex:points:1":"For medicine, Japanese uses 「薬を飲む」 (to drink), not 「食べる」","grammar:91209:ex2:trans":"It rained all day today.","grammar:91209:ex2:structure":"一日中＋〔noun〕でした","grammar:91209:ex2:words:0:zh":"today","grammar:91209:ex2:words:0:note":"きょう","grammar:91209:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91209:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91209:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91209:ex2:words:2:zh":"one day","grammar:91209:ex2:words:2:note":"いちにち","grammar:91209:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91209:ex2:words:3:zh":"the whole ～","grammar:91209:ex2:words:3:note":"じゅう; attached after a time word, it means the whole span of time","grammar:91209:ex2:words:3:pos":"suffix","grammar:91209:ex2:words:4:zh":"rain","grammar:91209:ex2:words:4:note":"あめ","grammar:91209:ex2:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91209:ex2:words:5:zh":"was (past, polite)","grammar:91209:ex2:words:5:pos":"copula","grammar:91209:ex2:points:0":"一日中＝all day long (一日＋中); 中 is read as じゅう and means the whole period","grammar:91209:ex2:points:1":"雨でした＝it was rainy (past); the past form of 「です」 is 「でした」","exam:91209:zh":"Twenty-four hours is exactly one day.","exam:91209:why":"「一日」 means one day, which is twenty-four hours. 「一週間」 is seven days, longer than twenty-four hours."},"91210":{"example:91210:exzh":"The fifth is my birthday.","example:91210:exzh2":"I'm going to Osaka for five days.","grammar:91210:ex:trans":"The fifth is my birthday.","grammar:91210:ex:structure":"〔date〕は＋私の誕生日です","grammar:91210:ex:explain":"「五日」 is read as 「いつか」, and in this sentence it means the fifth day of the month. In Japanese the dates from the first to the tenth all have special readings, so you have to memorize them one by one. 「私の誕生日」＝my birthday, and 「の」 shows possession.","grammar:91210:ex:words:0:zh":"the fifth (of the month)","grammar:91210:ex:words:0:note":"いつか","grammar:91210:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91210:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91210:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91210:ex:words:2:zh":"I","grammar:91210:ex:words:2:note":"わたし","grammar:91210:ex:words:2:pos":"pronoun","grammar:91210:ex:words:3:zh":"'s","grammar:91210:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91210:ex:words:4:zh":"birthday","grammar:91210:ex:words:4:note":"たんじょうび","grammar:91210:ex:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91210:ex:words:5:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91210:ex:words:5:pos":"copula","grammar:91210:ex:points:0":"五日 is read as いつか and has two meanings: the fifth of the month and five days","grammar:91210:ex:points:1":"The dates from the 1st to the 10th have special readings: ついたち、ふつか、みっか、よっか、いつか……","grammar:91210:ex2:trans":"I am going to Osaka for five days.","grammar:91210:ex2:structure":"〔number of days〕間、〔place〕に行きます","grammar:91210:ex2:words:0:zh":"five days","grammar:91210:ex2:words:0:note":"いつか","grammar:91210:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91210:ex2:words:1:zh":"period, duration","grammar:91210:ex2:words:1:note":"かん; attached after a number of days, it means the period of ～","grammar:91210:ex2:words:1:pos":"suffix","grammar:91210:ex2:words:2:zh":"Osaka","grammar:91210:ex2:words:2:note":"おおさか","grammar:91210:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91210:ex2:words:3:zh":"(destination)","grammar:91210:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91210:ex2:words:4:zh":"go","grammar:91210:ex2:words:4:note":"いきます; masu-form of 行く","grammar:91210:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91210:ex2:points:0":"五日間＝a period of five days; adding 「間」 makes it clear that this is a length of time, not a date","grammar:91210:ex2:points:1":"行きます can express the future: here it means the speaker is going to go","exam:91210:zh":"Today is the fourth, so I'll send the package tomorrow, on the fifth.","exam:91210:why":"The day after the fourth is 「五日」. 「六日」 is two days after the fourth, so it can't mean tomorrow."},"91211":{"example:91211:exzh":"Five oranges, please.","example:91211:exzh2":"The child turns five this year.","grammar:91211:ex:trans":"Please give me five mandarin oranges.","grammar:91211:ex:structure":"〔thing〕を＋〔quantity〕＋ください","grammar:91211:ex:explain":"「五つ」 is the native Japanese way of counting, used for things that have no special counter (fruit, snacks, and so on), and it only goes up to 「十（とお）」. When shopping, 「〔thing〕を〔quantity〕ください」 is the most basic pattern, with the quantity word placed between 「を」 and 「ください」.","grammar:91211:ex:words:0:zh":"mandarin orange","grammar:91211:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91211:ex:words:1:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91211:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91211:ex:words:2:zh":"five","grammar:91211:ex:words:2:note":"いつつ","grammar:91211:ex:words:2:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91211:ex:words:3:zh":"please give me","grammar:91211:ex:words:3:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91211:ex:points:0":"五つ＝five of something; native Japanese counting: ひとつ、ふたつ、みっつ、よっつ、いつつ","grammar:91211:ex:points:1":"The quantity word goes directly before the verb with no particle: 「みかんを五つください」","grammar:91211:ex2:trans":"The child turns five this year.","grammar:91211:ex2:structure":"〔person〕は＋〔age〕に＋なります","grammar:91211:ex2:words:0:zh":"child","grammar:91211:ex2:words:0:note":"こども","grammar:91211:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91211:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91211:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91211:ex2:words:2:zh":"this year","grammar:91211:ex2:words:2:note":"ことし","grammar:91211:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91211:ex2:words:3:zh":"five years old","grammar:91211:ex2:words:3:note":"いつつ","grammar:91211:ex2:words:3:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91211:ex2:words:4:zh":"(to become ～)","grammar:91211:ex2:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91211:ex2:words:5:zh":"become","grammar:91211:ex2:words:5:note":"; the masu-form of なる","grammar:91211:ex2:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91211:ex2:points:0":"五つ can also mean five years old, and it is mostly used for a child's age","grammar:91211:ex2:points:1":"〜になります＝to become ～; for age, 「〜になる」 means to turn ～ years old","exam:91211:zh":"I bought two apples and then three more, so there are five in all.","exam:91211:why":"Two plus three is five, so 「五つ」 is the answer. 「六つ」 is six, which doesn't match the total in the sentence."},"91212":{"example:91212:exzh":"I always get up at seven.","example:91212:exzh2":"Thank you as always.","grammar:91212:ex:trans":"I always get up at seven.","grammar:91212:ex:structure":"いつも＋〔time〕に＋〔action〕","grammar:91212:ex:explain":"「いつも」 is an adverb meaning always or every time, and it usually comes before the verb. This sentence describes the speaker's daily habit. Note that 「七時」 is read as 「しちじ」, and 「に」 after a time means that the action happens at that time.","grammar:91212:ex:words:0:zh":"I","grammar:91212:ex:words:0:note":"わたし","grammar:91212:ex:words:0:pos":"pronoun","grammar:91212:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91212:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91212:ex:words:2:zh":"always","grammar:91212:ex:words:2:pos":"adverb","grammar:91212:ex:words:3:zh":"seven o'clock","grammar:91212:ex:words:3:note":"しちじ","grammar:91212:ex:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91212:ex:words:4:zh":"(point in time)","grammar:91212:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91212:ex:words:5:zh":"to get up","grammar:91212:ex:words:5:note":"おきます; the masu-form of 起きる","grammar:91212:ex:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91212:ex:points:0":"いつも＝always, usually; often used when talking about a daily habit","grammar:91212:ex:points:1":"A specific point in time takes 「に」: 七時に起きます","grammar:91212:ex2:trans":"Thank you for everything, as always.","grammar:91212:ex2:structure":"いつも＋ありがとうございます","grammar:91212:ex2:words:0:zh":"always, all the time","grammar:91212:ex2:words:0:pos":"adverb","grammar:91212:ex2:words:1:zh":"thank you (polite)","grammar:91212:ex2:words:1:pos":"set phrase","grammar:91212:ex2:points:0":"いつもありがとうございます＝thank you for everything, as always; a very common greeting between shop staff and coworkers","grammar:91212:ex2:points:1":"「ありがとうございます」 is more polite than 「ありがとう」","exam:91212:zh":"My father gets up at six every morning without exception.","exam:91212:why":"「いつも」 means always, which fits a habit with no exceptions. 「よく」 means often, which can still have exceptions."},"91213":{"example:91213:exzh":"This shop has all kinds of clothes.","example:91213:exzh2":"Thank you for all your help.","grammar:91213:ex:trans":"This shop has all kinds of clothes.","grammar:91213:ex:structure":"〔place〕には＋いろいろな＋〔noun〕があります","grammar:91213:ex:explain":"「いろいろ」 means that there are many kinds, all sorts, and it becomes 「いろいろな」 when it comes before a noun. The pattern 「〜には〜があります」 is used to say what there is in a place; here it introduces the wide variety of goods in the shop.","grammar:91213:ex:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91213:ex:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91213:ex:words:1:zh":"store","grammar:91213:ex:words:1:note":"みせ","grammar:91213:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91213:ex:words:2:zh":"（place of existence）","grammar:91213:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91213:ex:words:3:zh":"(emphasizing the topic)","grammar:91213:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91213:ex:words:4:zh":"various","grammar:91213:ex:words:4:note":"; いろいろ＋な modifies a noun","grammar:91213:ex:words:4:pos":"na-adjective","grammar:91213:ex:words:5:zh":"clothes","grammar:91213:ex:words:5:note":"ふく","grammar:91213:ex:words:5:pos":"noun","grammar:91213:ex:words:6:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91213:ex:words:6:pos":"particle","grammar:91213:ex:words:7:zh":"there is / are","grammar:91213:ex:words:7:note":"; the masu-form of ある","grammar:91213:ex:words:7:pos":"verb","grammar:91213:ex:points:0":"いろいろ takes 「な」 when it modifies a noun: いろいろな服 (all kinds of clothes)","grammar:91213:ex:points:1":"〔place〕に〔thing〕があります＝there is something in a place; adding 「は」 to make 「には」 makes the place the topic","grammar:91213:ex2:trans":"Thank you for all your help.","grammar:91213:ex2:structure":"いろいろ＋ありがとうございました","grammar:91213:ex2:words:0:zh":"in many ways, various things","grammar:91213:ex2:words:0:pos":"adverb","grammar:91213:ex2:words:1:zh":"thank you (for something in the past)","grammar:91213:ex2:words:1:pos":"set phrase","grammar:91213:ex2:points:0":"When いろいろ stands alone before a verb or a sentence, it works as an adverb and does not take 「な」","grammar:91213:ex2:points:1":"After something is over, you thank people with the past form 「ありがとうございました」","exam:91213:zh":"This shop has bags in all sorts of colors—red, blue, yellow, and more.","exam:91213:why":"「いろいろな色」 means all kinds of colors. 「同じ」 means the same, which doesn't fit the multiple colors listed, and it doesn't take 「な」 here either."},"91214":{"example:91214:exzh":"I went to the movie theater on the weekend.","example:91214:exzh2":"There's a movie theater near the station.","grammar:91214:ex:trans":"I went to the movie theater on the weekend.","grammar:91214:ex:structure":"〔place〕へ＋行きました","grammar:91214:ex:explain":"「映画館」 is the place where you watch movies, and 「館」 means a public building. 「へ」 after a place shows movement toward it; it is written 「へ」 but pronounced 「え」. Time words like 週末 usually do not take 「に」.","grammar:91214:ex:words:0:zh":"weekend","grammar:91214:ex:words:0:note":"しゅうまつ","grammar:91214:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91214:ex:words:1:zh":"Movie theater","grammar:91214:ex:words:1:note":"えいがかん","grammar:91214:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91214:ex:words:2:zh":"(direction)","grammar:91214:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91214:ex:words:3:zh":"went","grammar:91214:ex:words:3:note":"いきました; the past form of 行く","grammar:91214:ex:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91214:ex:points:1":"「へ」 shows direction and is pronounced 「え」; you can also say 「映画館に行きました」","grammar:91214:ex2:trans":"There is a movie theater near the station.","grammar:91214:ex2:structure":"〔place〕の近くに＋〔thing〕があります","grammar:91214:ex2:words:0:zh":"station","grammar:91214:ex2:words:0:note":"えき","grammar:91214:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91214:ex2:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91214:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91214:ex2:words:2:zh":"nearby","grammar:91214:ex2:words:2:note":"ちかく","grammar:91214:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91214:ex2:words:3:zh":"（place of existence）","grammar:91214:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91214:ex2:words:4:zh":"Movie theater","grammar:91214:ex2:words:4:note":"えいがかん","grammar:91214:ex2:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91214:ex2:words:5:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91214:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91214:ex2:words:6:zh":"there is / are","grammar:91214:ex2:words:6:note":"; the masu-form of ある","grammar:91214:ex2:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91214:ex2:points:0":"〔place〕の近く＝near ～; very often used to say where a building is","grammar:91214:ex2:points:1":"For buildings and things, use 「あります」 to say that something exists","exam:91214:zh":"To watch a new movie on a big screen, I went to the movie theater in front of the station.","exam:91214:why":"「映画館」 is a place where movies are shown. 「美術館」 mainly displays works of art, which doesn't fit the purpose of seeing a new movie."},"91215":{"example:91215:exzh":"Yes, this is my first time in Japan.","example:91215:exzh2":"Yes, that's fine.","grammar:91215:ex:trans":"Yes, this is my first time in Japan.","grammar:91215:ex:structure":"ええ、〔noun〕は初めてです","grammar:91215:ex:explain":"「ええ」 is a casual way to say yes when answering someone's question, and it sounds a little lighter than 「はい」. This sentence is probably answering 「日本は初めてですか」 (Is this your first time in Japan?): first agree with 「ええ」, then say 「日本は初めてです」.","grammar:91215:ex:words:0:zh":"yeah, that's right","grammar:91215:ex:words:0:pos":"interjection","grammar:91215:ex:words:1:zh":"Japan","grammar:91215:ex:words:1:note":"にほん","grammar:91215:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91215:ex:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91215:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91215:ex:words:3:zh":"the first time","grammar:91215:ex:words:3:note":"はじめて","grammar:91215:ex:words:3:pos":"adverb","grammar:91215:ex:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91215:ex:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91215:ex:points:0":"ええ＝yeah, yes; more casual and softer than 「はい」, and it is best to use 「はい」 with people older than you","grammar:91215:ex:points:1":"〜は初めてです＝this is the first time for ～","grammar:91215:ex2:trans":"Yeah, that's fine.","grammar:91215:ex2:structure":"ええ、いいですよ","grammar:91215:ex2:words:0:zh":"yeah","grammar:91215:ex2:words:0:pos":"interjection","grammar:91215:ex2:words:1:zh":"good","grammar:91215:ex2:words:1:note":"; the casual form of 「よい」","grammar:91215:ex2:words:1:pos":"adjective","grammar:91215:ex2:words:2:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91215:ex2:words:2:pos":"copula","grammar:91215:ex2:words:3:zh":"you know (letting the other person know)","grammar:91215:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91215:ex2:points:0":"ええ is used when agreeing to someone's request and sounds friendly","grammar:91215:ex2:points:1":"いいですよ＝sure, that's fine; the 「よ」 at the end makes it sound more certain and friendly","exam:91215:zh":"The teacher asked me, \"Is this your notebook?\" so I answered, \"Yes, it's mine.\"","exam:91215:why":"「ええ」 is a casual yes, which is consistent with 「私のです」. 「いいえ」 is a no, which would contradict the answer that follows."},"91216":{"example:91216:exzh":"Mr. Tanaka's wife is a teacher.","example:91216:exzh2":"How is your wife?","grammar:91216:ex:trans":"Tanaka's wife is a teacher.","grammar:91216:ex:structure":"〔person〕さんの奥さん＝～'s wife","grammar:91216:ex:explain":"「奥さん」 is how you refer to someone else's wife and carries a respectful tone, so you cannot use it for your own wife. 「田中さんの奥さん」＝Tanaka's wife. Note that 「先生」 means teacher (or doctor) in Japanese, not Mr. as the same characters mean in Chinese.","grammar:91216:ex:words:0:zh":"Tanaka (surname)","grammar:91216:ex:words:0:note":"たなか","grammar:91216:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91216:ex:words:1:zh":"a respectful title (for teachers, doctors, and so on)","grammar:91216:ex:words:1:pos":"suffix","grammar:91216:ex:words:2:zh":"'s","grammar:91216:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91216:ex:words:3:zh":"wife","grammar:91216:ex:words:3:note":"おくさん","grammar:91216:ex:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91216:ex:words:4:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91216:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91216:ex:words:5:zh":"teacher","grammar:91216:ex:words:5:note":"せんせい","grammar:91216:ex:words:5:pos":"noun","grammar:91216:ex:words:6:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91216:ex:words:6:pos":"copula","grammar:91216:ex:points:0":"奥さん＝someone else's wife; for your own wife, use 「妻（つま）」 or 「家内」","grammar:91216:ex:points:1":"さん after someone's name shows politeness; you never add 「さん」 to your own name","grammar:91216:ex2:trans":"How is your wife?","grammar:91216:ex2:structure":"〔person〕は＋お元気ですか","grammar:91216:ex2:words:0:zh":"(your) wife","grammar:91216:ex2:words:0:note":"おくさん","grammar:91216:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91216:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91216:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91216:ex2:words:2:zh":"well, healthy","grammar:91216:ex2:words:2:note":"おげんき; 元気 with 「お」 is more polite","grammar:91216:ex2:words:2:pos":"na-adjective","grammar:91216:ex2:words:3:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91216:ex2:words:3:pos":"copula","grammar:91216:ex2:words:4:zh":"(question)","grammar:91216:ex2:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91216:ex2:points:0":"奥さん can be used directly to ask about the other person's wife; you do not need to say 「あなたの」","grammar:91216:ex2:points:1":"お元気ですか＝how are you (asking about someone's well-being); 「お」 makes it more polite","exam:91216:zh":"The woman who is married to Mr. Tanaka is Mr. Tanaka's wife.","exam:91216:why":"「奥さん」 can be used to refer to someone else's wife. 「お姉さん」 means older sister and doesn't indicate a marriage."},"91217":{"example:91217:exzh":"My father drinks every evening.","example:91217:exzh2":"I can't drink much alcohol.","grammar:91217:ex:trans":"My father drinks alcohol every evening.","grammar:91217:ex:structure":"〔person〕は＋毎晩＋お酒を飲みます","grammar:91217:ex:explain":"「お酒」 can refer to any kind of alcohol, including beer and wine, or specifically to sake. In everyday conversation people almost always say 「お酒」 with the 「お」. When talking about your own father to other people, use 「父」; for someone else's father, use 「お父さん」.","grammar:91217:ex:words:0:zh":"(my) father","grammar:91217:ex:words:0:note":"ちち","grammar:91217:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91217:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91217:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91217:ex:words:2:zh":"every night","grammar:91217:ex:words:2:note":"まいばん","grammar:91217:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91217:ex:words:3:zh":"alcohol, sake","grammar:91217:ex:words:3:note":"おさけ","grammar:91217:ex:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91217:ex:words:4:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91217:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91217:ex:words:5:zh":"to drink","grammar:91217:ex:words:5:note":"のみます: the masu-form of 飲む.","grammar:91217:ex:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91217:ex:points:0":"お酒＝alcohol (any kind of drink); the 「お」 makes it sound more polite and more natural","grammar:91217:ex:points:1":"For drinking alcohol, use 「お酒を飲む」; 「父」 is the word you use when telling other people about your own father","grammar:91217:ex2:trans":"I cannot drink much alcohol.","grammar:91217:ex2:structure":"〔thing〕が＋あまり＋〜ません","grammar:91217:ex2:words:0:zh":"I","grammar:91217:ex2:words:0:note":"わたし","grammar:91217:ex2:words:0:pos":"pronoun","grammar:91217:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91217:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91217:ex2:words:2:zh":"alcohol, sake","grammar:91217:ex2:words:2:note":"おさけ","grammar:91217:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91217:ex2:words:3:zh":"(object of the potential form)","grammar:91217:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91217:ex2:words:4:zh":"(not) very ～","grammar:91217:ex2:words:4:note":"; followed by a negative","grammar:91217:ex2:words:4:pos":"adverb","grammar:91217:ex2:words:5:zh":"Cannot drink","grammar:91217:ex2:words:5:note":"のめません; negative potential form of 飲む","grammar:91217:ex2:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91217:ex2:points:0":"「お酒が飲めない」＝cannot drink alcohol; the potential form often takes 「が」","grammar:91217:ex2:points:1":"あまり＋negative＝not very much","exam:91217:zh":"Children must not drink alcohol such as beer or wine.","exam:91217:why":"Beer and wine are both 「お酒」. 「お茶」 is a drink too, but it doesn't include beer or wine."},"91218":{"example:91218:exzh":"I met a friend the day before yesterday.","example:91218:exzh2":"It rained the day before yesterday.","grammar:91218:ex:trans":"I met a friend the day before yesterday.","grammar:91218:ex:structure":"〔person〕に＋会いました","grammar:91218:ex:explain":"「一昨日」 is read as 「おととい」 and means the day before yesterday; the kanji reading is unusual, so memorize it carefully. The key point here is 「〜に会う」: when you meet a person, that person takes 「に」, which learners often mistakenly replace with 「を」.","grammar:91218:ex:words:0:zh":"the day before yesterday","grammar:91218:ex:words:0:note":"おととい","grammar:91218:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91218:ex:words:1:zh":"friend","grammar:91218:ex:words:1:note":"ともだち","grammar:91218:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91218:ex:words:2:zh":"(object)","grammar:91218:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91218:ex:words:3:zh":"met","grammar:91218:ex:words:3:note":"あいました; the past form of 会う","grammar:91218:ex:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91218:ex:points:1":"To meet a friend, Japanese says 「友だちに会う」, with 「に」 for the person, not 「を」","grammar:91218:ex2:trans":"It rained the day before yesterday.","grammar:91218:ex2:structure":"〔time〕は＋〔noun〕でした","grammar:91218:ex2:words:0:zh":"the day before yesterday","grammar:91218:ex2:words:0:note":"おととい","grammar:91218:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91218:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91218:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91218:ex2:words:2:zh":"rain","grammar:91218:ex2:words:2:note":"あめ","grammar:91218:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91218:ex2:words:3:zh":"was (past, polite)","grammar:91218:ex2:words:3:pos":"copula","grammar:91218:ex2:points:0":"一昨日は〜でした＝the day before yesterday was ～; use 「でした」 to talk about the weather in the past","grammar:91218:ex2:points:1":"The past form of a noun sentence: 雨です → 雨でした","exam:91218:zh":"Today is the tenth, so a letter that arrived on the eighth arrived the day before yesterday.","exam:91218:why":"「一昨日」 means two days before today. 「昨日」 means only one day before, which would be the ninth."},"91219":{"example:91219:exzh":"I came to Japan the year before last.","example:91219:exzh2":"The summer the year before last was hot.","grammar:91219:ex:trans":"I came to Japan the year before last.","grammar:91219:ex:structure":"おととし＋〔place〕に来ました","grammar:91219:ex:explain":"「おととし」 means the year before last; it is written 「一昨年」 in kanji, but in everyday writing it is often written in hiragana. It looks a lot like 「おととい」 (the day before yesterday); one is a year and the other is a day, so be careful not to mix them up. The time word 「おととし」 does not take 「に」.","grammar:91219:ex:words:0:zh":"the year before last (two years ago)","grammar:91219:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91219:ex:words:1:zh":"Japan","grammar:91219:ex:words:1:note":"にほん","grammar:91219:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91219:ex:words:2:zh":"(destination)","grammar:91219:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91219:ex:words:3:zh":"came","grammar:91219:ex:words:3:note":"きました; the past form of 来る","grammar:91219:ex:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91219:ex:points:1":"To come to a place, use 「〔place〕に来ました」","grammar:91219:ex2:trans":"The summer the year before last was hot.","grammar:91219:ex2:structure":"おととしの＋〔noun〕は＋〔i-adjective ta-form〕です","grammar:91219:ex2:words:0:zh":"the year before last","grammar:91219:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91219:ex2:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91219:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91219:ex2:words:2:zh":"summer","grammar:91219:ex2:words:2:note":"なつ","grammar:91219:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91219:ex2:words:3:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91219:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91219:ex2:words:4:zh":"was hot (past)","grammar:91219:ex2:words:4:note":"あつかった; the past form of 暑い","grammar:91219:ex2:words:4:pos":"adjective","grammar:91219:ex2:words:5:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91219:ex2:words:5:pos":"copula","grammar:91219:ex2:points:0":"おととしの夏＝the summer of the year before last; a time word plus 「の」 modifies a noun","grammar:91219:ex2:points:1":"Past form of an i-adjective: 暑い → 暑かったです (never 「暑いでした」)","exam:91219:zh":"This year is 2026, so the trip in 2024 was the year before last.","exam:91219:why":"「おととし」 means two years before this year. 「去年」 means only one year before, which would be 2025."},"91220":{"example:91220:exzh":"How old is your older brother?","example:91220:exzh2":"My older brother lives in Tokyo.","grammar:91220:ex:trans":"How old is your older brother?","grammar:91220:ex:structure":"〔person〕は＋何歳ですか","grammar:91220:ex:explain":"「お兄さん」 is used for someone else's older brother, or to address your own older brother at home. When you talk about your own older brother to other people, say 「兄」 instead. This sentence asks how old the other person's older brother is.","grammar:91220:ex:words:0:zh":"(your) older brother","grammar:91220:ex:words:0:note":"おにいさん","grammar:91220:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91220:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91220:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91220:ex:words:2:zh":"how old","grammar:91220:ex:words:2:note":"なんさい","grammar:91220:ex:words:2:pos":"question word","grammar:91220:ex:words:3:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91220:ex:words:3:pos":"copula","grammar:91220:ex:words:4:zh":"(question)","grammar:91220:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91220:ex:points:0":"お兄さん＝someone else's older brother, or your own when you address him at home; to other people, your own older brother is 「兄（あに）」","grammar:91220:ex:points:1":"何歳ですか＝how old?; the more polite version is 「おいくつですか」","grammar:91220:ex2:trans":"My older brother lives in Tokyo.","grammar:91220:ex2:structure":"〔person〕は＋〔place〕に住んでいます","grammar:91220:ex2:words:0:zh":"older brother","grammar:91220:ex2:words:0:note":"おにいさん","grammar:91220:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91220:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91220:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91220:ex2:words:2:zh":"Tokyo","grammar:91220:ex2:words:2:note":"とうきょう","grammar:91220:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91220:ex2:words:3:zh":"(the place of residence)","grammar:91220:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91220:ex2:words:4:zh":"lives (in)","grammar:91220:ex2:words:4:note":"すんでいます; 住む＋ています","grammar:91220:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91220:ex2:points:0":"お兄さん can also be used for your own older brother (within the family)","grammar:91220:ex2:points:1":"To live in a place, say 「〜に住んでいます」, using 「ています」 for the current state","exam:91220:zh":"Tanaka has a male sibling three years older than him, and that person is Tanaka's older brother.","exam:91220:why":"「お兄さん」 is used for someone else's older brother. 「弟さん」 is also a male sibling, but younger than the person."},"91221":{"example:91221:exzh":"Your older sister is pretty, isn't she?","example:91221:exzh2":"I went shopping with my older sister.","grammar:91221:ex:trans":"Your older sister is beautiful.","grammar:91221:ex:structure":"〔person〕は＋きれいですね","grammar:91221:ex:explain":"「お姉さん」 is used for someone else's older sister, and also to address your own older sister. It can also be used to call a young female shop assistant. 「きれい」 looks like an i-adjective but is actually a na-adjective, and its negative is 「きれいじゃないです」.","grammar:91221:ex:words:0:zh":"(your) older sister","grammar:91221:ex:words:0:note":"おねえさん","grammar:91221:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91221:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91221:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91221:ex:words:2:zh":"beautiful","grammar:91221:ex:words:2:note":"; na-adjective","grammar:91221:ex:words:2:pos":"na-adjective","grammar:91221:ex:words:3:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91221:ex:words:3:pos":"copula","grammar:91221:ex:words:4:zh":"right? (seeking agreement)","grammar:91221:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91221:ex:points:0":"お姉さん＝someone else's older sister; your own older sister is 「姉（あね）」","grammar:91221:ex:points:1":"きれい is a na-adjective (even though it ends in 「い」), so it takes 「です」 directly","grammar:91221:ex2:trans":"I went shopping with my older sister.","grammar:91221:ex2:structure":"〔person〕と一緒に＋〔noun〕に行きます","grammar:91221:ex2:words:0:zh":"older sister","grammar:91221:ex2:words:0:note":"おねえさん","grammar:91221:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91221:ex2:words:1:zh":"with","grammar:91221:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91221:ex2:words:2:zh":"together","grammar:91221:ex2:words:2:note":"いっしょ","grammar:91221:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91221:ex2:words:3:zh":"(made adverbial)","grammar:91221:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91221:ex2:words:4:zh":"shopping","grammar:91221:ex2:words:4:note":"かいもの","grammar:91221:ex2:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91221:ex2:words:5:zh":"(purpose)","grammar:91221:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91221:ex2:words:6:zh":"went","grammar:91221:ex2:words:6:note":"いきました; the past form of 行く","grammar:91221:ex2:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91221:ex2:points:0":"お姉さんと一緒に＝together with older sister; 「〜と一緒に」 is the fixed expression for doing something together","grammar:91221:ex2:points:1":"買い物に行く＝to go shopping; 「に」 shows the purpose of going","exam:91221:zh":"A female sibling three years older than Tanaka is his older sister.","exam:91221:why":"「お姉さん」 means older sister, matching both the female and the older condition. 「お兄さん」 is an older sibling too, but male."},"91222":{"example:91222:exzh":"My aunt's house is in Kyoto.","example:91222:exzh2":"The lady next door gave me some sweets.","grammar:91222:ex:trans":"My aunt's house is in Kyoto.","grammar:91222:ex:structure":"〔thing〕は＋〔place〕にあります","grammar:91222:ex:explain":"「おばさん」 can refer to a parent's sister or an aunt by marriage and other female relatives, and it can be written 「伯母さん・叔母さん」 in kanji. It can also be used to address a middle-aged woman you do not know, though calling someone that directly can sometimes be rude. This sentence uses 「〜は〜にあります」 to describe a location.","grammar:91222:ex:words:0:zh":"aunt","grammar:91222:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91222:ex:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91222:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91222:ex:words:2:zh":"house","grammar:91222:ex:words:2:note":"いえ","grammar:91222:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91222:ex:words:3:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91222:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91222:ex:words:4:zh":"Kyoto","grammar:91222:ex:words:4:note":"きょうと","grammar:91222:ex:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91222:ex:words:5:zh":"（place of existence）","grammar:91222:ex:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91222:ex:words:6:zh":"exist","grammar:91222:ex:words:6:note":"; the masu-form of ある","grammar:91222:ex:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91222:ex:points:0":"おばさん＝aunt (a parent's sister, an aunt by marriage, and so on); it can also be used for a middle-aged woman","grammar:91222:ex:points:1":"For a house or building in a place, use 「〜にあります」","grammar:91222:ex2:trans":"The woman next door gave me some sweets.","grammar:91222:ex2:structure":"〔person〕に＋〔thing〕を＋もらいました","grammar:91222:ex2:words:0:zh":"next door","grammar:91222:ex2:words:0:note":"となり","grammar:91222:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91222:ex2:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91222:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91222:ex2:words:2:zh":"aunt","grammar:91222:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91222:ex2:words:3:zh":"(from ～)","grammar:91222:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91222:ex2:words:4:zh":"Snacks, candy","grammar:91222:ex2:words:4:note":"おかし","grammar:91222:ex2:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91222:ex2:words:5:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91222:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91222:ex2:words:6:zh":"received, got","grammar:91222:ex2:words:6:note":"; the past form of もらう","grammar:91222:ex2:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91222:ex2:points:0":"隣のおばさん＝the woman next door (here a middle-aged neighbor, not a relative)","grammar:91222:ex2:points:1":"〔person〕に〔thing〕をもらう＝to receive something from someone","exam:91222:zh":"My aunt is my mother's older sister, and she is two years older than my mother.","exam:91222:why":"「おばさん」 can mean a parent's sister; here it is the mother's older sister. 「おばあさん」 means a grandmother or an elderly woman, which is a different family relationship from this one."},"91223":{"example:91223:exzh":"My grandmother is eighty years old.","example:91223:exzh2":"The old woman got on the bus.","grammar:91223:ex:trans":"Grandma is eighty.","grammar:91223:ex:structure":"〔person〕は＋〔age〕です","grammar:91223:ex:explain":"「おばあさん」 can refer to your own or someone else's grandmother, or to an elderly woman you do not know. Be careful not to confuse it with 「おばさん」 (aunt); the only difference is the long 「あ」. When talking about your own grandmother to other people, people often say 「祖母（そぼ）」.","grammar:91223:ex:words:0:zh":"grandmother","grammar:91223:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91223:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91223:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91223:ex:words:2:zh":"eighty years old","grammar:91223:ex:words:2:note":"はちじゅっさい","grammar:91223:ex:words:2:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91223:ex:words:3:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91223:ex:words:3:pos":"copula","grammar:91223:ex:points:0":"おばあさん＝grandmother, or an elderly woman in general","grammar:91223:ex:points:1":"For age, use 「〜歳です」","grammar:91223:ex2:trans":"The old woman got on the bus.","grammar:91223:ex2:structure":"〔vehicle〕に＋乗りました","grammar:91223:ex2:words:0:zh":"old woman","grammar:91223:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91223:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91223:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91223:ex2:words:2:zh":"bus","grammar:91223:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91223:ex2:words:3:zh":"(the vehicle you board)","grammar:91223:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91223:ex2:words:4:zh":"took (a ride)","grammar:91223:ex2:words:4:note":"のりました; the past form of 乗る","grammar:91223:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91223:ex2:points:0":"Here おばあさん refers to an elderly woman, not necessarily a family member","grammar:91223:ex2:points:1":"To ride a vehicle, say 「バスに乗る」, with 「に」 for the vehicle","exam:91223:zh":"Your friend's mother's mother is that friend's grandmother.","exam:91223:why":"「おばあさん」 can mean either grandmother—on your father's side or your mother's side. 「おじいさん」 means grandfather, so the gender is different."},"91224":{"example:91224:exzh":"I asked a police officer for directions.","example:91224:exzh2":"There's a police officer at the police box.","grammar:91224:ex:trans":"I asked a police officer for directions.","grammar:91224:ex:structure":"〔person〕に＋道を聞きました","grammar:91224:ex:explain":"「おまわりさん」 is a friendly word Japanese people use for police officers who patrol the streets or work at a police box, a warm and familiar way of saying police officer. Here 「聞く」 means to ask, and 「〔person〕に道を聞く」 means to ask someone for directions.","grammar:91224:ex:words:0:zh":"police officer","grammar:91224:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91224:ex:words:1:zh":"(the person being asked)","grammar:91224:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91224:ex:words:2:zh":"road","grammar:91224:ex:words:2:note":"みち","grammar:91224:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91224:ex:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91224:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91224:ex:words:4:zh":"asked","grammar:91224:ex:words:4:note":"ききました; the past form of 聞く","grammar:91224:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91224:ex:points:0":"おまわりさん＝a friendly word for a police officer, used by children and adults alike; the formal word is 「警察官」","grammar:91224:ex:points:1":"道を聞く＝to ask for directions; 「聞く」 means both to listen and to ask, and the person you ask takes 「に」","grammar:91224:ex2:trans":"There is a police officer at the police box.","grammar:91224:ex2:structure":"〔place〕に＋〔person〕がいます","grammar:91224:ex2:words:0:zh":"police box","grammar:91224:ex2:words:0:note":"こうばん","grammar:91224:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91224:ex2:words:1:zh":"（place of existence）","grammar:91224:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91224:ex2:words:2:zh":"police","grammar:91224:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91224:ex2:words:3:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91224:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91224:ex2:words:4:zh":"there is (a person)","grammar:91224:ex2:words:4:note":"; the masu-form of いる","grammar:91224:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91224:ex2:points:0":"おまわりさん often appears together with 「交番」 (police box)","grammar:91224:ex2:points:1":"For people being in a place, use 「います」, not 「あります」","exam:91224:zh":"I got lost, so I asked the police officer at the police box for directions to the station.","exam:91224:why":"「おまわりさん」 is a friendly way to address a police officer, which fits the police box situation. 「店員さん」 is a person who works at a shop."},"91225":{"example:91225:exzh":"I want to travel abroad.","example:91225:exzh2":"This school has a lot of foreign students.","grammar:91225:ex:trans":"I want to travel abroad.","grammar:91225:ex:structure":"〔place〕へ＋〔noun〕に＋行きたいです","grammar:91225:ex:explain":"「外国」 means a country other than your own, so 「外国へ旅行に行く」 means to travel abroad. There are two key points here: the 「に」 in 「旅行に行く」 shows purpose, and 「行きたい」 means want to go. 「〜たいです」 is used to talk about your own wishes.","grammar:91225:ex:words:0:zh":"abroad, a foreign country","grammar:91225:ex:words:0:note":"がいこく","grammar:91225:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91225:ex:words:1:zh":"(direction)","grammar:91225:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91225:ex:words:2:zh":"travel / trip","grammar:91225:ex:words:2:note":"りょこう","grammar:91225:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91225:ex:words:3:zh":"(purpose)","grammar:91225:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91225:ex:words:4:zh":"want to go","grammar:91225:ex:words:4:note":"いきたい; 行く＋たい","grammar:91225:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91225:ex:words:5:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91225:ex:words:5:pos":"copula","grammar:91225:ex:points:1":"〜に行く shows the purpose of going; the masu-form of a verb minus ます plus 「たい」＝to want to do ～","grammar:91225:ex2:trans":"This school has a lot of foreign students.","grammar:91225:ex2:structure":"〔place〕には＋〔noun〕が多いです","grammar:91225:ex2:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91225:ex2:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91225:ex2:words:1:zh":"school","grammar:91225:ex2:words:1:note":"がっこう","grammar:91225:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91225:ex2:words:2:zh":"（place of existence）","grammar:91225:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91225:ex2:words:3:zh":"(emphasizing the topic)","grammar:91225:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91225:ex2:words:4:zh":"foreign country","grammar:91225:ex2:words:4:note":"がいこく","grammar:91225:ex2:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91225:ex2:words:5:zh":"'s","grammar:91225:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91225:ex2:words:6:zh":"student","grammar:91225:ex2:words:6:note":"がくせい","grammar:91225:ex2:words:6:pos":"noun","grammar:91225:ex2:words:7:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91225:ex2:words:7:pos":"particle","grammar:91225:ex2:words:8:zh":"many","grammar:91225:ex2:words:8:note":"おおい","grammar:91225:ex2:words:8:pos":"adjective","grammar:91225:ex2:words:9:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91225:ex2:words:9:pos":"copula","grammar:91225:ex2:points:0":"外国の学生＝foreign students; you can also say 「留学生」 (international students)","grammar:91225:ex2:points:1":"〜が多いです＝there are a lot of ～; 「多い」 is an adjective, so you cannot put it directly before a noun as 「多い学生」","exam:91225:zh":"A country other than your own is called a foreign country.","exam:91225:why":"「外国」 means a country other than your own. 「国」 just means country and does not distinguish your own country from other countries."},"91226":{"example:91226:exzh":"Please write your name in katakana.","example:91226:exzh2":"Katakana is still difficult for me.","grammar:91226:ex:trans":"Please write your name in katakana.","grammar:91226:ex:structure":"〔thing〕を＋〔script〕で＋書いてください","grammar:91226:ex:explain":"「カタカナ」 is katakana; foreign names are usually written in katakana in Japan, so when you fill in forms you are often asked 「カタカナで書いてください」. 「で」 shows the means or script used, and 「書いてください」 is a very basic polite request.","grammar:91226:ex:words:0:zh":"name","grammar:91226:ex:words:0:note":"なまえ","grammar:91226:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91226:ex:words:1:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91226:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91226:ex:words:2:zh":"Katakana","grammar:91226:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91226:ex:words:3:zh":"(means, method)","grammar:91226:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91226:ex:words:4:zh":"write","grammar:91226:ex:words:4:note":"かいて；the te-form of 書く","grammar:91226:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91226:ex:words:5:zh":"please (do ～)","grammar:91226:ex:words:5:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91226:ex:points:0":"カタカナ＝katakana, mostly used for loanwords and foreign names; hiragana is 「ひらがな」","grammar:91226:ex:points:1":"「で」 shows what method is used: カタカナで書く＝to write in katakana","grammar:91226:ex:points:2":"te-form of a verb＋ください＝please (do ～)","grammar:91226:ex2:trans":"Katakana is still difficult for me.","grammar:91226:ex2:structure":"〔noun〕は＋まだ＋〔adjective〕です","grammar:91226:ex2:words:0:zh":"Katakana","grammar:91226:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91226:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91226:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91226:ex2:words:2:zh":"still / yet","grammar:91226:ex2:words:2:pos":"adverb","grammar:91226:ex2:words:3:zh":"difficult","grammar:91226:ex2:words:3:note":"むずかしい","grammar:91226:ex2:words:3:pos":"adjective","grammar:91226:ex2:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91226:ex2:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91226:ex2:points:1":"まだ＝still (it is still the case now)","exam:91226:zh":"The word 「テレビ」 is written in katakana, not in hiragana or kanji.","exam:91226:why":"「テレビ」 uses 「カタカナ」. 「ひらがな」 would be the kind of writing seen in 「てれび」."},"91227":{"example:91227:exzh":"Pour the coffee into the cup.","example:91227:exzh2":"This cup is cute, isn't it?","grammar:91227:ex:trans":"I pour coffee into the cup.","grammar:91227:ex:structure":"〔container〕に＋〔thing〕を入れます","grammar:91227:ex:explain":"「カップ」 usually means a cup with a handle for coffee or tea, unlike 「コップ」, a glass for water. The pattern 「〜に〜を入れる」 means to put something into a container, and it works for pouring coffee and for making tea.","grammar:91227:ex:words:0:zh":"cup","grammar:91227:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91227:ex:words:1:zh":"(the place it goes into)","grammar:91227:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91227:ex:words:2:zh":"coffee","grammar:91227:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91227:ex:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91227:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91227:ex:words:4:zh":"pour in, put in","grammar:91227:ex:words:4:note":"いれます; the masu-form of 入れる","grammar:91227:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91227:ex:points:0":"カップ＝a cup with a handle (coffee cup, mug); a glass is 「コップ」","grammar:91227:ex:points:1":"「〔container〕に〔thing〕を入れる」＝to put ～ into ～; 「に」 marks where it goes","grammar:91227:ex2:trans":"This cup is cute, isn't it?","grammar:91227:ex2:structure":"この〔noun〕は＋〔adjective〕ですね","grammar:91227:ex2:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91227:ex2:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91227:ex2:words:1:zh":"cup","grammar:91227:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91227:ex2:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91227:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91227:ex2:words:3:zh":"cute","grammar:91227:ex2:words:3:pos":"adjective","grammar:91227:ex2:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91227:ex2:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91227:ex2:words:5:zh":"right? (seeking agreement)","grammar:91227:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91227:ex2:points:0":"Common combinations with カップ: コーヒーカップ, マグカップ, 紙カップ","grammar:91227:ex2:points:1":"ね shows that you are sharing a feeling and want the other person to agree","exam:91227:zh":"I poured the hot coffee into a cup with a handle.","exam:91227:why":"「カップ」 is a cup for drinks such as coffee, and it often has a handle. 「皿」 is a plate, which doesn't fit the container for coffee here."},"91228":{"example:91228:exzh":"I have Japanese class on Tuesday.","example:91228:exzh2":"Next Tuesday is a day off.","grammar:91228:ex:trans":"I have Japanese class on Tuesday.","grammar:91228:ex:structure":"〔day of the week〕に＋〔noun〕があります","grammar:91228:ex:explain":"「火曜日」 is Tuesday, and the days of the week in Japanese use the seven characters 「月、火、水、木、金、土、日」. When saying what you do on a certain day of the week, add 「に」. 「授業があります」＝there is a class, and 「あります」 is also used for classes, meetings and other events.","grammar:91228:ex:words:0:zh":"Tuesday","grammar:91228:ex:words:0:note":"かようび","grammar:91228:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91228:ex:words:1:zh":"(point in time)","grammar:91228:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91228:ex:words:2:zh":"Japanese","grammar:91228:ex:words:2:note":"にほんご","grammar:91228:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91228:ex:words:3:zh":"'s","grammar:91228:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91228:ex:words:4:zh":"class","grammar:91228:ex:words:4:note":"じゅぎょう","grammar:91228:ex:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91228:ex:words:5:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91228:ex:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91228:ex:words:6:zh":"there is / are","grammar:91228:ex:words:6:note":"; the masu-form of ある","grammar:91228:ex:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91228:ex:points:0":"火曜日＝Tuesday; adding 「に」 after a day of the week means on that day","grammar:91228:ex:points:1":"日本語の授業＝Japanese class; 「授業」 means a class or a lesson","grammar:91228:ex2:trans":"Next Tuesday is a day off.","grammar:91228:ex2:structure":"来週の〔day of the week〕は＋休みです","grammar:91228:ex2:words:0:zh":"next week","grammar:91228:ex2:words:0:note":"らいしゅう","grammar:91228:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91228:ex2:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91228:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91228:ex2:words:2:zh":"Tuesday","grammar:91228:ex2:words:2:note":"かようび","grammar:91228:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91228:ex2:words:3:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91228:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91228:ex2:words:4:zh":"day off","grammar:91228:ex2:words:4:note":"やすみ","grammar:91228:ex2:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91228:ex2:words:5:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91228:ex2:words:5:pos":"copula","grammar:91228:ex2:points:0":"来週の火曜日＝next Tuesday; 「今週の」＝this week's, 「先週の」＝last week's","grammar:91228:ex2:points:1":"休みです＝is a day off; noun＋です","exam:91228:zh":"Today is Monday, so I'll go to the library tomorrow, on Tuesday.","exam:91228:why":"The day after Monday is 「火曜日」. 「水曜日」 is Wednesday, one day later than tomorrow."},"91229":{"example:91229:exzh":"I borrowed a book at the library.","example:91229:exzh2":"May I borrow your pen?","grammar:91229:ex:trans":"I borrowed a book at the library.","grammar:91229:ex:structure":"〔place〕で＋〔thing〕を借りました","grammar:91229:ex:explain":"「借りる」 means to borrow something from someone and use it, the opposite direction from 「貸す」 (to lend to someone); the two are easy to confuse. For example, 「友だちにペンを借りる」＝to borrow a pen from a friend. Here the 「で」 in 「図書館で」 marks the place where the action of borrowing happened.","grammar:91229:ex:words:0:zh":"library","grammar:91229:ex:words:0:note":"としょかん","grammar:91229:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91229:ex:words:1:zh":"(place of the action)","grammar:91229:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91229:ex:words:2:zh":"book","grammar:91229:ex:words:2:note":"ほん","grammar:91229:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91229:ex:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91229:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91229:ex:words:4:zh":"borrowed","grammar:91229:ex:words:4:note":"かりました; the past form of 借りる","grammar:91229:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91229:ex:points:0":"借りる＝to borrow (something comes in to you); to lend is 「貸す（かす）」","grammar:91229:ex:points:1":"Use 「で」 for the place where an action happens: 図書館で借りる","grammar:91229:ex2:trans":"May I borrow your pen?","grammar:91229:ex2:structure":"〔verb te-form〕＋もいいですか","grammar:91229:ex2:words:0:zh":"pen","grammar:91229:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91229:ex2:words:1:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91229:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91229:ex2:words:2:zh":"borrow","grammar:91229:ex2:words:2:note":"かりて; the te-form of 借りる","grammar:91229:ex2:words:2:pos":"verb","grammar:91229:ex2:words:3:zh":"(even if ～)","grammar:91229:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91229:ex2:words:4:zh":"okay, good","grammar:91229:ex2:words:4:pos":"adjective","grammar:91229:ex2:words:5:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91229:ex2:words:5:pos":"copula","grammar:91229:ex2:words:6:zh":"(question)","grammar:91229:ex2:words:6:pos":"particle","grammar:91229:ex2:points:0":"ペンを借りる＝(I) borrow a pen; since you are the one borrowing, use 「借りる」","grammar:91229:ex2:points:1":"〜てもいいですか＝may I ～?; used to ask for permission","exam:91229:zh":"You don't have to buy the library's books; you can borrow them for just two weeks.","exam:91229:why":"「借りる」 means to borrow something from another person or an organization and return it later. 「貸す」 is to lend something to someone else—the opposite direction."},"91230":{"example:91230:exzh":"I'll wait for my friend at the coffee shop in front of the station.","example:91230:exzh2":"The coffee at this coffee shop is good.","grammar:91230:ex:trans":"I wait for my friend at the coffee shop in front of the station.","grammar:91230:ex:structure":"〔place〕で＋〔person〕を待ちます","grammar:91230:ex:explain":"「喫茶店」 is a slightly old-fashioned coffee shop that also often sells sandwiches and sweets. 「駅の前の喫茶店」 links two 「の」: the coffee shop in front of the station. The person you are waiting for takes 「を」.","grammar:91230:ex:words:0:zh":"station","grammar:91230:ex:words:0:note":"えき","grammar:91230:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91230:ex:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91230:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91230:ex:words:2:zh":"front","grammar:91230:ex:words:2:note":"まえ","grammar:91230:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91230:ex:words:3:zh":"'s","grammar:91230:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91230:ex:words:4:zh":"café","grammar:91230:ex:words:4:note":"きっさてん","grammar:91230:ex:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91230:ex:words:5:zh":"(place of the action)","grammar:91230:ex:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91230:ex:words:6:zh":"friend","grammar:91230:ex:words:6:note":"ともだち","grammar:91230:ex:words:6:pos":"noun","grammar:91230:ex:words:7:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91230:ex:words:7:pos":"particle","grammar:91230:ex:words:8:zh":"wait","grammar:91230:ex:words:8:note":"まちます; the masu-form of 待つ","grammar:91230:ex:words:8:pos":"verb","grammar:91230:ex:points:0":"喫茶店＝a (traditional) coffee shop that also serves light meals; chain coffee shops are often called 「カフェ」","grammar:91230:ex:points:1":"To wait for someone, use 「〔person〕を待つ」, with 「を」 for the person","grammar:91230:ex2:trans":"The coffee at this coffee shop is good.","grammar:91230:ex2:structure":"〔shop〕の＋〔thing〕は＋おいしいです","grammar:91230:ex2:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91230:ex2:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91230:ex2:words:1:zh":"café","grammar:91230:ex2:words:1:note":"きっさてん","grammar:91230:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91230:ex2:words:2:zh":"'s","grammar:91230:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91230:ex2:words:3:zh":"coffee","grammar:91230:ex2:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91230:ex2:words:4:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91230:ex2:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91230:ex2:words:5:zh":"tasty, delicious","grammar:91230:ex2:words:5:pos":"adjective","grammar:91230:ex2:words:6:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91230:ex2:words:6:pos":"copula","grammar:91230:ex2:points:0":"喫茶店のコーヒー＝the coffee shop's coffee","grammar:91230:ex2:points:1":"「おいしい」 can be used for food and also for drinks, meaning tasty.","exam:91230:zh":"After shopping, I went into a coffee shop in front of the station to rest with a cup of coffee.","exam:91230:why":"「喫茶店」 is a shop where you can drink coffee, tea, and other beverages. 「本屋」 mainly sells books, which doesn't fit the purpose described here."},"91231":{"example:91231:exzh":"There are thirty students in the classroom.","example:91231:exzh2":"Please don't eat in the classroom.","grammar:91231:ex:trans":"There are thirty students in the classroom.","grammar:91231:ex:structure":"〔place〕に＋〔person〕が＋〔number of people〕います","grammar:91231:ex:explain":"「教室」 is a room where classes are held, and in Japanese it is also often used for cram schools or lessons, as in 「ピアノ教室」. This sentence uses 「〜に〜がいます」 to say that people are somewhere; the number 「三十人」 goes directly before 「います」 with no particle needed.","grammar:91231:ex:words:0:zh":"classroom","grammar:91231:ex:words:0:note":"きょうしつ","grammar:91231:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91231:ex:words:1:zh":"（place of existence）","grammar:91231:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91231:ex:words:2:zh":"student","grammar:91231:ex:words:2:note":"がくせい","grammar:91231:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91231:ex:words:3:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91231:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91231:ex:words:4:zh":"thirty people","grammar:91231:ex:words:4:note":"さんじゅうにん","grammar:91231:ex:words:4:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91231:ex:words:5:zh":"there is (a person)","grammar:91231:ex:words:5:note":"; the masu-form of いる","grammar:91231:ex:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91231:ex:points:0":"教室 = a classroom; it can also mean a lesson class, as in 「料理教室」 (cooking class)","grammar:91231:ex:points:1":"The number of people goes before the verb: 学生が三十人います","grammar:91231:ex2:trans":"Please don't eat food in the classroom.","grammar:91231:ex2:structure":"〔place〕で＋〜ないでください","grammar:91231:ex2:words:0:zh":"classroom","grammar:91231:ex2:words:0:note":"きょうしつ","grammar:91231:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91231:ex2:words:1:zh":"(place of the action)","grammar:91231:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91231:ex2:words:2:zh":"food","grammar:91231:ex2:words:2:note":"たべもの","grammar:91231:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91231:ex2:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91231:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91231:ex2:words:4:zh":"don't eat","grammar:91231:ex2:words:4:note":"たべないで; the nai-form of 食べる plus で","grammar:91231:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91231:ex2:words:5:zh":"please (do ～)","grammar:91231:ex2:words:5:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91231:ex2:points:0":"教室で食べる: doing something in the classroom takes 「で」; 教室に＋います (being there) takes 「に」","grammar:91231:ex2:points:1":"nai-form ＋ でください = please don't ～","exam:91231:zh":"Class was about to start, so the students went into their classroom and greeted the teacher standing in front of the blackboard.","exam:91231:why":"The class, the blackboard, and the teacher together point to 「教室」 as the place. 「図書館」 is also part of school life, but it doesn't fit a regular class being held here."},"91232":{"example:91232:exzh":"It will probably cloud over from the afternoon.","example:91232:exzh2":"It's been cloudy since this morning.","grammar:91232:ex:trans":"It'll probably get cloudy starting in the afternoon.","grammar:91232:ex:structure":"〔time〕から＋〔verb〕でしょう","grammar:91232:ex:explain":"「曇る」 is a verb meaning the sky turns from clear to cloudy. This sentence sounds like a weather forecast: 「午後から (starting in the afternoon)」＋「曇るでしょう (it will probably get cloudy)」. 「でしょう」 expresses a guess, not absolute certainty.","grammar:91232:ex:words:0:zh":"afternoon","grammar:91232:ex:words:0:note":"ごご","grammar:91232:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91232:ex:words:1:zh":"from","grammar:91232:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91232:ex:words:2:zh":"get cloudy","grammar:91232:ex:words:2:note":"くもる","grammar:91232:ex:words:2:pos":"verb","grammar:91232:ex:words:3:zh":"probably ～","grammar:91232:ex:words:3:note":"; a guess","grammar:91232:ex:words:3:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91232:ex:points:0":"曇る = (the sky) becoming cloudy; the opposite of 晴れる (to clear up)","grammar:91232:ex:points:1":"〜でしょう = probably ～; the most common phrasing in weather forecasts","grammar:91232:ex2:trans":"It's been cloudy since this morning today.","grammar:91232:ex2:structure":"〔time〕から＋曇っています","grammar:91232:ex2:words:0:zh":"today","grammar:91232:ex2:words:0:note":"きょう","grammar:91232:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91232:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91232:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91232:ex2:words:2:zh":"morning","grammar:91232:ex2:words:2:note":"あさ","grammar:91232:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91232:ex2:words:3:zh":"from","grammar:91232:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91232:ex2:words:4:zh":"cloudy (state)","grammar:91232:ex2:words:4:note":"くもっています; 曇る＋ています","grammar:91232:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91232:ex2:points:0":"曇っています = it is cloudy right now; weather states often use 「ています」","grammar:91232:ex2:points:1":"The noun 「曇り」 (cloudy weather) is also common: 今日は曇りです","exam:91232:zh":"According to the weather forecast, there will be more clouds tomorrow and the sky looks like it will turn cloudy.","exam:91232:why":"「曇る」 means the sky is covered by clouds and the weather turns cloudy. 「晴れる」 means to clear up, the opposite of having more clouds."},"91233":{"example:91233:exzh":"Three hundred grams of meat, please.","example:91233:exzh2":"This letter weighs fifty grams.","grammar:91233:ex:trans":"Please give me three hundred grams of meat.","grammar:91233:ex:structure":"〔thing〕を＋〔number〕グラム＋ください","grammar:91233:ex:explain":"「グラム」 is the unit gram, often used when buying things by weight at a butcher shop or supermarket. The quantity also goes directly before 「ください」. Note that 「三百」 is read 「さんびゃく」, 「六百」 is 「ろっぴゃく」, and 「八百」 is 「はっぴゃく」.","grammar:91233:ex:words:0:zh":"meat","grammar:91233:ex:words:0:note":"にく","grammar:91233:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91233:ex:words:1:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91233:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91233:ex:words:2:zh":"three hundred","grammar:91233:ex:words:2:note":"さんびゃく","grammar:91233:ex:words:2:pos":"numeral","grammar:91233:ex:words:3:zh":"gram","grammar:91233:ex:words:3:pos":"suffix","grammar:91233:ex:words:4:zh":"please (do ～)","grammar:91233:ex:words:4:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91233:ex:points:0":"グラム = gram (g); キロ = kilogram","grammar:91233:ex:points:1":"三百 is read さんびゃく (ひゃく becomes びゃく)","grammar:91233:ex2:trans":"This letter weighs fifty grams.","grammar:91233:ex2:structure":"〔thing〕は＋〔number〕グラムです","grammar:91233:ex2:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91233:ex2:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91233:ex2:words:1:zh":"letter","grammar:91233:ex2:words:1:note":"てがみ","grammar:91233:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91233:ex2:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91233:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91233:ex2:words:3:zh":"fifty","grammar:91233:ex2:words:3:note":"ごじゅう","grammar:91233:ex2:words:3:pos":"numeral","grammar:91233:ex2:words:4:zh":"gram","grammar:91233:ex2:words:4:pos":"suffix","grammar:91233:ex2:words:5:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91233:ex2:words:5:pos":"copula","grammar:91233:ex2:points:0":"Letters get weighed when you mail them, and the postage depends on how many grams it is.","grammar:91233:ex2:points:1":"「手紙」 means a letter, not toilet paper.","exam:91233:zh":"If you divide one kilogram of sugar into two portions of the same weight, each portion is 500 grams.","exam:91233:why":"One kilogram is 1,000 grams, so half of it is 500 「グラム」. 「キログラム」 is also a unit of weight, but 500 kilograms is far more than the original weight.","exam_official:91233:zh":"When I put the meat on the scale, it weighed 300 grams.","exam_official:91233:why":"「グラム」 is a unit of weight. 「リットル」 is a unit of volume, so it can't express the weight measured on a scale."},"91234":{"example:91234:exzh":"Mondays are always busy.","example:91234:exzh2":"There's a meeting on Monday morning.","grammar:91234:ex:trans":"Mondays are always busy.","grammar:91234:ex:structure":"〔day of the week〕は＋いつも＋〔adjective〕です","grammar:91234:ex:explain":"「月曜日」 is Monday; this sentence takes Monday as the topic with 「は」 and then says 「always busy」. 「いつも」 goes before the adjective to mean it is like that every time.","grammar:91234:ex:words:0:zh":"Monday","grammar:91234:ex:words:0:note":"げつようび","grammar:91234:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91234:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91234:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91234:ex:words:2:zh":"always","grammar:91234:ex:words:2:pos":"adverb","grammar:91234:ex:words:3:zh":"busy","grammar:91234:ex:words:3:note":"いそがしい","grammar:91234:ex:words:3:pos":"adjective","grammar:91234:ex:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91234:ex:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91234:ex:points:0":"月曜日 = Monday; 月曜日は = as for Monday(s)","grammar:91234:ex:points:1":"忙しい = busy; the past tense is 忙しかったです","grammar:91234:ex2:trans":"There's a meeting on Monday morning.","grammar:91234:ex2:structure":"〔time〕、〔activity〕があります","grammar:91234:ex2:words:0:zh":"Monday","grammar:91234:ex2:words:0:note":"げつようび","grammar:91234:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91234:ex2:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91234:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91234:ex2:words:2:zh":"morning","grammar:91234:ex2:words:2:note":"あさ","grammar:91234:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91234:ex2:words:3:zh":"meeting","grammar:91234:ex2:words:3:note":"かいぎ","grammar:91234:ex2:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91234:ex2:words:4:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91234:ex2:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91234:ex2:words:5:zh":"there is / are","grammar:91234:ex2:words:5:note":"; the masu-form of ある","grammar:91234:ex2:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91234:ex2:points:0":"月曜日の朝 = Monday morning; 「の」 links two time words","grammar:91234:ex2:points:1":"Meetings, events, and exams can all use 「があります」 to mean there is one.","exam:91234:zh":"Today is Sunday, so school starts again tomorrow, on Monday.","exam:91234:why":"The day after Sunday is 「月曜日」. 「火曜日」 is Tuesday, which isn't tomorrow."},"91235":{"example:91235:exzh":"Please turn right at the next intersection.","example:91235:exzh2":"There's a bank near the intersection.","grammar:91235:ex:trans":"Please turn right at the next intersection.","grammar:91235:ex:structure":"〔place〕を＋〔direction〕に曲がってください","grammar:91235:ex:explain":"「交差点」 is a place where roads cross; you often hear 「次の交差点を右に曲がってください」 in taxis or when asking for directions. Here 「を」 marks the place you pass through, not the object; 「右に」 shows the direction, to the right.","grammar:91235:ex:words:0:zh":"next","grammar:91235:ex:words:0:note":"つぎ","grammar:91235:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91235:ex:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91235:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91235:ex:words:2:zh":"intersection","grammar:91235:ex:words:2:note":"こうさてん","grammar:91235:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91235:ex:words:3:zh":"(the place you pass through)","grammar:91235:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91235:ex:words:4:zh":"the right side","grammar:91235:ex:words:4:note":"みぎ","grammar:91235:ex:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91235:ex:words:5:zh":"(direction)","grammar:91235:ex:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91235:ex:words:6:zh":"turn","grammar:91235:ex:words:6:note":"まがって; the te-form of 曲がる","grammar:91235:ex:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91235:ex:words:7:zh":"please (do ～)","grammar:91235:ex:words:7:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91235:ex:points:0":"交差点 = intersection (where roads cross); essential for asking directions and giving routes","grammar:91235:ex:points:1":"「交差点を曲がる」: the place you turn through takes 「を」, the direction takes 「に」","grammar:91235:ex2:trans":"There's a bank near the intersection.","grammar:91235:ex2:structure":"〔place〕の近くに＋〔thing〕があります","grammar:91235:ex2:words:0:zh":"intersection","grammar:91235:ex2:words:0:note":"こうさてん","grammar:91235:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91235:ex2:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91235:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91235:ex2:words:2:zh":"nearby","grammar:91235:ex2:words:2:note":"ちかく","grammar:91235:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91235:ex2:words:3:zh":"（place of existence）","grammar:91235:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91235:ex2:words:4:zh":"bank","grammar:91235:ex2:words:4:note":"ぎんこう","grammar:91235:ex2:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91235:ex2:words:5:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91235:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91235:ex2:words:6:zh":"there is / are","grammar:91235:ex2:words:6:note":"; the masu-form of ある","grammar:91235:ex2:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91235:ex2:points:0":"交差点の近く = near the intersection; often used to say where a shop is","grammar:91235:ex2:points:1":"Buildings use 「あります」 to say they exist","exam:91235:zh":"I waited for the light to turn green at the intersection where two roads cross in the shape of a cross.","exam:91235:why":"「交差点」 means an intersection where roads meet. 「橋」 is a structure that spans an obstacle such as a river, and it does not mean a place where roads meet."},"91236":{"example:91236:exzh":"When I don't know the way, I ask at the police box.","example:91236:exzh2":"There's a police box in front of the station.","grammar:91236:ex:trans":"When I don't know the way, I ask at the police box.","grammar:91236:ex:structure":"〜ときは＋〔place〕で＋〔action〕","grammar:91236:ex:explain":"「交番」 is a small police box on a Japanese street corner; besides keeping the peace, it also gives directions and handles lost property, so travelers often go there for help. The pattern 「〜ときは」 means when ～, followed by what to do at that time. The 「で」 in 「交番で聞く」 means asking there.","grammar:91236:ex:words:0:zh":"road","grammar:91236:ex:words:0:note":"みち","grammar:91236:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91236:ex:words:1:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91236:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91236:ex:words:2:zh":"don't know","grammar:91236:ex:words:2:note":"; the nai-form of わかる","grammar:91236:ex:words:2:pos":"verb","grammar:91236:ex:words:3:zh":"when ～","grammar:91236:ex:words:3:pos":"formal noun","grammar:91236:ex:words:4:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91236:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91236:ex:words:5:zh":"police box","grammar:91236:ex:words:5:note":"こうばん","grammar:91236:ex:words:5:pos":"noun","grammar:91236:ex:words:6:zh":"(place of the action)","grammar:91236:ex:words:6:pos":"particle","grammar:91236:ex:words:7:zh":"ask","grammar:91236:ex:words:7:note":"ききます; the masu-form of 聞く","grammar:91236:ex:words:7:pos":"verb","grammar:91236:ex:points:0":"交番 = police box; a small police post common on Japanese streets where you can ask for directions or report lost items","grammar:91236:ex:points:1":"plain-form verb ＋ とき = when ～; with 「わかる」 the thing understood takes 「が」","grammar:91236:ex2:trans":"There's a police box in front of the station.","grammar:91236:ex2:structure":"〔place〕に＋〔thing〕があります","grammar:91236:ex2:words:0:zh":"station","grammar:91236:ex2:words:0:note":"えき","grammar:91236:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91236:ex2:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91236:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91236:ex2:words:2:zh":"front","grammar:91236:ex2:words:2:note":"まえ","grammar:91236:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91236:ex2:words:3:zh":"（place of existence）","grammar:91236:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91236:ex2:words:4:zh":"police box","grammar:91236:ex2:words:4:note":"こうばん","grammar:91236:ex2:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91236:ex2:words:5:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91236:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91236:ex2:words:6:zh":"there is / are","grammar:91236:ex2:words:6:note":"; the masu-form of ある","grammar:91236:ex2:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91236:ex2:points:0":"交番 is a building, so it uses 「あります」; the police officers inside use 「います」","grammar:91236:ex2:points:1":"駅の前 = in front of the station; 「前」 is a position word","exam:91236:zh":"I found a wallet, so I took it to the small police box nearby where a police officer was stationed.","exam:91236:why":"「交番」 is a place where a police officer is stationed and where you can hand in lost property. A 「銀行」 is connected with money, but it isn't the place to turn in a wallet you found."},"91237":{"example:91237:exzh":"It's cold, so I'll wear a coat.","example:91237:exzh2":"This coat is a little long.","grammar:91237:ex:trans":"It's cold, so I'll wear a coat.","grammar:91237:ex:structure":"〔reason〕ので＋〔result〕","grammar:91237:ex:explain":"「コート」 is a coat worn as the outermost layer in winter. In Japanese, the word for wearing depends on the body part: tops and coats use 「着る」, pants and shoes use 「はく」, and hats use 「かぶる」. 「寒いので」 gives the reason, followed by the result.","grammar:91237:ex:words:0:zh":"cold","grammar:91237:ex:words:0:note":"さむい","grammar:91237:ex:words:0:pos":"adjective","grammar:91237:ex:words:1:zh":"because","grammar:91237:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91237:ex:words:2:zh":"coat","grammar:91237:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91237:ex:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91237:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91237:ex:words:4:zh":"wear","grammar:91237:ex:words:4:note":"きます; the masu-form of 着る","grammar:91237:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91237:ex:points:0":"コート = coat; wear 「着る」 for clothing on the upper body","grammar:91237:ex:points:1":"〜ので = because ～; softer than 「から」 and often used to explain a reason","grammar:91237:ex2:trans":"This coat is a little long.","grammar:91237:ex2:structure":"〔thing〕は＋少し＋〔adjective〕です","grammar:91237:ex2:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91237:ex2:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91237:ex2:words:1:zh":"coat","grammar:91237:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91237:ex2:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91237:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91237:ex2:words:3:zh":"a little","grammar:91237:ex2:words:3:note":"すこし","grammar:91237:ex2:words:3:pos":"adverb","grammar:91237:ex2:words:4:zh":"long","grammar:91237:ex2:words:4:note":"ながい","grammar:91237:ex2:words:4:pos":"adjective","grammar:91237:ex2:words:5:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91237:ex2:words:5:pos":"copula","grammar:91237:ex2:points:0":"コート can also mean a court, like a tennis court, depending on the context","grammar:91237:ex2:points:1":"少し = a little, slightly; placed before an adjective","exam:91237:zh":"On cold winter days, I put on a thick knee-length coat over my sweater and go out.","exam:91237:why":"「コート」 is a coat worn as an outer layer to keep warm. 「シャツ」 is a shirt and is not usually used as this kind of thick, knee-length winter outerwear."},"91238":{"example:91238:exzh":"I'll be back from my trip on the ninth.","example:91238:exzh2":"This job took nine days.","grammar:91238:ex:trans":"I'll be back from my trip on the ninth.","grammar:91238:ex:structure":"〔date〕に＋〔place〕から帰ります","grammar:91238:ex:explain":"「九日」 is read 「ここのか」 and here means the ninth of the month. A specific point in time like a date takes 「に」. 「帰る」 means to return home or to where you came from, so 「旅行から帰る」 is to come back from a trip.","grammar:91238:ex:words:0:zh":"the ninth","grammar:91238:ex:words:0:note":"ここのか","grammar:91238:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91238:ex:words:1:zh":"(point in time)","grammar:91238:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91238:ex:words:2:zh":"travel / trip","grammar:91238:ex:words:2:note":"りょこう","grammar:91238:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91238:ex:words:3:zh":"from","grammar:91238:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91238:ex:words:4:zh":"come back","grammar:91238:ex:words:4:note":"かえります; the masu-form of 帰る","grammar:91238:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91238:ex:points:0":"九日 is read ここのか and can mean either 'the ninth' or 'nine days'","grammar:91238:ex:points:1":"Add 「に」 after a date; 「旅行から帰る」 = to come back from a trip","grammar:91238:ex2:trans":"This job took nine days.","grammar:91238:ex2:structure":"〔task〕は＋〔time〕かかりました","grammar:91238:ex2:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91238:ex2:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91238:ex2:words:1:zh":"work","grammar:91238:ex2:words:1:note":"しごと","grammar:91238:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91238:ex2:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91238:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91238:ex2:words:3:zh":"nine days","grammar:91238:ex2:words:3:note":"ここのか","grammar:91238:ex2:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91238:ex2:words:4:zh":"took (time)","grammar:91238:ex2:words:4:note":"; the past tense of かかる","grammar:91238:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91238:ex2:points:0":"Here 九日 means 'nine days', showing a duration","grammar:91238:ex2:points:1":"time ＋ かかる = to take (an amount of time); no particle is needed after the time","exam:91238:zh":"Today is the eighth, so my friend is coming tomorrow, on the ninth.","exam:91238:why":"The day after the eighth is 「九日」. 「十日」 is two days after the eighth."},"91239":{"example:91239:exzh":"There are nine balls in the box.","example:91239:exzh2":"My daughter turned nine.","grammar:91239:ex:trans":"There are nine balls in the box.","grammar:91239:ex:structure":"〔place〕に＋〔thing〕が＋〔quantity〕あります","grammar:91239:ex:explain":"「九つ」 is read 「ここのつ」, the ninth in the native Japanese counting system. This system can count all kinds of things. The pattern 「〜に〜が〜あります」 says how many of something are somewhere, with the quantity right before the verb.","grammar:91239:ex:words:0:zh":"box","grammar:91239:ex:words:0:note":"はこ","grammar:91239:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91239:ex:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91239:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91239:ex:words:2:zh":"inside","grammar:91239:ex:words:2:note":"なか","grammar:91239:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91239:ex:words:3:zh":"（place of existence）","grammar:91239:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91239:ex:words:4:zh":"ball","grammar:91239:ex:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91239:ex:words:5:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91239:ex:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91239:ex:words:6:zh":"nine (of something)","grammar:91239:ex:words:6:note":"ここのつ","grammar:91239:ex:words:6:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91239:ex:words:7:zh":"there is / are","grammar:91239:ex:words:7:note":"; the masu-form of ある","grammar:91239:ex:words:7:pos":"verb","grammar:91239:ex:points:0":"九つ = nine (of something); the native Japanese counting system: …ななつ、やっつ、ここのつ、とお","grammar:91239:ex:points:1":"箱の中 = inside the box; the quantity goes before 「あります」","grammar:91239:ex2:trans":"My daughter is nine years old now.","grammar:91239:ex2:structure":"〔person〕は＋〔age〕になりました","grammar:91239:ex2:words:0:zh":"(my) daughter","grammar:91239:ex2:words:0:note":"むすめ","grammar:91239:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91239:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91239:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91239:ex2:words:2:zh":"nine years old","grammar:91239:ex2:words:2:note":"ここのつ","grammar:91239:ex2:words:2:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91239:ex2:words:3:zh":"(to become ～)","grammar:91239:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91239:ex2:words:4:zh":"became","grammar:91239:ex2:words:4:note":"; the past tense of なる","grammar:91239:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91239:ex2:points:0":"九つ can also mean 'nine years old', usually for children","grammar:91239:ex2:points:1":"〜になりました = has become ～; 「なる」 is used for getting older","exam:91239:zh":"There were ten oranges in the box, and I ate one, so nine are left.","exam:91239:why":"Ten minus one leaves 「九つ」. 「八つ」 is eight, which would mean two had been eaten."},"91240":{"example:91240:exzh":"This book is interesting.","example:91240:exzh2":"Is there a convenience store near here?","grammar:91240:ex:trans":"This book is interesting.","grammar:91240:ex:structure":"この＋〔noun〕は＋〔adjective〕です","grammar:91240:ex:explain":"「この」 points to something near you and must be used with a noun: 「この本」. Without a noun, you say 「これはおもしろいです」. 「この・その・あの・どの」 form a set, differing in distance.","grammar:91240:ex:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91240:ex:words:0:note":"; it must always be followed by a noun","grammar:91240:ex:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91240:ex:words:1:zh":"book","grammar:91240:ex:words:1:note":"ほん","grammar:91240:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91240:ex:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91240:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91240:ex:words:3:zh":"interesting","grammar:91240:ex:words:3:pos":"adjective","grammar:91240:ex:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91240:ex:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91240:ex:points:0":"この = this (near the speaker); it must be followed by a noun — to say 'this' on its own, use 「これ」","grammar:91240:ex:points:1":"おもしろい = interesting, good (books, movies)","grammar:91240:ex2:trans":"Is there a convenience store near here?","grammar:91240:ex2:structure":"この近くに＋〔thing〕はありますか","grammar:91240:ex2:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91240:ex2:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91240:ex2:words:1:zh":"nearby","grammar:91240:ex2:words:1:note":"ちかく","grammar:91240:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91240:ex2:words:2:zh":"（place of existence）","grammar:91240:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91240:ex2:words:3:zh":"convenience store","grammar:91240:ex2:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91240:ex2:words:4:zh":"(topic, asking whether it exists)","grammar:91240:ex2:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91240:ex2:words:5:zh":"there is / are","grammar:91240:ex2:words:5:note":"; the masu-form of ある","grammar:91240:ex2:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91240:ex2:words:6:zh":"(question)","grammar:91240:ex2:words:6:pos":"particle","grammar:91240:ex2:points:0":"この近く = around here; handy when asking directions or looking for a shop","grammar:91240:ex2:points:1":"When asking whether something exists, the thing is often brought up with 「は」: コンビニはありますか","exam:91240:zh":"Please read this book that I'm holding in my hand now, not the one you're holding.","exam:91240:why":"「この」 refers to something close to the speaker, which fits the book in my hand. 「その」 usually refers to something close to the listener."},"91241":{"example:91241:exzh":"Come on, let's go.","example:91241:exzh2":"Well, I'm not really sure.","grammar:91241:ex:trans":"Come on, let's go.","grammar:91241:ex:structure":"さあ、〔verb〕ましょう","grammar:91241:ex:explain":"「さあ」 is used when you're about to start doing something or urging someone on, like 'come on' or 'okay'. It is often followed by 「〜ましょう (let's ～ together)」. This is different from 「さあ」 in the next sentence meaning 'I don't know' — it depends on the tone and what follows.","grammar:91241:ex:words:0:zh":"come on, okay","grammar:91241:ex:words:0:pos":"interjection","grammar:91241:ex:words:1:zh":"let's go","grammar:91241:ex:words:1:note":"いきましょう; the invitation form of 行く","grammar:91241:ex:words:1:pos":"verb","grammar:91241:ex:points:0":"さあ = come on, okay; urging everyone to start doing something","grammar:91241:ex:points:1":"drop ます from the masu-form and add 「ましょう」 = let's ～","grammar:91241:ex2:trans":"Hmm, I'm not really sure.","grammar:91241:ex2:structure":"さあ、よくわかりません","grammar:91241:ex2:words:0:zh":"hmm","grammar:91241:ex2:words:0:pos":"interjection","grammar:91241:ex2:words:1:zh":"(not) much","grammar:91241:ex2:words:1:pos":"adverb","grammar:91241:ex2:words:2:zh":"don't know","grammar:91241:ex2:words:2:note":"; the negative of わかる","grammar:91241:ex2:words:2:pos":"verb","grammar:91241:ex2:points:0":"さあ can also mean 'hmm…', used when someone asks you something you don't know, with the ending drawn out","grammar:91241:ex2:points:1":"よく＋negative = not very ～; わかりません = I don't know","exam:91241:zh":"Everyone had finished getting ready, so I urged them to set off and said, \"All right, let's go!\"","exam:91241:why":"「さあ」 can be used to urge or invite someone to start an action. 「おや」 expresses surprise when you notice something unexpected, and it does not mean urging someone to set off."},"91242":{"example:91242:exzh":"I wrote an essay in Japanese.","example:91242:exzh2":"I have an essay assignment.","grammar:91242:ex:trans":"I wrote a composition in Japanese.","grammar:91242:ex:structure":"〔language〕で＋作文を書きました","grammar:91242:ex:explain":"「作文」 means a composition or a short piece of writing. Here 「で」 shows the means — writing in Japanese. 「書きました」 is the polite past form of 「書く」.","grammar:91242:ex:words:0:zh":"Japanese","grammar:91242:ex:words:0:note":"にほんご","grammar:91242:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91242:ex:words:1:zh":"(means)","grammar:91242:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91242:ex:words:2:zh":"essay","grammar:91242:ex:words:2:note":"さくぶん","grammar:91242:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91242:ex:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91242:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91242:ex:words:4:zh":"wrote","grammar:91242:ex:words:4:note":"かきました; the past tense of 書く","grammar:91242:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91242:ex:points:0":"作文を書く = to write a composition; a set combination","grammar:91242:ex:points:1":"「で」 shows the language used: 日本語で書く","grammar:91242:ex2:trans":"I have a composition assignment.","grammar:91242:ex2:structure":"〔noun〕の宿題があります","grammar:91242:ex2:words:0:zh":"essay","grammar:91242:ex2:words:0:note":"さくぶん","grammar:91242:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91242:ex2:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91242:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91242:ex2:words:2:zh":"homework","grammar:91242:ex2:words:2:note":"しゅくだい","grammar:91242:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91242:ex2:words:3:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91242:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91242:ex2:words:4:zh":"there is / are","grammar:91242:ex2:words:4:note":"; the masu-form of ある","grammar:91242:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91242:ex2:points:0":"作文の宿題 = composition homework; 「の」 links two nouns","grammar:91242:ex2:points:1":"宿題 = homework; be careful, the kanji word 作業 means 'work' in Japanese, not homework","exam:91242:zh":"In Japanese class, I wrote a composition about my own family made up of ten sentences.","exam:91242:why":"「作文」 is a piece of writing that puts sentences together into a text. 「言葉」 means a word or language, and can't refer to a piece of writing made up of ten sentences like this one."},"91243":{"example:91243:exzh":"Because it's raining, I'll put up an umbrella.","example:91243:exzh2":"I walked along holding an umbrella.","grammar:91243:ex:trans":"It's raining, so I'll put up an umbrella.","grammar:91243:ex:structure":"〔noun〕なので＋〔result〕","grammar:91243:ex:explain":"The most common beginner use of 「差す」 is 「傘を差す」 (to put up an umbrella). 「雨なので」 here is noun ＋ な ＋ ので, showing the reason. For the English 'put up an umbrella', Japanese uses 「差す」.","grammar:91243:ex:words:0:zh":"rain","grammar:91243:ex:words:0:note":"あめ","grammar:91243:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91243:ex:words:1:zh":"(the form 「だ」 takes before 「ので」)","grammar:91243:ex:words:1:pos":"copula","grammar:91243:ex:words:2:zh":"because","grammar:91243:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91243:ex:words:3:zh":"umbrella","grammar:91243:ex:words:3:note":"かさ","grammar:91243:ex:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91243:ex:words:4:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91243:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91243:ex:words:5:zh":"hold up","grammar:91243:ex:words:5:note":"さします; the masu-form of 差す","grammar:91243:ex:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91243:ex:points:0":"傘を差す = to put up an umbrella; the most common use of 「差す」","grammar:91243:ex:points:1":"Nouns take 「な」 before 「ので」: 雨なので (because it's raining)","grammar:91243:ex2:trans":"I walked holding an umbrella.","grammar:91243:ex2:structure":"〔verb te-form〕＋〔verb〕","grammar:91243:ex2:words:0:zh":"umbrella","grammar:91243:ex2:words:0:note":"かさ","grammar:91243:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91243:ex2:words:1:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91243:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91243:ex2:words:2:zh":"holding (an umbrella)","grammar:91243:ex2:words:2:note":"さして; the te-form of 差す","grammar:91243:ex2:words:2:pos":"verb","grammar:91243:ex2:words:3:zh":"went","grammar:91243:ex2:words:3:note":"あるきました; the past tense of 歩く","grammar:91243:ex2:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91243:ex2:points:0":"傘を差して歩く = to walk with an umbrella up; the te-form shows doing the next action in that way","grammar:91243:ex2:points:1":"歩きました = walked (past)","exam:91243:zh":"So that I wouldn't get wet in the rain, I decided to open my umbrella and hold it over my head.","exam:91243:why":"The set phrase for holding an umbrella is 「傘を差す」. 「かぶる」 is used for things worn directly on the head, such as hats, and cannot mean holding an umbrella."},"91244":{"example:91244:exzh":"I'll graduate from university the year after next.","example:91244:exzh2":"I want to live in Japan the year after next.","grammar:91244:ex:trans":"I'll graduate from university the year after next.","grammar:91244:ex:structure":"〔time〕、〔school〕を卒業します","grammar:91244:ex:explain":"「再来年」 means 'the year after next', that is, two years from now. 「再来週 (the week after next)」 and 「再来月 (the month after next)」 are formed the same way. The key point here is 「大学を卒業する」: leaving a school takes 「を」, not 「から」.","grammar:91244:ex:words:0:zh":"the year after next (two years from now)","grammar:91244:ex:words:0:note":"さらいねん","grammar:91244:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91244:ex:words:1:zh":"university","grammar:91244:ex:words:1:note":"だいがく","grammar:91244:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91244:ex:words:2:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91244:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91244:ex:words:3:zh":"graduation","grammar:91244:ex:words:3:note":"そつぎょうします; the masu-form of 卒業する","grammar:91244:ex:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91244:ex:points:1":"Graduating from a school is 「〔school〕を卒業する」, with 「を」","grammar:91244:ex2:trans":"The year after next, I want to live in Japan.","grammar:91244:ex2:structure":"〔time〕は＋〔place〕に住みたいです","grammar:91244:ex2:words:0:zh":"the year after next","grammar:91244:ex2:words:0:note":"さらいねん","grammar:91244:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91244:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91244:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91244:ex2:words:2:zh":"Japan","grammar:91244:ex2:words:2:note":"にほん","grammar:91244:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91244:ex2:words:3:zh":"(the place of residence)","grammar:91244:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91244:ex2:words:4:zh":"want to live","grammar:91244:ex2:words:4:note":"すみたい; 住む＋たい","grammar:91244:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91244:ex2:words:5:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91244:ex2:words:5:pos":"copula","grammar:91244:ex2:points:0":"再来年は = as for the year after next; adding 「は」 to a time word makes it a topic or a contrast","grammar:91244:ex2:points:1":"〜たいです = want to ～ (stating your own wish)","exam:91244:zh":"This year I'll study Japanese, next year I'll save money, and the year after that I'll go to Japan.","exam:91244:why":"「再来年」 means two years after this year. 「来年」 is only next year, and the sentence says that is the year for saving money."},"91245":{"example:91245:exzh":"I look up words I don't understand in a dictionary.","example:91245:exzh2":"This dictionary is very handy.","grammar:91245:ex:trans":"I look up words I don't understand in a dictionary.","grammar:91245:ex:structure":"〔thing〕を＋辞書で調べます","grammar:91245:ex:explain":"「辞書」 is a dictionary; looking something up can be 「辞書で調べる」 or 「辞書を引く」. The 「わからない言葉」 at the start puts the nai-form of a verb directly before a noun, meaning words you don't understand — the basic way to modify a noun in Japanese.","grammar:91245:ex:words:0:zh":"that I don't understand","grammar:91245:ex:words:0:note":"; the nai-form of わかる","grammar:91245:ex:words:0:pos":"verb","grammar:91245:ex:words:1:zh":"word, language","grammar:91245:ex:words:1:note":"ことば","grammar:91245:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91245:ex:words:2:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91245:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91245:ex:words:3:zh":"dictionary","grammar:91245:ex:words:3:note":"じしょ","grammar:91245:ex:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91245:ex:words:4:zh":"(means)","grammar:91245:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91245:ex:words:5:zh":"Look up","grammar:91245:ex:words:5:note":"しらべます; the masu-form of 調べる","grammar:91245:ex:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91245:ex:points:0":"辞書で調べる = to look up in a dictionary; 「辞書を引く」 also means to look something up","grammar:91245:ex:points:1":"A plain-form verb can modify a noun directly: わからない言葉 (a word I don't understand)","grammar:91245:ex2:trans":"This dictionary is really handy.","grammar:91245:ex2:structure":"〔thing〕は＋とても＋〔adjective〕です","grammar:91245:ex2:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91245:ex2:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91245:ex2:words:1:zh":"dictionary","grammar:91245:ex2:words:1:note":"じしょ","grammar:91245:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91245:ex2:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91245:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91245:ex2:words:3:zh":"extremely","grammar:91245:ex2:words:3:pos":"adverb","grammar:91245:ex2:words:4:zh":"convenient, handy","grammar:91245:ex2:words:4:note":"べんり","grammar:91245:ex2:words:4:pos":"na-adjective","grammar:91245:ex2:words:5:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91245:ex2:words:5:pos":"copula","grammar:91245:ex2:points:1":"便利 is a na-adjective: 便利な辞書 (a handy dictionary)","exam:91245:zh":"To look up the meaning of words I didn't know, I used a dictionary that lists words together with their explanations.","exam:91245:why":"「辞書」 is for information such as the meaning of words. 「新聞」 mainly reports the news, and you can't use it like a dictionary to look up individual words."},"91246":{"example:91246:exzh":"I go to school by bicycle every day.","example:91246:exzh2":"I bought a new bicycle.","grammar:91246:ex:trans":"I go to school by bicycle every day.","grammar:91246:ex:structure":"〔means of transport〕で＋〔place〕へ行きます","grammar:91246:ex:explain":"「自転車」 is a bicycle. To say what means of transport you use to get around, add 「で」 after it. Note that the action of riding a bicycle is 「自転車に乗る」, but 'go by bicycle' is simply 「自転車で行く」.","grammar:91246:ex:words:0:zh":"every day","grammar:91246:ex:words:0:note":"まいにち","grammar:91246:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91246:ex:words:1:zh":"bicycle","grammar:91246:ex:words:1:note":"じてんしゃ","grammar:91246:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91246:ex:words:2:zh":"(means of transport)","grammar:91246:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91246:ex:words:3:zh":"school","grammar:91246:ex:words:3:note":"がっこう","grammar:91246:ex:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91246:ex:words:4:zh":"(direction)","grammar:91246:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91246:ex:words:5:zh":"go","grammar:91246:ex:words:5:note":"いきます; masu-form of 行く","grammar:91246:ex:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91246:ex:points:0":"自転車 = bicycle; 「自転車で行く」 = go by bicycle","grammar:91246:ex:points:1":"The means of transport takes 「で」: 電車で、バスで、自転車で","grammar:91246:ex2:trans":"I bought a new bicycle.","grammar:91246:ex2:structure":"い-adjective＋〔noun〕を買いました","grammar:91246:ex2:words:0:zh":"new","grammar:91246:ex2:words:0:note":"あたらしい","grammar:91246:ex2:words:0:pos":"adjective","grammar:91246:ex2:words:1:zh":"bicycle","grammar:91246:ex2:words:1:note":"じてんしゃ","grammar:91246:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91246:ex2:words:2:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91246:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91246:ex2:words:3:zh":"bought","grammar:91246:ex2:words:3:note":"かいました; the past tense of 買う","grammar:91246:ex2:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91246:ex2:points:0":"新しい自転車 = a new bicycle; i-adjectives go directly before nouns","grammar:91246:ex2:points:1":"買いました = bought (past)","exam:91246:zh":"I go to school by bicycle, which moves forward by pedaling with my own feet and uses no gasoline.","exam:91246:why":"A 「自転車」 moves forward by pedaling. A 「自動車」 does have pedals, but it doesn't move forward by turning them with the power of your feet."},"91247":{"example:91247:exzh":"Please close the window.","example:91247:exzh2":"The shop closes at eight.","grammar:91247:ex:trans":"Please close the window.","grammar:91247:ex:structure":"〔thing〕を＋閉めてください","grammar:91247:ex:explain":"「閉める」 means to close something that opens, like a door or a window; since a person does it, you say 「窓を閉める」. It isn't used for switches — to turn off a light or a TV you say 「消す」. 「te-form ＋ ください」 politely asks someone to do something.","grammar:91247:ex:words:0:zh":"window","grammar:91247:ex:words:0:note":"まど","grammar:91247:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91247:ex:words:1:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91247:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91247:ex:words:2:zh":"close (shut)","grammar:91247:ex:words:2:note":"しめて; the te-form of 閉める","grammar:91247:ex:words:2:pos":"verb","grammar:91247:ex:words:3:zh":"please (do ～)","grammar:91247:ex:words:3:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91247:ex:points:0":"閉める = to close (doors, windows, lids, etc.); the opposite is 「開ける」 (to open)","grammar:91247:ex:points:1":"閉める is when you close something yourself, so it takes 「を」; when something closes by itself, use 「閉まる」","grammar:91247:ex2:trans":"The shop closes at eight.","grammar:91247:ex2:structure":"〔shop〕は＋〔time〕に閉めます","grammar:91247:ex2:words:0:zh":"store","grammar:91247:ex2:words:0:note":"みせ","grammar:91247:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91247:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91247:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91247:ex2:words:2:zh":"eight o'clock","grammar:91247:ex2:words:2:note":"はちじ","grammar:91247:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91247:ex2:words:3:zh":"(point in time)","grammar:91247:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91247:ex2:words:4:zh":"close (the shop)","grammar:91247:ex2:words:4:note":"しめます; the masu-form of 閉める","grammar:91247:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91247:ex2:points:0":"店を閉める = to close the shop for the day; 「店を」 is left out in this sentence","grammar:91247:ex2:points:1":"A specific time takes 「に」: 八時に","exam:91247:zh":"Because cold wind was coming in through the window, I decided to close the open window.","exam:91247:why":"To close doors and windows you use 「閉める」. 「消す」 is used for turning off lights or putting out a fire, and it can't be used to mean closing a window."},"91248":{"example:91248:exzh":"My father puts on a tie every morning.","example:91248:exzh2":"Please fasten your seatbelt when you get in the car.","grammar:91248:ex:trans":"My father puts on a tie every morning.","grammar:91248:ex:structure":"〔thing〕を＋締めます","grammar:91248:ex:explain":"「締める」 means to tighten something like a strap; the most common uses are putting on a tie, fastening a seatbelt, and tightening a belt. It is pronounced 「しめる」, the same as 「閉める」 (to close doors and windows), so when listening you tell them apart by the object in front.","grammar:91248:ex:words:0:zh":"(my) father","grammar:91248:ex:words:0:note":"ちち","grammar:91248:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91248:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91248:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91248:ex:words:2:zh":"every morning","grammar:91248:ex:words:2:note":"まいあさ","grammar:91248:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91248:ex:words:3:zh":"tie","grammar:91248:ex:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91248:ex:words:4:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91248:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91248:ex:words:5:zh":"fasten, tie (a tie)","grammar:91248:ex:words:5:note":"しめます; the masu-form of 締める","grammar:91248:ex:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91248:ex:points:0":"締める = to fasten tight; typical combinations: ネクタイを締める、シートベルトを締める、ベルトを締める","grammar:91248:ex:points:1":"Same sound as 「閉める」 (to close) but a different kanji and a completely different meaning","grammar:91248:ex2:trans":"Please fasten your seatbelt once you get in the car.","grammar:91248:ex2:structure":"〜たら、〜てください","grammar:91248:ex2:words:0:zh":"car","grammar:91248:ex2:words:0:note":"くるま","grammar:91248:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91248:ex2:words:1:zh":"(the vehicle you board)","grammar:91248:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91248:ex2:words:2:zh":"after getting in (the car)","grammar:91248:ex2:words:2:note":"のったら; 乗る＋たら","grammar:91248:ex2:words:2:pos":"verb","grammar:91248:ex2:words:3:zh":"seatbelt","grammar:91248:ex2:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91248:ex2:words:4:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91248:ex2:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91248:ex2:words:5:zh":"fasten","grammar:91248:ex2:words:5:note":"しめて; the te-form of 締める","grammar:91248:ex2:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91248:ex2:words:6:zh":"please (do ～)","grammar:91248:ex2:words:6:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91248:ex2:points:0":"シートベルトを締める = to fasten a seatbelt; a set combination","grammar:91248:ex2:points:1":"verb ta-form ＋ ら = after ～ (do the second thing once the first is done)","exam:91248:zh":"Before going to the office, I make a habit of putting on a tie over my shirt.","exam:91248:why":"A tie goes with 「締める」, meaning to tie or fasten. 「着る」 is used for clothes such as a shirt and can't be used directly with ネクタイ."},"91249":{"example:91249:exzh":"I eat sushi dipped in soy sauce.","example:91249:exzh2":"Please pass me the soy sauce.","grammar:91249:ex:trans":"I eat sushi dipped in soy sauce.","grammar:91249:ex:structure":"〔food〕に＋醤油をつけて＋食べます","grammar:91249:ex:explain":"「醤油」 is soy sauce; the kanji are hard, so it is often written in hiragana as 「しょうゆ」. When eating sushi, dipping it in soy sauce is 「つける」, while pouring it over is 「かける」. 「つけて食べます」 uses the te-form to link two actions: dip, then eat.","grammar:91249:ex:words:0:zh":"sushi","grammar:91249:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91249:ex:words:1:zh":"(the thing it is put on)","grammar:91249:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91249:ex:words:2:zh":"soy sauce","grammar:91249:ex:words:2:note":"しょうゆ","grammar:91249:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91249:ex:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91249:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91249:ex:words:4:zh":"dip in","grammar:91249:ex:words:4:note":"; the te-form of つける","grammar:91249:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91249:ex:words:5:zh":"eat","grammar:91249:ex:words:5:note":"たべます; masu-form (polite form) of 「食べる」","grammar:91249:ex:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91249:ex:points:0":"醤油をつける = to dip in soy sauce; 「醤油をかける」 = to pour soy sauce over","grammar:91249:ex:points:1":"「〔A〕に〔B〕をつける」 = to put B onto A; the te-form links to the next action","grammar:91249:ex2:trans":"Please pass me the soy sauce.","grammar:91249:ex2:structure":"〔thing〕を＋取ってください","grammar:91249:ex2:words:0:zh":"soy sauce","grammar:91249:ex2:words:0:note":"しょうゆ","grammar:91249:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91249:ex2:words:1:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91249:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91249:ex2:words:2:zh":"take, pass","grammar:91249:ex2:words:2:note":"とって; the te-form of 取る","grammar:91249:ex2:words:2:pos":"verb","grammar:91249:ex2:words:3:zh":"please (do ～)","grammar:91249:ex2:words:3:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91249:ex2:points:0":"醤油を取ってください = please pass me the soy sauce; very common at meals","grammar:91249:ex2:points:1":"取る basically means 'take'; asking someone 「取って」 means asking them to hand it to you","exam:91249:zh":"For this dish, instead of salt, I add a little of the dark liquid made from soybeans—soy sauce.","exam:91249:why":"「醤油」 is a dark liquid seasoning made from ingredients such as soybeans. 「塩」 adds saltiness too, but it isn't the liquid described in the question."},"91250":{"example:91250:exzh":"Autumn is cool and nice, isn't it?","example:91250:exzh2":"This room is cool.","grammar:91250:ex:trans":"Autumn is cool and nice, isn't it?","grammar:91250:ex:structure":"〔noun〕は＋〔adjective te-form〕＋いいですね","grammar:91250:ex:explain":"「涼しい」 describes a pleasantly cool feeling, often used for autumn weather or an evening breeze. It is different from 「寒い」 (cold) — 「涼しい」 is positive. 「涼しくて」 is the te-form, linking 'cool' with 'nice'.","grammar:91250:ex:words:0:zh":"autumn","grammar:91250:ex:words:0:note":"あき","grammar:91250:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91250:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91250:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91250:ex:words:2:zh":"cool, and","grammar:91250:ex:words:2:note":"すずしくて; the te-form of 涼しい","grammar:91250:ex:words:2:pos":"adjective","grammar:91250:ex:words:3:zh":"good","grammar:91250:ex:words:3:pos":"adjective","grammar:91250:ex:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91250:ex:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91250:ex:words:5:zh":"right? (seeking agreement)","grammar:91250:ex:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91250:ex:points:0":"涼しい = (of weather or wind) pleasantly cool; if it's too cold, say 「寒い」","grammar:91250:ex:points:1":"For i-adjectives, change 「い」 to 「くて」 to continue the sentence: 涼しくていい = it's cool, and that's nice","grammar:91250:ex2:trans":"This room is cool.","grammar:91250:ex2:structure":"〔place〕は＋涼しいです","grammar:91250:ex2:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91250:ex2:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91250:ex2:words:1:zh":"room","grammar:91250:ex2:words:1:note":"へや","grammar:91250:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91250:ex2:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91250:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91250:ex2:words:3:zh":"cool","grammar:91250:ex2:words:3:note":"すずしい","grammar:91250:ex2:words:3:pos":"adjective","grammar:91250:ex2:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91250:ex2:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91250:ex2:points:0":"涼しい can describe a room or a place as nice and cool","grammar:91250:ex2:points:1":"The opposite is 「暑い」 (hot)","exam:91250:zh":"Summer in this town is neither hot nor cold; it's cool and pleasant.","exam:91250:why":"「涼しい」 means pleasantly cool and is often used to describe summer weather. 「冷たい」 mainly describes something cold to the touch and can't naturally describe the summer climate here."},"91251":{"example:91251:exzh":"I eat soup with a spoon.","example:91251:exzh2":"Please give me one spoon.","grammar:91251:ex:trans":"I eat soup with a spoon.","grammar:91251:ex:structure":"〔tool〕で＋〔thing〕を〔action〕","grammar:91251:ex:explain":"「スプーン」 is a spoon, a loanword. The tool you use takes 「で」, as in 「箸で食べる」 or 「スプーンで飲む」. Japanese uses 「飲む」 for soup, even when you scoop it with a spoon.","grammar:91251:ex:words:0:zh":"spoon","grammar:91251:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91251:ex:words:1:zh":"(means, tool)","grammar:91251:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91251:ex:words:2:zh":"soup","grammar:91251:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91251:ex:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91251:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91251:ex:words:4:zh":"to drink","grammar:91251:ex:words:4:note":"のみます: the masu-form of 飲む.","grammar:91251:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91251:ex:points:0":"スプーン = spoon; a fork is 「フォーク」 and a knife is 「ナイフ」","grammar:91251:ex:points:1":"The tool you use takes 「で」; for soup, Japanese uses 「飲む」","grammar:91251:ex2:trans":"Please give me one spoon.","grammar:91251:ex2:structure":"〔thing〕を＋〔quantity〕＋ください","grammar:91251:ex2:words:0:zh":"spoon","grammar:91251:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91251:ex2:words:1:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91251:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91251:ex2:words:2:zh":"one","grammar:91251:ex2:words:2:note":"いっぽん","grammar:91251:ex2:words:2:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91251:ex2:words:3:zh":"please (do ～)","grammar:91251:ex2:words:3:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91251:ex2:points:0":"スプーン is counted with 「本（ほん）」 because it's long and thin","grammar:91251:ex2:points:1":"一本 is read いっぽん (ほん becomes ぽん)","exam:91251:zh":"To scoop up the soup and bring it to my mouth, I used a spoon with a round, shallow tip.","exam:91251:why":"「スプーン」 can scoop up liquid and is suited to eating soup. 「フォーク」 is for spearing food and can't scoop soup the way a spoon does."},"91252":{"example:91252:exzh":"Because it's cold, I put on a sweater.","example:91252:exzh2":"I'm wearing a red sweater.","grammar:91252:ex:trans":"It was cold, so I wore a sweater.","grammar:91252:ex:structure":"〔reason〕ので＋〔thing〕を着ました","grammar:91252:ex:explain":"「セーター」 is a sweater, worn on the upper body, so it uses 「着る」. 「寒いので」 gives the reason. Note that 「着ました」 and 「来ました」 are both pronounced 「きました」; the 「セーターを」 tells you it means 'wore'.","grammar:91252:ex:words:0:zh":"cold","grammar:91252:ex:words:0:note":"さむい","grammar:91252:ex:words:0:pos":"adjective","grammar:91252:ex:words:1:zh":"because","grammar:91252:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91252:ex:words:2:zh":"sweater","grammar:91252:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91252:ex:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91252:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91252:ex:words:4:zh":"put on","grammar:91252:ex:words:4:note":"きました; past form of 着る","grammar:91252:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91252:ex:points:0":"セーター = sweater; use 「着る」 to wear a sweater","grammar:91252:ex:points:1":"着ました is read きました, which sounds the same as 「来ました」 (came), so you have to judge from the context","grammar:91252:ex2:trans":"I'm wearing a red sweater.","grammar:91252:ex2:structure":"〔clothes〕を＋着ています","grammar:91252:ex2:words:0:zh":"red","grammar:91252:ex2:words:0:note":"あかい","grammar:91252:ex2:words:0:pos":"adjective","grammar:91252:ex2:words:1:zh":"sweater","grammar:91252:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91252:ex2:words:2:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91252:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91252:ex2:words:3:zh":"Wearing","grammar:91252:ex2:words:3:note":"きています; 着る + ています","grammar:91252:ex2:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91252:ex2:points:0":"着ています = wearing (the current state); common when describing what someone is wearing","grammar:91252:ex2:points:1":"赤い セーター: a color i-adjective goes directly before the noun","exam:91252:zh":"The long-sleeved sweater my mother knitted for me from wool is warm to wear in winter.","exam:91252:why":"The garment knitted from wool and worn on the upper body is a 「セーター」. 「ワイシャツ」 is a dress shirt, which doesn't fit the description of being knitted from wool."},"91253":{"example:91253:exzh":"My father wears a suit to the office.","example:91253:exzh2":"I bought a new suit.","grammar:91253:ex:trans":"My dad wears a suit and goes to the office.","grammar:91253:ex:structure":"〔clothes〕を着て＋〔place〕へ行きます","grammar:91253:ex:explain":"「背広」 is a suit men wear to work; it's a somewhat old-fashioned word, and younger people usually say 「スーツ」. This sentence uses 「着て」 to connect “putting on a suit” with “going to the office,” showing that he leaves the house wearing the suit.","grammar:91253:ex:words:0:zh":"(my) father","grammar:91253:ex:words:0:note":"ちち","grammar:91253:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91253:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91253:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91253:ex:words:2:zh":"suit","grammar:91253:ex:words:2:note":"せびろ","grammar:91253:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91253:ex:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91253:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91253:ex:words:4:zh":"Wearing","grammar:91253:ex:words:4:note":"きて; the te-form of 着る","grammar:91253:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91253:ex:words:5:zh":"company","grammar:91253:ex:words:5:note":"かいしゃ","grammar:91253:ex:words:5:pos":"noun","grammar:91253:ex:words:6:zh":"(direction)","grammar:91253:ex:words:6:pos":"particle","grammar:91253:ex:words:7:zh":"go","grammar:91253:ex:words:7:note":"いきます; masu-form of 行く","grammar:91253:ex:words:7:pos":"verb","grammar:91253:ex:points:0":"背広 = a men's suit; a more traditional word, though 「スーツ」 is also common now","grammar:91253:ex:points:1":"The te-form shows doing the next action in that state: 背広を着て行く","grammar:91253:ex2:trans":"I bought a new suit.","grammar:91253:ex2:structure":"新しい＋〔noun〕を買いました","grammar:91253:ex2:words:0:zh":"new","grammar:91253:ex2:words:0:note":"あたらしい","grammar:91253:ex2:words:0:pos":"adjective","grammar:91253:ex2:words:1:zh":"suit","grammar:91253:ex2:words:1:note":"せびろ","grammar:91253:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91253:ex2:words:2:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91253:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91253:ex2:words:3:zh":"bought","grammar:91253:ex2:words:3:note":"かいました; the past tense of 買う","grammar:91253:ex2:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91253:ex2:points:0":"背広 usually means a men's suit (jacket + trousers)","grammar:91253:ex2:points:1":"新しい + noun: an i-adjective modifies the noun directly","exam:91253:zh":"My father wears a suit whose jacket and trousers are made of the same fabric to work.","exam:91253:why":"「背広」 means a men's suit, usually made up of a jacket and trousers in the same fabric. 「コート」 is a coat, which doesn't include matching trousers."},"91254":{"example:91254:exzh":"I went to Kyoto last week.","example:91254:exzh2":"Last week was very busy.","grammar:91254:ex:trans":"I went to Kyoto last week.","grammar:91254:ex:structure":"先週、〔place〕へ行きました","grammar:91254:ex:explain":"「先週」 means last week. Note that 「先」 here means “previous,” not “first.” Time words that are relative to now, like 「先週」「今日」「明日」, don't take 「に」; specific times like 「月曜日に」 and 「七時に」 do.","grammar:91254:ex:words:0:zh":"last week","grammar:91254:ex:words:0:note":"せんしゅう","grammar:91254:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91254:ex:words:1:zh":"Kyoto","grammar:91254:ex:words:1:note":"きょうと","grammar:91254:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91254:ex:words:2:zh":"(direction)","grammar:91254:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91254:ex:words:3:zh":"went","grammar:91254:ex:words:3:note":"いきました; the past form of 行く","grammar:91254:ex:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91254:ex:points:1":"Relative time words like 先週 don't take 「に」","grammar:91254:ex2:trans":"Last week was very busy.","grammar:91254:ex2:structure":"先週は＋〔adjective past〕です","grammar:91254:ex2:words:0:zh":"last week","grammar:91254:ex2:words:0:note":"せんしゅう","grammar:91254:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91254:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91254:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91254:ex2:words:2:zh":"extremely","grammar:91254:ex2:words:2:pos":"adverb","grammar:91254:ex2:words:3:zh":"very busy (past)","grammar:91254:ex2:words:3:note":"いそがしかった; past form of 忙しい","grammar:91254:ex2:words:3:pos":"adjective","grammar:91254:ex2:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91254:ex2:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91254:ex2:points:0":"先週は = as for last week (marking it as the topic)","grammar:91254:ex2:points:1":"i-adjective past form: 忙しい → 忙しかったです","exam:91254:zh":"This week is busy, but the week before it I was free.","exam:91254:why":"「先週」 is the week before this one. 「来週」 is next week, which doesn't match 「一週間前」."},"91255":{"example:91255:exzh":"What's over there?","example:91255:exzh2":"I'll go over there too.","grammar:91255:ex:trans":"What's there on your side?","grammar:91255:ex:structure":"そっちは＋何がありますか","grammar:91255:ex:explain":"「そっち」 is casual; the polite form is 「そちら」, and it refers to a place or direction closer to the listener. It belongs to the set 「こっち」 (this side), 「そっち」 (that side), 「あっち」 (that side, far away), 「どっち」 (which side). 「何が」 uses 「が」 because 「は」 can't be used when a question word is the subject.","grammar:91255:ex:words:0:zh":"over there","grammar:91255:ex:words:0:pos":"pronoun","grammar:91255:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91255:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91255:ex:words:2:zh":"what","grammar:91255:ex:words:2:note":"なに","grammar:91255:ex:words:2:pos":"question word","grammar:91255:ex:words:3:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91255:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91255:ex:words:4:zh":"there is / are","grammar:91255:ex:words:4:note":"; the masu-form of ある","grammar:91255:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91255:ex:words:5:zh":"(question)","grammar:91255:ex:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91255:ex:points:0":"そっち = the casual form of 「そちら」, meaning the side closer to the listener","grammar:91255:ex:points:1":"何がありますか = what is there? Use 「が」 when a question word is the subject","grammar:91255:ex2:trans":"I'm going there too.","grammar:91255:ex2:structure":"私も＋〔direction〕へ行きます","grammar:91255:ex2:words:0:zh":"I","grammar:91255:ex2:words:0:note":"わたし","grammar:91255:ex2:words:0:pos":"pronoun","grammar:91255:ex2:words:1:zh":"also, too","grammar:91255:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91255:ex2:words:2:zh":"over there","grammar:91255:ex2:words:2:pos":"pronoun","grammar:91255:ex2:words:3:zh":"(direction)","grammar:91255:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91255:ex2:words:4:zh":"go","grammar:91255:ex2:words:4:note":"いきます; masu-form of 行く","grammar:91255:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91255:ex2:points:0":"そっちへ行く = go over there; casual speech between friends","grammar:91255:ex2:points:1":"「も」 = also; it replaces 「は」: 私は → 私も","exam:91255:zh":"I'd like to ask about the place where you are on the phone—is it raining where you are now?","exam:91255:why":"「そっち」 refers to the listener's side. 「こっち」 refers to the speaker's side, which doesn't match asking about the other person's location."},"91256":{"example:91256:exzh":"That umbrella is mine.","example:91256:exzh2":"How much is that bag?","grammar:91256:ex:trans":"That umbrella is mine.","grammar:91256:ex:structure":"その〔noun〕は＋私のです","grammar:91256:ex:explain":"「その」 refers to something near the listener, close at hand — for example, the umbrella the other person is holding. 「私のです」 is short for 「私の傘です」; 「の」 can stand in for a noun that has already been mentioned.","grammar:91256:ex:words:0:zh":"that","grammar:91256:ex:words:0:note":"; refers to something near the listener","grammar:91256:ex:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91256:ex:words:1:zh":"umbrella","grammar:91256:ex:words:1:note":"かさ","grammar:91256:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91256:ex:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91256:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91256:ex:words:3:zh":"I","grammar:91256:ex:words:3:note":"わたし","grammar:91256:ex:words:3:pos":"pronoun","grammar:91256:ex:words:4:zh":"'s","grammar:91256:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91256:ex:words:5:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91256:ex:words:5:pos":"copula","grammar:91256:ex:points:0":"その = that (near the listener); it must be followed by a noun","grammar:91256:ex:points:1":"私のです = it's mine; the noun after 「の」 (傘) is left out","grammar:91256:ex2:trans":"How much is that bag?","grammar:91256:ex2:structure":"その〔noun〕は＋いくらですか","grammar:91256:ex2:words:0:zh":"that","grammar:91256:ex2:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91256:ex2:words:1:zh":"bag","grammar:91256:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91256:ex2:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91256:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91256:ex2:words:3:zh":"how much","grammar:91256:ex2:words:3:pos":"question word","grammar:91256:ex2:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91256:ex2:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91256:ex2:words:5:zh":"(question)","grammar:91256:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91256:ex2:points:0":"その is often used for something in the shop clerk's hands or right beside them","grammar:91256:ex2:points:1":"いくらですか = how much is it? A must for shopping","exam:91256:zh":"Please show me the book you're holding in your hand now, not the one here with me.","exam:91256:why":"For something in the other person's hand, use 「その」. 「この」 refers to something on the speaker's side, which doesn't match the position in the sentence."},"91257":{"example:91257:exzh":"I ate a meal. Then I watched TV.","example:91257:exzh2":"I'll walk to the station and then take a bus.","grammar:91257:ex:trans":"I ate a meal. Then I watched TV.","grammar:91257:ex:structure":"〔sentence〕。それから、〔sentence〕","grammar:91257:ex:explain":"「それから」 is a conjunction that links two things that happen in order, and it usually comes at the start of the second sentence. This sentence first says “I ate a meal,” then uses 「それから」 to add “I watched TV,” describing the order in which things were done.","grammar:91257:ex:words:0:zh":"meal","grammar:91257:ex:words:0:note":"ごはん","grammar:91257:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91257:ex:words:1:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91257:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91257:ex:words:2:zh":"ate","grammar:91257:ex:words:2:note":"たべました; the past form of 食べる","grammar:91257:ex:words:2:pos":"verb","grammar:91257:ex:words:3:zh":"and then","grammar:91257:ex:words:3:pos":"conjunction","grammar:91257:ex:words:4:zh":"TV","grammar:91257:ex:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91257:ex:words:5:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91257:ex:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91257:ex:words:6:zh":"watched","grammar:91257:ex:words:6:note":"みました; past form of 見る","grammar:91257:ex:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91257:ex:points:0":"それから = and then, after that; used to list what you did in order","grammar:91257:ex:points:1":"Use 「テレビを見る」 for watching TV","grammar:91257:ex2:trans":"I walk to the station, and then take the bus.","grammar:91257:ex2:structure":"〔verb te-form〕、それから＋〔verb〕","grammar:91257:ex2:words:0:zh":"station","grammar:91257:ex2:words:0:note":"えき","grammar:91257:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91257:ex2:words:1:zh":"until","grammar:91257:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91257:ex2:words:2:zh":"walking","grammar:91257:ex2:words:2:note":"あるいて; te-form of 歩く","grammar:91257:ex2:words:2:pos":"verb","grammar:91257:ex2:words:3:zh":"and then","grammar:91257:ex2:words:3:pos":"conjunction","grammar:91257:ex2:words:4:zh":"bus","grammar:91257:ex2:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91257:ex2:words:5:zh":"(the vehicle you board)","grammar:91257:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91257:ex2:words:6:zh":"took","grammar:91257:ex2:words:6:note":"のります; masu-form of 乗る","grammar:91257:ex2:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91257:ex2:points:0":"それから can also follow the te-form to emphasize “and then”","grammar:91257:ex2:points:1":"駅まで歩く = walk to the station; 「まで」 = up to","exam:91257:zh":"First wash the vegetables, and then cut them into small pieces.","exam:91257:why":"「それから」 is used to connect the next action. 「だから」 shows the result of a cause, but here you are describing the order in which the vegetables are prepared."},"91258":{"example:91258:exzh":"Now then, let's start the class.","example:91258:exzh2":"Well then, see you tomorrow.","grammar:91258:ex:trans":"All right, let's begin class.","grammar:91258:ex:structure":"それでは、〔action〕","grammar:91258:ex:explain":"「それでは」 is the “well then” you say when moving on to a new stage; teachers starting a class and people opening a meeting use it a lot. In casual speech it shortens to 「では」 or, more casually, 「じゃあ」. 「始めます」 means someone actively starts something, so it takes 「授業を」.","grammar:91258:ex:words:0:zh":"that much","grammar:91258:ex:words:0:pos":"conjunction","grammar:91258:ex:words:1:zh":"class","grammar:91258:ex:words:1:note":"じゅぎょう","grammar:91258:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91258:ex:words:2:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91258:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91258:ex:words:3:zh":"begins","grammar:91258:ex:words:3:note":"はじめます; masu-form of 始める","grammar:91258:ex:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91258:ex:points:0":"それでは = well then; used when starting or ending something, and more formal than 「では」 or 「じゃあ」","grammar:91258:ex:points:1":"授業を始める = start class; 「始める」 takes 「を」","grammar:91258:ex2:trans":"Well then, see you tomorrow.","grammar:91258:ex2:structure":"それでは、また〔time〕","grammar:91258:ex2:words:0:zh":"that much","grammar:91258:ex2:words:0:pos":"conjunction","grammar:91258:ex2:words:1:zh":"again","grammar:91258:ex2:words:1:pos":"adverb","grammar:91258:ex2:words:2:zh":"tomorrow","grammar:91258:ex2:words:2:note":"あした","grammar:91258:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91258:ex2:points:0":"それでは、また明日 = well then, see you tomorrow; a set phrase for saying goodbye","grammar:91258:ex2:points:1":"また = again; 「また来週」 = see you next week","exam:91258:zh":"It looks like everyone is ready, so let's start the class.","exam:91258:why":"「それでは」 is used to pick up from the current situation and start the next action. 「しかし」 marks a contrast, and there is nothing contrasting here."},"91259":{"example:91259:exzh":"The embassy is near the station.","example:91259:exzh2":"Where is the Japanese embassy?","grammar:91259:ex:trans":"The embassy is near the station.","grammar:91259:ex:structure":"〔thing〕は＋〔place〕にあります","grammar:91259:ex:explain":"「大使館」 means “embassy”; it's read たいしかん. The pattern 「〜は〜にあります」 says where something is: the topic (大使館) comes first and the location comes last.","grammar:91259:ex:words:0:zh":"Embassy","grammar:91259:ex:words:0:note":"たいしかん","grammar:91259:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91259:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91259:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91259:ex:words:2:zh":"station","grammar:91259:ex:words:2:note":"えき","grammar:91259:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91259:ex:words:3:zh":"'s","grammar:91259:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91259:ex:words:4:zh":"nearby","grammar:91259:ex:words:4:note":"ちかく","grammar:91259:ex:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91259:ex:words:5:zh":"（place of existence）","grammar:91259:ex:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91259:ex:words:6:zh":"exist","grammar:91259:ex:words:6:note":"; the masu-form of ある","grammar:91259:ex:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91259:ex:points:0":"大使館 = the office a country sets up in another country; you go there for visas and other paperwork","grammar:91259:ex:points:1":"〜は〜にあります = ~ is in/at ~ (stating a location)","grammar:91259:ex2:trans":"Where is the Japanese embassy?","grammar:91259:ex2:structure":"〔place〕は＋どこですか","grammar:91259:ex2:words:0:zh":"Japan","grammar:91259:ex2:words:0:note":"にほん","grammar:91259:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91259:ex2:words:1:zh":"Embassy","grammar:91259:ex2:words:1:note":"たいしかん","grammar:91259:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91259:ex2:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91259:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91259:ex2:words:3:zh":"where","grammar:91259:ex2:words:3:pos":"question word","grammar:91259:ex2:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91259:ex2:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91259:ex2:words:5:zh":"(question)","grammar:91259:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91259:ex2:points:0":"日本大使館 = the Japanese embassy; the country name goes directly in front","grammar:91259:ex2:points:1":"〜はどこですか = where is ~?","exam:91259:zh":"I lost my passport abroad, so I called the embassy of my own country.","exam:91259:why":"「大使館」 is a diplomatic office that your country sets up in a foreign country, which fits the situation of asking for help after losing a passport. 「銀行」 handles financial business and can't deal with this kind of passport problem."},"91260":{"example:91260:exzh":"Has anyone come?","example:91260:exzh2":"Could someone give me a hand?","grammar:91260:ex:trans":"Did anyone come?","grammar:91260:ex:structure":"誰か＋〔verb〕か","grammar:91260:ex:explain":"「誰か」 is 「誰」 (who) + 「か」, which shifts the meaning from “who” to “someone, anyone.” So 「誰か来ましたか」 doesn't ask “who came?” — it asks “did anyone come?”, and the answer is 「はい、来ました」 or 「いいえ」. The same rule applies to 何か (something), どこか (somewhere).","grammar:91260:ex:words:0:zh":"someone, anyone","grammar:91260:ex:words:0:note":"だれか","grammar:91260:ex:words:0:pos":"pronoun","grammar:91260:ex:words:1:zh":"came","grammar:91260:ex:words:1:note":"きました; the past form of 来る","grammar:91260:ex:words:1:pos":"verb","grammar:91260:ex:words:2:zh":"(question)","grammar:91260:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91260:ex:points:0":"誰か = someone (an unspecified person); adding 「か」 to 「誰」 turns it into “someone”","grammar:91260:ex:points:1":"誰か來ましたか asks “did anyone come?”, and you answer with 「はい／いいえ」","grammar:91260:ex2:trans":"Could someone give me a hand?","grammar:91260:ex2:structure":"誰か＋〔verb te-form〕ください","grammar:91260:ex2:words:0:zh":"someone, anyone","grammar:91260:ex2:words:0:note":"だれか","grammar:91260:ex2:words:0:pos":"pronoun","grammar:91260:ex2:words:1:zh":"help","grammar:91260:ex2:words:1:note":"てつだって: the te-form of 手伝う","grammar:91260:ex2:words:1:pos":"verb","grammar:91260:ex2:words:2:zh":"please (do ～)","grammar:91260:ex2:words:2:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91260:ex2:points:0":"誰か～てください = someone please ~ (asking an unspecified person who is there for help)","grammar:91260:ex2:points:1":"手伝う = to help (with a task)","exam:91260:zh":"It's too heavy for me to carry alone, so could someone give me a hand?","exam:91260:why":"「誰か」 refers to an unspecified person, which fits asking for help. 「何か」 refers to a thing and can't be the person who helps."},"91261":{"example:91261:exzh":"When is your birthday?","example:91261:exzh2":"I ate cake on my birthday.","grammar:91261:ex:trans":"When is your birthday?","grammar:91261:ex:structure":"〔noun〕は＋いつですか","grammar:91261:ex:explain":"「誕生日」 means birthday; when asking someone else, adding 「お」 and saying 「お誕生日はいつですか」 is more polite. 「いつ」 is the question word for time, and you can answer with 「五月五日です」.","grammar:91261:ex:words:0:zh":"birthday","grammar:91261:ex:words:0:note":"たんじょうび","grammar:91261:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91261:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91261:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91261:ex:words:2:zh":"when","grammar:91261:ex:words:2:pos":"question word","grammar:91261:ex:words:3:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91261:ex:words:3:pos":"copula","grammar:91261:ex:words:4:zh":"(question)","grammar:91261:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91261:ex:points:0":"誕生日 = birthday; to congratulate someone, say 「お誕生日おめでとう」 (happy birthday)","grammar:91261:ex:points:1":"いつですか = when? Used to ask about a date or time","grammar:91261:ex2:trans":"I ate cake on my birthday.","grammar:91261:ex2:structure":"〔special day〕に＋〔action〕","grammar:91261:ex2:words:0:zh":"birthday","grammar:91261:ex2:words:0:note":"たんじょうび","grammar:91261:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91261:ex2:words:1:zh":"(point in time)","grammar:91261:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91261:ex2:words:2:zh":"cake","grammar:91261:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91261:ex2:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91261:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91261:ex2:words:4:zh":"ate","grammar:91261:ex2:words:4:note":"たべました; the past form of 食べる","grammar:91261:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91261:ex2:points:0":"誕生日に = on the birthday; special days take 「に」","grammar:91261:ex2:points:1":"食べました = ate","exam:91261:zh":"I was born on May 3rd, so every year on May 3rd we celebrate a birthday.","exam:91261:why":"「誕生日」 is the day of the year when your birth date comes around. 「お正月」 means New Year and has nothing to do with a May 3rd birth date."},"91262":{"example:91262:exzh":"I go to the office by subway.","example:91262:exzh2":"The subway station is over there.","grammar:91262:ex:trans":"I take the subway to work.","grammar:91262:ex:structure":"〔means of transport〕で＋〔place〕へ行きます","grammar:91262:ex:explain":"「地下鉄」 is a railway that runs underground — the equivalent of a subway or metro. To describe how you commute, use 「〔transportation〕で行きます」; 「で」 marks the means.","grammar:91262:ex:words:0:zh":"subway","grammar:91262:ex:words:0:note":"ちかてつ","grammar:91262:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91262:ex:words:1:zh":"(means of transport)","grammar:91262:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91262:ex:words:2:zh":"company","grammar:91262:ex:words:2:note":"かいしゃ","grammar:91262:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91262:ex:words:3:zh":"(direction)","grammar:91262:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91262:ex:words:4:zh":"go","grammar:91262:ex:words:4:note":"いきます; masu-form of 行く","grammar:91262:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91262:ex:points:1":"Transportation takes 「で」: 地下鉄で行く = go by subway","grammar:91262:ex2:trans":"The subway station is over there.","grammar:91262:ex2:structure":"〔place〕は＋あそこです","grammar:91262:ex2:words:0:zh":"subway","grammar:91262:ex2:words:0:note":"ちかてつ","grammar:91262:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91262:ex2:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91262:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91262:ex2:words:2:zh":"station","grammar:91262:ex2:words:2:note":"えき","grammar:91262:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91262:ex2:words:3:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91262:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91262:ex2:words:4:zh":"there","grammar:91262:ex2:words:4:pos":"pronoun","grammar:91262:ex2:words:5:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91262:ex2:words:5:pos":"copula","grammar:91262:ex2:points:0":"地下鉄の駅 = subway station; 「地下鉄の入口」 (subway entrance) is also common","grammar:91262:ex2:points:1":"あそこ = over there (far from both people); ここ・そこ・あそこ・どこ form a set","exam:91262:zh":"A train that runs underground the whole way from this station to the next one is the subway.","exam:91262:why":"「地下鉄」 is a railway that runs underground. A 「バス」 is also a form of transport, but it isn't a train."},"91263":{"example:91263:exzh":"I put rice into a bowl.","example:91263:exzh2":"This bowl is very old.","grammar:91263:ex:trans":"Put the rice into a bowl.","grammar:91263:ex:structure":"〔container〕に＋〔thing〕を入れます","grammar:91263:ex:explain":"「茶碗」 originally meant a bowl for drinking tea, but in everyday life it now usually means a rice bowl, so don't translate it literally as a teacup. The 「に」 in 「茶碗にご飯を入れる」 marks where the rice goes in.","grammar:91263:ex:words:0:zh":"rice bowl","grammar:91263:ex:words:0:note":"ちゃわん","grammar:91263:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91263:ex:words:1:zh":"(the place it goes into)","grammar:91263:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91263:ex:words:2:zh":"meal","grammar:91263:ex:words:2:note":"ごはん","grammar:91263:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91263:ex:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91263:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91263:ex:words:4:zh":"to serve, to put in","grammar:91263:ex:words:4:note":"いれます; the masu-form of 入れる","grammar:91263:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91263:ex:points:0":"茶碗 = the bowl Japanese families serve rice in (it has 「茶」 in it, but in daily life it usually means a rice bowl)","grammar:91263:ex:points:1":"For serving rice people also say 「ご飯をよそう」; 「入れる」 is the simplest way to say it","grammar:91263:ex2:trans":"This bowl is very old.","grammar:91263:ex2:structure":"〔thing〕は＋とても＋〔adjective〕です","grammar:91263:ex2:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91263:ex2:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91263:ex2:words:1:zh":"bowl","grammar:91263:ex2:words:1:note":"ちゃわん","grammar:91263:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91263:ex2:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91263:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91263:ex2:words:3:zh":"extremely","grammar:91263:ex2:words:3:pos":"adverb","grammar:91263:ex2:words:4:zh":"old, ancient","grammar:91263:ex2:words:4:note":"ふるい","grammar:91263:ex2:words:4:pos":"adjective","grammar:91263:ex2:words:5:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91263:ex2:words:5:pos":"copula","grammar:91263:ex2:points:0":"茶碗 can also mean a tea bowl used in tea ceremony; sentences like this often describe antiques or pottery","grammar:91263:ex2:points:1":"古い = old (for things); you can't use 「古い」 for an older person — say 「年を取った」 instead","exam:91263:zh":"When you eat a meal, you hold the rice bowl in your left hand and use chopsticks with your right hand.","exam:91263:why":"「茶碗」 is a bowl for serving rice, which fits holding the bowl in your left hand and using chopsticks with your right. 「スプーン」 is a spoon, not the container used for rice here."},"91264":{"example:91264:exzh":"From the first of April, I'll go to a new school.","example:91264:exzh2":"I pay the rent on the first of every month.","grammar:91264:ex:trans":"From April 1st I'm going to a new school.","grammar:91264:ex:structure":"〔date〕から＋〔action〕","grammar:91264:ex:explain":"「ついたち」 is the first day of the month, written 「一日」 in kanji — the same characters as 「いちにち」 (one day), but read differently. Japanese schools start in April, so 「四月ついたちから」 is a natural thing to say. Note that 「四月」 is read しがつ, not よんがつ.","grammar:91264:ex:words:0:zh":"April","grammar:91264:ex:words:0:note":"しがつ","grammar:91264:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91264:ex:words:1:zh":"the first (of the month)","grammar:91264:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91264:ex:words:2:zh":"from","grammar:91264:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91264:ex:words:3:zh":"new","grammar:91264:ex:words:3:note":"あたらしい","grammar:91264:ex:words:3:pos":"adjective","grammar:91264:ex:words:4:zh":"school","grammar:91264:ex:words:4:note":"がっこう","grammar:91264:ex:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91264:ex:words:5:zh":"(destination)","grammar:91264:ex:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91264:ex:words:6:zh":"to go (to school)","grammar:91264:ex:words:6:note":"いきます; masu-form of 行く","grammar:91264:ex:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91264:ex:points:0":"ついたち = the first of the month (written 「一日」 in kanji); the same spelling as 「一日」 (いちにち) = one day","grammar:91264:ex:points:1":"〜から = starting from ~; the Japanese school year begins in April","grammar:91264:ex2:trans":"I pay the rent on the first of every month.","grammar:91264:ex2:structure":"毎月〔date〕に＋〔action〕","grammar:91264:ex2:words:0:zh":"every month","grammar:91264:ex2:words:0:note":"まいつき","grammar:91264:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91264:ex2:words:1:zh":"the first (of the month)","grammar:91264:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91264:ex2:words:2:zh":"(point in time)","grammar:91264:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91264:ex2:words:3:zh":"rent","grammar:91264:ex2:words:3:note":"やちん","grammar:91264:ex2:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91264:ex2:words:4:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91264:ex2:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91264:ex2:words:5:zh":"to pay (money)","grammar:91264:ex2:words:5:note":"はらいます; masu-form of 払う","grammar:91264:ex2:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91264:ex2:points:0":"毎月ついたちに = on the first of every month; specific dates take 「に」","grammar:91264:ex2:points:1":"家賃を払う = pay the rent; 「払う」 = to pay (money)","exam:91264:zh":"The day after April 30th is May 1st.","exam:91264:why":"「ついたち」 means the first day of the month. 「ふつか」 is the second, not the day after April 30th."},"91265":{"example:91265:exzh":"I walked a lot today and got tired.","example:91265:exzh2":"If you're tired, please rest a little.","grammar:91265:ex:trans":"I walked a lot today, so I'm tired.","grammar:91265:ex:structure":"〔reason te-form〕＋疲れました","grammar:91265:ex:explain":"「疲れる」 is a verb. English says “I'm tired,” but Japanese usually uses the past form 「疲れました」, because you've already gotten into a tired state. The 「歩いて」 here is the te-form, showing the reason for being tired. In casual speech people often just say 「疲れた」.","grammar:91265:ex:words:0:zh":"today","grammar:91265:ex:words:0:note":"きょう","grammar:91265:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91265:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91265:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91265:ex:words:2:zh":"a lot","grammar:91265:ex:words:2:pos":"adverb","grammar:91265:ex:words:3:zh":"walked, so","grammar:91265:ex:words:3:note":"あるいて; te-form of 歩く","grammar:91265:ex:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91265:ex:words:4:zh":"tired","grammar:91265:ex:words:4:note":"つかれました; past form of 疲れる","grammar:91265:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91265:ex:points:0":"疲れる = to get tired; to say “I'm tired” use the past form 「疲れました」, which shows you've already become tired","grammar:91265:ex:points:1":"The te-form can also show a cause: 歩いて疲れた = got tired from walking","grammar:91265:ex2:trans":"If you're tired, take a short break.","grammar:91265:ex2:structure":"〜たら、〜てください","grammar:91265:ex2:words:0:zh":"if you're tired","grammar:91265:ex2:words:0:note":"つかれたら; 疲れる + たら","grammar:91265:ex2:words:0:pos":"verb","grammar:91265:ex2:words:1:zh":"a little, a bit","grammar:91265:ex2:words:1:note":"すこし","grammar:91265:ex2:words:1:pos":"adverb","grammar:91265:ex2:words:2:zh":"rest","grammar:91265:ex2:words:2:note":"やすんで; te-form of 休む","grammar:91265:ex2:words:2:pos":"verb","grammar:91265:ex2:words:3:zh":"please (do ～)","grammar:91265:ex2:words:3:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91265:ex2:points:0":"疲れたら = if you're tired; the ta-form plus 「ら」 shows a condition","grammar:91265:ex2:points:1":"休んでください = please rest; often used when showing concern for someone","exam:91265:zh":"Walking for a long time makes me tired, so I sit down and rest along the way.","exam:91265:why":"「疲れる」 means your body feels tired, which is why you need to rest. 「休む」 is the act of resting, not the physical state caused by walking for a long time."},"91266":{"example:91266:exzh":"The train arrives in Tokyo at nine.","example:91266:exzh2":"Please call me when you get home.","grammar:91266:ex:trans":"The train arrives in Tokyo at nine.","grammar:91266:ex:structure":"〔place〕に＋着きます","grammar:91266:ex:explain":"「着く」 means to arrive, to reach a place, and the destination takes 「に」. This sentence has two 「に」: 「九時に」 is the time and 「東京に」 is the place you arrive at. Note that 「着く」 and 「着る」 (to wear) share a kanji but differ in reading and meaning.","grammar:91266:ex:words:0:zh":"train","grammar:91266:ex:words:0:note":"でんしゃ","grammar:91266:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91266:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91266:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91266:ex:words:2:zh":"nine o'clock","grammar:91266:ex:words:2:note":"くじ","grammar:91266:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91266:ex:words:3:zh":"(point in time)","grammar:91266:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91266:ex:words:4:zh":"Tokyo","grammar:91266:ex:words:4:note":"とうきょう","grammar:91266:ex:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91266:ex:words:5:zh":"(the destination point)","grammar:91266:ex:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91266:ex:words:6:zh":"arrive","grammar:91266:ex:words:6:note":"つきます; masu-form of 着く","grammar:91266:ex:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91266:ex:points:0":"着く = to arrive; the arrival point takes 「に」: 東京に着く","grammar:91266:ex:points:1":"九時 is read くじ (not きゅうじ)","grammar:91266:ex2:trans":"Call me when you get home.","grammar:91266:ex2:structure":"〔place〕に着いたら、〜てください","grammar:91266:ex2:words:0:zh":"house","grammar:91266:ex2:words:0:note":"いえ","grammar:91266:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91266:ex2:words:1:zh":"(the destination point)","grammar:91266:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91266:ex2:words:2:zh":"after arriving","grammar:91266:ex2:words:2:note":"ついたら; 着く + たら","grammar:91266:ex2:words:2:pos":"verb","grammar:91266:ex2:words:3:zh":"make a phone call","grammar:91266:ex2:words:3:note":"でんわして; te-form of 電話する","grammar:91266:ex2:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91266:ex2:words:4:zh":"please (do ～)","grammar:91266:ex2:words:4:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91266:ex2:points:0":"着いたら = after arriving; the ta-form of 「着く」 is 「着いた」","grammar:91266:ex2:points:1":"電話する = to phone; noun + する makes it a verb"},"91267":{"example:91267:exzh":"My older brother works at a bank.","example:91267:exzh2":"I worked at this company for ten years.","grammar:91267:ex:trans":"My older brother works at a bank.","grammar:91267:ex:structure":"〔company〕に＋勤めています","grammar:91267:ex:explain":"「勤める」 means to work long-term at an organization, and the company name takes 「に」. It differs from 「働く」 (to work, to labor): 「働く」 takes 「で」 for the location, as in 「銀行で働いています」. To describe your current job, use 「勤めています」, not 「勤めます」.","grammar:91267:ex:words:0:zh":"(my) older brother","grammar:91267:ex:words:0:note":"あに","grammar:91267:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91267:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91267:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91267:ex:words:2:zh":"bank","grammar:91267:ex:words:2:note":"ぎんこう","grammar:91267:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91267:ex:words:3:zh":"(where one works)","grammar:91267:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91267:ex:words:4:zh":"to work at ~","grammar:91267:ex:words:4:note":"つとめています; 勤める + ています","grammar:91267:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91267:ex:points:0":"勤める = to be employed at (a company or organization); the place takes 「に」","grammar:91267:ex:points:1":"For your current job, use 「〜ています」, which shows an ongoing state","grammar:91267:ex2:trans":"I worked at this company for ten years.","grammar:91267:ex2:structure":"〔company〕に＋〔period〕勤めました","grammar:91267:ex2:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91267:ex2:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91267:ex2:words:1:zh":"company","grammar:91267:ex2:words:1:note":"かいしゃ","grammar:91267:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91267:ex2:words:2:zh":"(where one works)","grammar:91267:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91267:ex2:words:3:zh":"ten years","grammar:91267:ex2:words:3:note":"じゅうねん","grammar:91267:ex2:words:3:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91267:ex2:words:4:zh":"worked (there)","grammar:91267:ex2:words:4:note":"つとめました; past form of 勤める","grammar:91267:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91267:ex2:points:0":"勤めました = worked (there); the past form shows that the job is already over","grammar:91267:ex2:points:1":"A duration like 「十年」 goes directly before the verb, with no particle","exam:91267:zh":"From next month, my older brother will be working at this company as an employee.","exam:91267:why":"「勤める」 means to be employed at an organization such as a company, used as 「会社に勤める」. 「働く」 is usually used as 「会社で働く」 and can't go directly with 「会社に」 here."},"91268":{"example:91268:exzh":"This movie was boring.","example:91268:exzh2":"This class was boring.","grammar:91268:ex:trans":"This movie was boring.","grammar:91268:ex:structure":"〔thing〕は＋つまらないです","grammar:91268:ex:explain":"「つまらない」 describes movies, books, classes and so on that are boring or don't hold your interest. It's an i-adjective, and its past form is 「つまらなかった」. Note the difference from 「退屈」 (たいくつ): 「退屈」 usually means you yourself are bored with nothing to do.","grammar:91268:ex:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91268:ex:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91268:ex:words:1:zh":"movie","grammar:91268:ex:words:1:note":"えいが","grammar:91268:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91268:ex:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91268:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91268:ex:words:3:zh":"boring","grammar:91268:ex:words:3:pos":"adjective","grammar:91268:ex:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91268:ex:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91268:ex:points:0":"つまらない = boring, dull; the opposite is 「おもしろい」 (interesting)","grammar:91268:ex:points:1":"つまらない is an i-adjective, so 「です」 attaches directly","grammar:91268:ex2:trans":"This class was boring.","grammar:91268:ex2:structure":"〔thing〕は＋〔adjective past〕です","grammar:91268:ex2:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91268:ex2:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91268:ex2:words:1:zh":"class","grammar:91268:ex2:words:1:note":"じゅぎょう","grammar:91268:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91268:ex2:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91268:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91268:ex2:words:3:zh":"was boring","grammar:91268:ex2:words:3:note":"; past form of つまらない","grammar:91268:ex2:words:3:pos":"adjective","grammar:91268:ex2:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91268:ex2:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91268:ex2:points:0":"つまらない → つまらなかったです (change い to かった)","grammar:91268:ex2:points:1":"For a class that's already over, use the past form","exam:91268:zh":"The story of this movie isn't interesting, and watching it is boring.","exam:91268:why":"「つまらない」 means boring or uninteresting, which fits the clue that the story isn't interesting. 「難しい」 means difficult to understand, which isn't the same as boring."},"91269":{"example:91269:exzh":"The exit is that way.","example:91269:exzh2":"Let's meet at the east exit of the station.","grammar:91269:ex:trans":"The exit is that way.","grammar:91269:ex:structure":"〔place〕は＋あちらです","grammar:91269:ex:explain":"「出口」 is read でぐち, not the on-yomi しゅっこう. Its counterpart is 「入口」 (いりぐち). 「あちら」 is the politer way to say “that way,” and you'll often hear it in stations or when a shop clerk gives directions.","grammar:91269:ex:words:0:zh":"export","grammar:91269:ex:words:0:note":"でぐち","grammar:91269:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91269:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91269:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91269:ex:words:2:zh":"over there","grammar:91269:ex:words:2:pos":"pronoun","grammar:91269:ex:words:3:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91269:ex:words:3:pos":"copula","grammar:91269:ex:points:0":"出口 = exit; the opposite is 「入口」 (いりぐち)","grammar:91269:ex:points:1":"あちら = that way, the polite form of 「あそこ」/「あっち」; shop clerks use it a lot","grammar:91269:ex2:trans":"Let's meet at the east exit of the station.","grammar:91269:ex2:structure":"〔place〕で＋会いましょう","grammar:91269:ex2:words:0:zh":"station","grammar:91269:ex2:words:0:note":"えき","grammar:91269:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91269:ex2:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91269:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91269:ex2:words:2:zh":"the east","grammar:91269:ex2:words:2:note":"ひがし","grammar:91269:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91269:ex2:words:3:zh":"'s","grammar:91269:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91269:ex2:words:4:zh":"export","grammar:91269:ex2:words:4:note":"でぐち","grammar:91269:ex2:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91269:ex2:words:5:zh":"(place of the action)","grammar:91269:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91269:ex2:words:6:zh":"let's meet","grammar:91269:ex2:words:6:note":"あいましょう; volitional form of 会う","grammar:91269:ex2:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91269:ex2:points:0":"東の出口 = east exit; 「東口」 (ひがしぐち) is also common","grammar:91269:ex2:points:1":"Use 「で」 for the meeting place; 〜ましょう = let's ~","exam:91269:zh":"I'd like to go outside the station—where is the exit?","exam:91269:why":"「出口」 is the place that leads from inside to outside. 「入り口」 is the place you enter from, the opposite direction from wanting to leave the station."},"91270":{"example:91270:exzh":"Well then, let's begin.","example:91270:exzh2":"Well then, see you next week.","grammar:91270:ex:trans":"Okay, let's begin.","grammar:91270:ex:structure":"では、〔verb〕ましょう","grammar:91270:ex:explain":"「では」 is the “well then” you say when starting something; it's a little shorter than 「それでは」 and more formal than 「じゃあ」. Followed by 「始めましょう」, it invites everyone to start together.","grammar:91270:ex:words:0:zh":"that much","grammar:91270:ex:words:0:pos":"conjunction","grammar:91270:ex:words:1:zh":"let's start","grammar:91270:ex:words:1:note":"はじめましょう; volitional form of 始める","grammar:91270:ex:words:1:pos":"verb","grammar:91270:ex:points:0":"では = well then; a shorter 「それでは」, while casual speech uses 「じゃあ」","grammar:91270:ex:points:1":"〜ましょう = let's ~","grammar:91270:ex2:trans":"Well then, see you next week.","grammar:91270:ex2:structure":"では、また〔time〕","grammar:91270:ex2:words:0:zh":"that much","grammar:91270:ex2:words:0:pos":"conjunction","grammar:91270:ex2:words:1:zh":"again","grammar:91270:ex2:words:1:pos":"adverb","grammar:91270:ex2:words:2:zh":"next week","grammar:91270:ex2:words:2:note":"らいしゅう","grammar:91270:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91270:ex2:points:0":"では、また〜 = see you ~; used when saying goodbye","grammar:91270:ex2:points:1":"来週 = next week","exam:91270:zh":"If there are no more questions, we'll finish the class here.","exam:91270:why":"「では」 picks up the condition just given and announces the end. 「それに」 is used to add information, so it doesn't suit this kind of wrap-up."},"91271":{"example:91271:exzh":"This shop is cheap. But it's far.","example:91271:exzh2":"I want to go. But I don't have time.","grammar:91271:ex:trans":"This shop is cheap. But it's far.","grammar:91271:ex:structure":"〔sentence〕。でも、〔sentence〕","grammar:91271:ex:explain":"「でも」 is a conjunction: the first part gives the good point, “it's cheap,” and then 「でも」 adds the drawback, “it's far.” It goes at the start of a sentence, usually followed by a comma. Note that 「遠い」 is read とおい.","grammar:91271:ex:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91271:ex:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91271:ex:words:1:zh":"store","grammar:91271:ex:words:1:note":"みせ","grammar:91271:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91271:ex:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91271:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91271:ex:words:3:zh":"cheap","grammar:91271:ex:words:3:note":"やすい","grammar:91271:ex:words:3:pos":"adjective","grammar:91271:ex:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91271:ex:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91271:ex:words:5:zh":"but","grammar:91271:ex:words:5:pos":"conjunction","grammar:91271:ex:words:6:zh":"far","grammar:91271:ex:words:6:note":"とおい","grammar:91271:ex:words:6:pos":"adjective","grammar:91271:ex:words:7:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91271:ex:words:7:pos":"copula","grammar:91271:ex:points:0":"でも = but, however; it goes at the start of the second sentence and states something contrasting","grammar:91271:ex:points:1":"でも is casual; in writing or formal situations use 「しかし」","grammar:91271:ex2:trans":"I want to go. But I don't have time.","grammar:91271:ex2:structure":"〔wish〕。でも、〔reason〕","grammar:91271:ex2:words:0:zh":"want to go","grammar:91271:ex2:words:0:note":"いきたい; 行く＋たい","grammar:91271:ex2:words:0:pos":"verb","grammar:91271:ex2:words:1:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91271:ex2:words:1:pos":"copula","grammar:91271:ex2:words:2:zh":"but","grammar:91271:ex2:words:2:pos":"conjunction","grammar:91271:ex2:words:3:zh":"time","grammar:91271:ex2:words:3:note":"じかん","grammar:91271:ex2:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91271:ex2:words:4:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91271:ex2:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91271:ex2:words:5:zh":"don't have","grammar:91271:ex2:words:5:note":"; the negative of ある","grammar:91271:ex2:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91271:ex2:points:0":"でも is often used when refusing or explaining why you can't do something","grammar:91271:ex2:points:1":"時間がありません = I don't have time; the negative of 「ある」 is 「ありません」","exam:91271:zh":"These shoes are cheap. However, they're too small to wear, so I'm not buying them.","exam:91271:why":"「でも」 connects two contrasting situations: although the shoes are cheap, you're not buying them because they don't fit. 「だから」 shows cause and effect, and it can't make being cheap the reason the shoes are too small."},"91272":{"example:91272:exzh":"Why were you late?","example:91272:exzh2":"Why are you studying Japanese?","grammar:91272:ex:trans":"Why were you late?","grammar:91272:ex:structure":"どうして＋〔plain form〕んですか","grammar:91272:ex:explain":"「どうして」 is the question word for asking why. The ending 「〜んですか」 is the casual form of 「のですか」 and shows “I want to know the reason,” so it fits naturally when asking why. You can answer with 「〜から」 to give the reason.","grammar:91272:ex:words:0:zh":"why","grammar:91272:ex:words:0:pos":"question word","grammar:91272:ex:words:1:zh":"was late","grammar:91272:ex:words:1:note":"おくれた; past form of 遅れる","grammar:91272:ex:words:1:pos":"verb","grammar:91272:ex:words:2:zh":"(explanatory tone)","grammar:91272:ex:words:2:note":"; the casual form of 「の」","grammar:91272:ex:words:2:pos":"formal noun","grammar:91272:ex:words:3:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91272:ex:words:3:pos":"copula","grammar:91272:ex:words:4:zh":"(question)","grammar:91272:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91272:ex:points:0":"どうして = why; casual speech can say 「なんで」, and a more formal word is 「なぜ」","grammar:91272:ex:points:1":"〜んですか = asks the other person to explain the reason; very common when asking why","grammar:91272:ex2:trans":"Why are you studying Japanese?","grammar:91272:ex2:structure":"どうして＋〔verb〕ていますか","grammar:91272:ex2:words:0:zh":"why","grammar:91272:ex2:words:0:pos":"question word","grammar:91272:ex2:words:1:zh":"Japanese","grammar:91272:ex2:words:1:note":"にほんご","grammar:91272:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91272:ex2:words:2:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91272:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91272:ex2:words:3:zh":"study","grammar:91272:ex2:words:3:note":"べんきょうして; te-form of 勉強する","grammar:91272:ex2:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91272:ex2:words:4:zh":"(currently, ongoing)","grammar:91272:ex2:words:4:note":"; the masu-form of いる","grammar:91272:ex2:words:4:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91272:ex2:words:5:zh":"(question)","grammar:91272:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91272:ex2:points:0":"どうして simply asks for a reason, and it's more casual than 「なぜ」","grammar:91272:ex2:points:1":"勉強しています = am studying (now); ています shows something in progress","exam:91272:zh":"I want to know why you were absent from school yesterday, so please tell me why you stayed home.","exam:91272:why":"「どうして」 asks about a reason. 「いつ」 asks about a time, but the sentence already says it was yesterday."},"91273":{"example:91273:exzh":"Thank you very much.","example:91273:exzh2":"Thanks for the other day.","grammar:91273:ex:trans":"Thank you very much.","grammar:91273:ex:structure":"どうも＋ありがとうございます","grammar:91273:ex:explain":"「どうも」 goes before 「ありがとうございます」 to make the thanks stronger. On its own it has many uses: a casual “thanks” or a “hi.” With someone older, though, it's best to say the full 「どうもありがとうございます」.","grammar:91273:ex:words:0:zh":"really, truly","grammar:91273:ex:words:0:pos":"adverb","grammar:91273:ex:words:1:zh":"thank you (polite)","grammar:91273:ex:words:1:pos":"set phrase","grammar:91273:ex:points:0":"どうもありがとうございます = thank you very much; 「どうも」 strengthens the gratitude","grammar:91273:ex:points:1":"Just 「どうも」 alone can also be a quick thanks or a greeting","grammar:91273:ex2:trans":"Thanks for the other day.","grammar:91273:ex2:structure":"〔time〕は＋どうも","grammar:91273:ex2:words:0:zh":"the other day","grammar:91273:ex2:words:0:note":"せんじつ","grammar:91273:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91273:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91273:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91273:ex2:words:2:zh":"thank you","grammar:91273:ex2:words:2:note":"; 「ありがとうございました」 is left out","grammar:91273:ex2:words:2:pos":"adverb","grammar:91273:ex2:points:0":"先日はどうも = thanks for the other day; used to thank someone for their help when you meet again","grammar:91273:ex2:points:1":"どうも can stand alone — with just 「どうも」 the other person understands","exam:91273:zh":"Thank you very much for carrying the heavy luggage yesterday.","exam:91273:why":"「どうもありがとうございました」 is a natural way to strengthen your thanks. 「どうぞ」 is used to invite someone or ask them to do something, and it can't be used here to strengthen gratitude."},"91274":{"example:91274:exzh":"Please submit your report by the 10th.","example:91274:exzh2":"I was in the hospital for ten days.","grammar:91274:ex:trans":"Please hand in your report by the tenth.","grammar:91274:ex:structure":"〔deadline〕までに＋〔verb〕てください","grammar:91274:ex:explain":"「十日」 is read とおか, and here it means the tenth of the month. 「までに」 marks a deadline — you have to finish by then; it's different from 「まで」 (up until), and beginners often mix them up. 「レポートを出す」 = hand in a report.","grammar:91274:ex:words:0:zh":"the tenth","grammar:91274:ex:words:0:note":"とおか","grammar:91274:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91274:ex:words:1:zh":"by ~","grammar:91274:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91274:ex:words:2:zh":"report","grammar:91274:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91274:ex:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91274:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91274:ex:words:4:zh":"hand over","grammar:91274:ex:words:4:note":"だして; the te-form of 出す","grammar:91274:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91274:ex:words:5:zh":"please (do ～)","grammar:91274:ex:words:5:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91274:ex:points:0":"十日 is read とおか and can mean either “the tenth” or “ten days”","grammar:91274:ex:points:1":"〜までに = by ~ (a deadline); 〜まで = until ~ (continuation)","grammar:91274:ex2:trans":"I was in the hospital for ten days.","grammar:91274:ex2:structure":"〔number of days〕間＋〔action〕","grammar:91274:ex2:words:0:zh":"ten days","grammar:91274:ex2:words:0:note":"とおか","grammar:91274:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91274:ex2:words:1:zh":"period, duration","grammar:91274:ex2:words:1:note":"かん","grammar:91274:ex2:words:1:pos":"suffix","grammar:91274:ex2:words:2:zh":"was hospitalized","grammar:91274:ex2:words:2:note":"にゅういんしました; past form of 入院する","grammar:91274:ex2:words:2:pos":"verb","grammar:91274:ex2:points:0":"十日間 = ten days (a period); adding 「間」 shows how long something lasts","grammar:91274:ex2:points:1":"入院する = to stay in the hospital; leaving is 「退院」 (たいいん) する","exam:91274:zh":"The day after May 9th is May 10th.","exam:91274:why":"In dates, 「十日」 means the tenth. 「二十日」 is the twentieth, and you shouldn't mix them up just because both contain 「十」."},"91275":{"example:91275:exzh":"I study at the library.","example:91275:exzh2":"The library is closed on Mondays.","grammar:91275:ex:trans":"I study at the library.","grammar:91275:ex:structure":"〔place〕で＋勉強します","grammar:91275:ex:explain":"「図書館」 means library; it's read としょかん. 「勉強する」 in Japanese means “to study,” not “to endure hardship.” Use 「で」 for the place where you do something — that's the big difference from 「に」, which marks existence.","grammar:91275:ex:words:0:zh":"library","grammar:91275:ex:words:0:note":"としょかん","grammar:91275:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91275:ex:words:1:zh":"(place of the action)","grammar:91275:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91275:ex:words:2:zh":"study","grammar:91275:ex:words:2:note":"べんきょうします; masu-form of 勉強する","grammar:91275:ex:words:2:pos":"verb","grammar:91275:ex:points:0":"図書館 = library; 「図書館で本を借りる」 is also very common","grammar:91275:ex:points:1":"Use 「で」 for the place where an action takes place: 図書館で勉強する","grammar:91275:ex2:trans":"The library is closed on Mondays.","grammar:91275:ex2:structure":"〔place〕は＋〔weekday〕が休みです","grammar:91275:ex2:words:0:zh":"library","grammar:91275:ex2:words:0:note":"としょかん","grammar:91275:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91275:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91275:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91275:ex2:words:2:zh":"Monday","grammar:91275:ex2:words:2:note":"げつようび","grammar:91275:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91275:ex2:words:3:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91275:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91275:ex2:words:4:zh":"a day off; closed","grammar:91275:ex2:words:4:note":"やすみ","grammar:91275:ex2:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91275:ex2:words:5:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91275:ex2:words:5:pos":"copula","grammar:91275:ex2:points:0":"図書館は月曜日が休みです = the library is closed on Mondays; the 「AはBが〜」 pattern","grammar:91275:ex2:points:1":"休み = a day off; closed","exam:91275:zh":"I went to the library to borrow three books and return them two weeks later.","exam:91275:why":"「図書館」 offers both borrowing and returning books. 「本屋」 is a place to buy books, which doesn't fit borrowing books and then returning them."},"91276":{"example:91276:exzh":"Which would you like, coffee or tea?","example:91276:exzh2":"What country are you from?","grammar:91276:ex:trans":"Coffee or tea — which would you like?","grammar:91276:ex:structure":"AとB、どちらがいいですか","grammar:91276:ex:explain":"「どちら」 is used when choosing between two things and means 'which one'; it is polite and very common, while casual speech says 「どっち」. When a question word is the subject it takes 「が」, so the sentence is 「どちらがいいですか」. For three or more options, switch to 「どれ」.","grammar:91276:ex:words:0:zh":"coffee","grammar:91276:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91276:ex:words:1:zh":"with","grammar:91276:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91276:ex:words:2:zh":"black tea","grammar:91276:ex:words:2:note":"こうちゃ","grammar:91276:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91276:ex:words:3:zh":"which one","grammar:91276:ex:words:3:pos":"question word","grammar:91276:ex:words:4:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91276:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91276:ex:words:5:zh":"good","grammar:91276:ex:words:5:pos":"adjective","grammar:91276:ex:words:6:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91276:ex:words:6:pos":"copula","grammar:91276:ex:words:7:zh":"(question)","grammar:91276:ex:words:7:pos":"particle","grammar:91276:ex:points:0":"どちら = which one (of two); used when choosing between two options","grammar:91276:ex:points:1":"Answers: 「コーヒーがいいです」 or 「どちらでもいいです」 (either is fine)","grammar:91276:ex2:trans":"What country are you from?","grammar:91276:ex2:structure":"〔noun〕は＋どちらですか","grammar:91276:ex2:words:0:zh":"(your) country","grammar:91276:ex2:words:0:note":"おくに","grammar:91276:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91276:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91276:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91276:ex2:words:2:zh":"where","grammar:91276:ex2:words:2:pos":"question word","grammar:91276:ex2:words:3:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91276:ex2:words:3:pos":"copula","grammar:91276:ex2:words:4:zh":"(question)","grammar:91276:ex2:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91276:ex2:points:0":"どちら is also a polite way to say 「どこ」: お国はどちらですか = what country are you from?","grammar:91276:ex2:points:1":"お国: the 「お」 makes it more polite","exam:91276:zh":"There are two bags, a red one and a blue one — which do you like?","exam:91276:why":"「どちら」 asks which of two options. 「どこ」 asks about a place, so it can't ask which bag you like."},"91277":{"example:91277:exzh":"The cat is meowing.","example:91277:exzh2":"The birds were singing in the morning.","grammar:91277:ex:trans":"The cat is meowing.","grammar:91277:ex:structure":"〔animal〕が＋〔onomatopoeia〕＋鳴いています","grammar:91277:ex:explain":"「鳴く」 is used specifically for the sounds animals make — cats, dogs, birds, cicadas. For a person crying, the same-sounding word is written 「泣く」, so you tell them apart by the kanji or the subject. 「鳴いています」 means it is in the middle of making that sound.","grammar:91277:ex:words:0:zh":"cat","grammar:91277:ex:words:0:note":"ねこ","grammar:91277:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91277:ex:words:1:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91277:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91277:ex:words:2:zh":"meow meow","grammar:91277:ex:words:2:pos":"onomatopoeia","grammar:91277:ex:words:3:zh":"is in the middle of calling","grammar:91277:ex:words:3:note":"ないています；鳴く＋ています","grammar:91277:ex:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91277:ex:points:0":"鳴く = the sound animals, birds and insects make; a person crying is the homophone 「泣く」, written with a different kanji","grammar:91277:ex:points:1":"The onomatopoeia goes before the verb: にゃあにゃあ鳴く (a cat), ワンワン鳴く (a dog)","grammar:91277:ex2:trans":"The birds were chirping in the morning.","grammar:91277:ex2:structure":"〔time〕、〔animal〕が＋鳴いていました","grammar:91277:ex2:words:0:zh":"morning","grammar:91277:ex2:words:0:note":"あさ","grammar:91277:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91277:ex2:words:1:zh":"bird","grammar:91277:ex2:words:1:note":"とり","grammar:91277:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91277:ex2:words:2:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91277:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91277:ex2:words:3:zh":"(at that time) was calling","grammar:91277:ex2:words:3:note":"ないていました；鳴く＋ていました","grammar:91277:ex2:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91277:ex2:points:0":"鳥が鳴く = a bird chirps; the subject of 鳴く is an animal","grammar:91277:ex2:points:1":"〜ていました = was doing ~ at some point in the past","exam:91277:zh":"Every morning I can hear the birds chirping in the garden.","exam:91277:why":"「鳴く」 means birds or other animals making sounds, which fits 「チュンチュン」. 「飛ぶ」 means to fly, which can't express birds chirping."},"91278":{"example:91278:exzh":"I lost a wallet.","example:91278:exzh2":"Please don't lose the important key.","grammar:91278:ex:trans":"I lost a wallet.","grammar:91278:ex:structure":"〔thing〕を＋なくしました","grammar:91278:ex:explain":"「なくす」 means to lose something through your own carelessness; it can also be written 「無くす」 in kanji. If you want to say a thing disappeared on its own, use 「財布がなくなりました」. If you lose something while travelling, you can tell the 「交番」 (police box): 「財布をなくしました」.","grammar:91278:ex:words:0:zh":"wallet","grammar:91278:ex:words:0:note":"さいふ","grammar:91278:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91278:ex:words:1:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91278:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91278:ex:words:2:zh":"lost","grammar:91278:ex:words:2:note":"; the past form of なくす","grammar:91278:ex:words:2:pos":"verb","grammar:91278:ex:points:0":"なくす = to lose something; when a thing 'goes missing', use the intransitive verb 「なくなる」","grammar:91278:ex:points:1":"With なくす you are the one who lost it, so it takes 「を」","grammar:91278:ex2:trans":"Please don't lose an important key.","grammar:91278:ex2:structure":"〔thing〕を＋〜ないでください","grammar:91278:ex2:words:0:zh":"important","grammar:91278:ex2:words:0:note":"だいじな","grammar:91278:ex2:words:0:pos":"na-adjective","grammar:91278:ex2:words:1:zh":"key","grammar:91278:ex2:words:1:note":"かぎ","grammar:91278:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91278:ex2:words:2:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91278:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91278:ex2:words:3:zh":"don't lose (it)","grammar:91278:ex2:words:3:note":"; the nai-form of なくす plus で","grammar:91278:ex2:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91278:ex2:words:4:zh":"please (do ～)","grammar:91278:ex2:words:4:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91278:ex2:points:0":"The nai-form of なくす is なくさない; add 「でください」 = please don't ~","grammar:91278:ex2:points:1":"大事な＋noun = important ~; a na-adjective needs 「な」 to modify a noun","exam:91278:zh":"Losing a wallet while traveling would be a problem, so I'll put it deep in my bag first.","exam:91278:why":"「なくす」 means to lose something, which fits putting the wallet away so it isn't lost. 「貸す」 means to lend, and tucking it deep in a bag isn't about avoiding lending it out."},"91279":{"example:91279:exzh":"I went to the beach during summer vacation.","example:91279:exzh2":"When does summer vacation start?","grammar:91279:ex:trans":"I went to the beach during summer vacation.","grammar:91279:ex:structure":"〔time〕に＋〔place〕へ行きました","grammar:91279:ex:explain":"「夏休み」 is the school summer holiday, and it can also mean a company's summer break. Here 「夏休みに」 marks what was done during summer vacation. Where English says 'go to the seaside', Japanese simply says 「海へ行く」 — literally 'go to the sea'.","grammar:91279:ex:words:0:zh":"summer vacation","grammar:91279:ex:words:0:note":"なつやすみ","grammar:91279:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91279:ex:words:1:zh":"(point in time)","grammar:91279:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91279:ex:words:2:zh":"seaside","grammar:91279:ex:words:2:note":"うみ","grammar:91279:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91279:ex:words:3:zh":"(direction)","grammar:91279:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91279:ex:words:4:zh":"went","grammar:91279:ex:words:4:note":"いきました; the past form of 行く","grammar:91279:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91279:ex:points:0":"夏休み = summer vacation (夏 + 休み); winter break is 「冬休み」 and spring break is 「春休み」","grammar:91279:ex:points:1":"夏休みに = during summer vacation; 「海へ行く」 = go to the beach","grammar:91279:ex2:trans":"When does summer vacation start?","grammar:91279:ex2:structure":"〔noun〕は＋いつからですか","grammar:91279:ex2:words:0:zh":"summer vacation","grammar:91279:ex2:words:0:note":"なつやすみ","grammar:91279:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91279:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91279:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91279:ex2:words:2:zh":"when","grammar:91279:ex2:words:2:pos":"question word","grammar:91279:ex2:words:3:zh":"from","grammar:91279:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91279:ex2:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91279:ex2:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91279:ex2:words:5:zh":"(question)","grammar:91279:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91279:ex2:points:0":"夏休みはいつからですか = when does summer vacation start?","grammar:91279:ex2:points:1":"いつから = from when; いつまで = until when","exam:91279:zh":"At this school, summer vacation runs from the end of July through all of August, so there are no classes.","exam:91279:why":"「夏休み」 is the long summer holiday and fits the period from the end of July through August. 「春休み」 is spring break, a different season."},"91280":{"example:91280:exzh":"I bought seven eggs.","example:91280:exzh2":"My little brother is seven.","grammar:91280:ex:trans":"I bought seven eggs.","grammar:91280:ex:structure":"〔thing〕を＋〔quantity〕＋買いました","grammar:91280:ex:explain":"「七つ」 is read ななつ, the native Japanese counting system. Eggs, fruit, snacks and similar things can all be counted with 「〜つ」. The structure is 'thing を + quantity + verb' — this word order is the basic pattern for counting in Japanese.","grammar:91280:ex:words:0:zh":"egg","grammar:91280:ex:words:0:note":"たまご","grammar:91280:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91280:ex:words:1:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91280:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91280:ex:words:2:zh":"seven (things)","grammar:91280:ex:words:2:note":"ななつ","grammar:91280:ex:words:2:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91280:ex:words:3:zh":"bought","grammar:91280:ex:words:3:note":"かいました; the past tense of 買う","grammar:91280:ex:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91280:ex:points:0":"七つ = seven (of something); read ななつ, not しちつ","grammar:91280:ex:points:1":"The quantity goes directly before the verb","grammar:91280:ex2:trans":"My little brother is seven.","grammar:91280:ex2:structure":"〔person〕は＋〔age〕です","grammar:91280:ex2:words:0:zh":"(my) younger brother","grammar:91280:ex2:words:0:note":"おとうと","grammar:91280:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91280:ex2:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91280:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91280:ex2:words:2:zh":"seven years old","grammar:91280:ex2:words:2:note":"ななつ","grammar:91280:ex2:words:2:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91280:ex2:words:3:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91280:ex2:words:3:pos":"copula","grammar:91280:ex2:points:0":"七つ can also mean 'seven years old'; it is commonly used for children's ages","grammar:91280:ex2:points:1":"You can also say 「七歳（ななさい）です」","exam:91280:zh":"There are five apples and two mandarins, so there are seven pieces of fruit in total.","exam:91280:why":"Five plus two is 「七つ」. 「六つ」 means six, which is one short."},"91281":{"example:91281:exzh":"July 7th is Tanabata.","example:91281:exzh2":"I traveled around Japan for seven days.","grammar:91281:ex:trans":"July 7th is Tanabata.","grammar:91281:ex:structure":"〔date〕は＋〔holiday〕です","grammar:91281:ex:explain":"「七日」 is read なのか and means the seventh day of the month. Japan's Tanabata falls on July 7th of the solar calendar, when people write their wishes on strips of paper and hang them on bamboo branches — it does not carry the same meaning as Valentine's Day in Taiwan. The sentence uses 「〜は〜です」 to introduce the holiday.","grammar:91281:ex:words:0:zh":"July","grammar:91281:ex:words:0:note":"しちがつ","grammar:91281:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91281:ex:words:1:zh":"the seventh day; the 7th","grammar:91281:ex:words:1:note":"なのか","grammar:91281:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91281:ex:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91281:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91281:ex:words:3:zh":"Tanabata (the Star Festival)","grammar:91281:ex:words:3:note":"たなばた","grammar:91281:ex:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91281:ex:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91281:ex:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91281:ex:points:0":"七日 is read なのか and has two meanings: 'the 7th' and 'seven days'","grammar:91281:ex:points:1":"七月 is read しちがつ; 七夕 is read たなばた (an irregular reading)","grammar:91281:ex2:trans":"I travelled around Japan for seven days.","grammar:91281:ex2:structure":"〔number of days〕間、〔place〕を旅行しました","grammar:91281:ex2:words:0:zh":"seven days","grammar:91281:ex2:words:0:note":"なのか","grammar:91281:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91281:ex2:words:1:zh":"period, duration","grammar:91281:ex2:words:1:note":"かん","grammar:91281:ex2:words:1:pos":"suffix","grammar:91281:ex2:words:2:zh":"Japan","grammar:91281:ex2:words:2:note":"にほん","grammar:91281:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91281:ex2:words:3:zh":"(the area passed through or moved around)","grammar:91281:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91281:ex2:words:4:zh":"traveled","grammar:91281:ex2:words:4:note":"りょこうしました；旅行する的過去式","grammar:91281:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91281:ex2:points:0":"七日間 = seven days; adding 「間」 marks a period of time","grammar:91281:ex2:points:1":"「日本を旅行する」: 「を」 marks moving around within an area","exam:91281:zh":"Today is June 6th, so tomorrow is June 7th.","exam:91281:why":"「七日」 means the seventh of the month, and the day after June 6th is June 7th. 「八日」 is the 8th, one day later still."},"91282":{"example:91282:exzh":"People are lining up in front of the station.","example:91282:exzh2":"Please line up in a single file.","grammar:91282:ex:trans":"People are lined up in front of the station.","grammar:91282:ex:structure":"〔place〕に＋〔people〕が並んでいます","grammar:91282:ex:explain":"「並ぶ」 is an intransitive verb: people or things line up by themselves, so the subject takes 「が」. People in Japan queue a lot, so 「並んでいます」 often describes the sight of a line. Its paired transitive verb is 「並べる」 (to line things up).","grammar:91282:ex:words:0:zh":"station","grammar:91282:ex:words:0:note":"えき","grammar:91282:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91282:ex:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91282:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91282:ex:words:2:zh":"front","grammar:91282:ex:words:2:note":"まえ","grammar:91282:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91282:ex:words:3:zh":"（place of existence）","grammar:91282:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91282:ex:words:4:zh":"person","grammar:91282:ex:words:4:note":"ひと","grammar:91282:ex:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91282:ex:words:5:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91282:ex:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91282:ex:words:6:zh":"is standing in line","grammar:91282:ex:words:6:note":"ならんでいます；並ぶ＋ています","grammar:91282:ex:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91282:ex:points:0":"並ぶ = to line up, to form a line; people or things line up by themselves (intransitive verb), so it takes 「が」","grammar:91282:ex:points:1":"並んでいます = a line has formed (a state); the location takes 「に」","grammar:91282:ex2:trans":"Please line up in single file.","grammar:91282:ex2:structure":"〔form〕に＋並んでください","grammar:91282:ex2:words:0:zh":"a single file; one line","grammar:91282:ex2:words:0:note":"いちれつ","grammar:91282:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91282:ex2:words:1:zh":"(the resulting form)","grammar:91282:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91282:ex2:words:2:zh":"lining up","grammar:91282:ex2:words:2:note":"ならんで；並ぶ的て形","grammar:91282:ex2:words:2:pos":"verb","grammar:91282:ex2:words:3:zh":"please (do ～)","grammar:91282:ex2:words:3:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91282:ex2:points:0":"一列に並ぶ = line up in single file; 「に」 shows the shape they form","grammar:91282:ex2:points:1":"並んでください = please line up; you hear it often at stations and events","exam:91282:zh":"Many people are buying tickets, so we decided to line up at the back of the queue too.","exam:91282:why":"「並ぶ」 means to line up, which goes naturally with 「列の後ろに」. 「登る」 means to climb up high and doesn't mean joining a queue."},"91283":{"example:91283:exzh":"I set the plates out on the desk.","example:91283:exzh2":"I arranged the books in order of size.","grammar:91283:ex:trans":"I set the plates out on the desk.","grammar:91283:ex:structure":"〔place〕に＋〔thing〕を並べます","grammar:91283:ex:explain":"「並べる」 is when someone lines things up one by one, so the thing takes 「皿を」. The place you put them takes 「に」. It pairs with 「並ぶ」: 「並ぶ」 is lining up by yourself, 「並べる」 is lining things up. Also, 「机」 usually means a desk; a dining table is normally 「テーブル」.","grammar:91283:ex:words:0:zh":"desk","grammar:91283:ex:words:0:note":"つくえ","grammar:91283:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91283:ex:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91283:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91283:ex:words:2:zh":"on top of","grammar:91283:ex:words:2:note":"うえ","grammar:91283:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91283:ex:words:3:zh":"(the place where they are placed)","grammar:91283:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91283:ex:words:4:zh":"dish","grammar:91283:ex:words:4:note":"さら","grammar:91283:ex:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91283:ex:words:5:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91283:ex:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91283:ex:words:6:zh":"to arrange; to line up","grammar:91283:ex:words:6:note":"ならべます；並べる的ます形","grammar:91283:ex:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91283:ex:points:0":"並べる = (someone) lines things up, sets things out; a transitive verb, so it takes 「を」","grammar:91283:ex:points:1":"Pairs with 「並ぶ」 (to line up by oneself): 人が並ぶ / 皿を並べる","grammar:91283:ex2:trans":"I arranged the books in order of size.","grammar:91283:ex2:structure":"〔thing〕を＋〔basis〕の順に並べます","grammar:91283:ex2:words:0:zh":"book","grammar:91283:ex2:words:0:note":"ほん","grammar:91283:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91283:ex2:words:1:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91283:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91283:ex2:words:2:zh":"size","grammar:91283:ex2:words:2:note":"おおきさ","grammar:91283:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91283:ex2:words:3:zh":"'s","grammar:91283:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91283:ex2:words:4:zh":"order","grammar:91283:ex2:words:4:note":"じゅん","grammar:91283:ex2:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91283:ex2:words:5:zh":"(the order used)","grammar:91283:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91283:ex2:words:6:zh":"arranged (them)","grammar:91283:ex2:words:6:note":"ならべました；並べる的過去式","grammar:91283:ex2:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91283:ex2:points:0":"〜の順に並べる = to arrange in the order of ~","grammar:91283:ex2:points:1":"大きさ = size; drop the 「い」 from the adjective 「大きい」 and add 「さ」 to make a noun","exam:91283:zh":"Before taking a photo, I decided to line the dolls up in a row.","exam:91283:why":"「並べる」 means to arrange things in a certain order or position and often goes with 「一列に」. 「集める」 just means to gather them together and doesn't mean lining them up in a row."},"91284":{"example:91284:exzh":"This town is very lively.","example:91284:exzh2":"I like lively places.","grammar:91284:ex:trans":"This town is very lively.","grammar:91284:ex:structure":"〔place〕は＋とても＋にぎやかです","grammar:91284:ex:explain":"「にぎやか」 describes a place full of people and sound, buzzing with energy — it can be used for streets, cities or an atmosphere. It is a na-adjective, written 「賑やか」 in kanji. Its opposite 「静か」 is also a na-adjective.","grammar:91284:ex:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91284:ex:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91284:ex:words:1:zh":"town","grammar:91284:ex:words:1:note":"まち","grammar:91284:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91284:ex:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91284:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91284:ex:words:3:zh":"extremely","grammar:91284:ex:words:3:pos":"adverb","grammar:91284:ex:words:4:zh":"busy, lively","grammar:91284:ex:words:4:pos":"na-adjective","grammar:91284:ex:words:5:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91284:ex:words:5:pos":"copula","grammar:91284:ex:points:0":"にぎやか = lively, crowded and full of energy; the opposite is 「静か」 (quiet)","grammar:91284:ex:points:1":"にぎやか is a na-adjective and takes 「です」 directly","grammar:91284:ex2:trans":"I like lively places.","grammar:91284:ex2:structure":"〔noun〕が＋好きです","grammar:91284:ex2:words:0:zh":"lively","grammar:91284:ex2:words:0:note":"; にぎやか＋な modifies a noun","grammar:91284:ex2:words:0:pos":"na-adjective","grammar:91284:ex2:words:1:zh":"place","grammar:91284:ex2:words:1:note":"ところ","grammar:91284:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91284:ex2:words:2:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91284:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91284:ex2:words:3:zh":"to like","grammar:91284:ex2:words:3:note":"すき","grammar:91284:ex2:words:3:pos":"na-adjective","grammar:91284:ex2:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91284:ex2:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91284:ex2:points:0":"にぎやかな所 = a lively place; a na-adjective needs 「な」 to modify a noun","grammar:91284:ex2:points:1":"〜が好きです = to like ~; the thing you like takes 「が」","exam:91284:zh":"There are lots of people in front of the station and you can hear everyone talking, so it's very lively.","exam:91284:why":"「にぎやか」 means lively, with many people and voices. 「静か」 means quiet, the opposite of the clues in the sentence."},"91285":{"example:91285:exzh":"The sun sets in the west.","example:91285:exzh2":"There is a park west of the station.","grammar:91285:ex:trans":"The sun sets in the west.","grammar:91285:ex:structure":"〔thing〕は＋〔direction〕に沈みます","grammar:91285:ex:explain":"「西」 is a direction noun read にし. Japanese describes the sun going down as 「西に沈む」 (sink to the west), using 「に」 for the direction it sinks toward — not 'from the west' as English would put it.","grammar:91285:ex:words:0:zh":"sun","grammar:91285:ex:words:0:note":"たいよう","grammar:91285:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91285:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91285:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91285:ex:words:2:zh":"the west","grammar:91285:ex:words:2:note":"にし","grammar:91285:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91285:ex:words:3:zh":"(the destination)","grammar:91285:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91285:ex:words:4:zh":"to sink; to go down","grammar:91285:ex:words:4:note":"しずみます；沈む的ます形","grammar:91285:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91285:ex:points:0":"西 = west; the four directions are ひがし、にし、みなみ、きた","grammar:91285:ex:points:1":"太陽が西に沈む: Japanese uses 「に」 (sinking to the west), where English says 'sets in the west'","grammar:91285:ex2:trans":"There is a park west of the station.","grammar:91285:ex2:structure":"〔place〕の西に＋〔thing〕があります","grammar:91285:ex2:words:0:zh":"station","grammar:91285:ex2:words:0:note":"えき","grammar:91285:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91285:ex2:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91285:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91285:ex2:words:2:zh":"the west","grammar:91285:ex2:words:2:note":"にし","grammar:91285:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91285:ex2:words:3:zh":"（place of existence）","grammar:91285:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91285:ex2:words:4:zh":"park","grammar:91285:ex2:words:4:note":"こうえん","grammar:91285:ex2:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91285:ex2:words:5:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91285:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91285:ex2:words:6:zh":"there is / are","grammar:91285:ex2:words:6:note":"; the masu-form of ある","grammar:91285:ex2:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91285:ex2:points:0":"駅の西 = west of the station; station exits are also often called 「西口（にしぐち）」","grammar:91285:ex2:points:1":"〜に〜があります = there is something somewhere","exam:91285:zh":"The sun rises in the east and sets in the west in the evening.","exam:91285:why":"「西」 is the direction the sun sets in the evening. 「東」 is the direction it rises in, so it can't go here."},"91286":{"example:91286:exzh":"On Sundays I rest at home.","example:91286:exzh2":"Would you like to go out together this Sunday?","grammar:91286:ex:trans":"I rest at home on Sundays.","grammar:91286:ex:structure":"〔weekday〕は＋〔place〕で休みます","grammar:91286:ex:explain":"「日曜日」 is Sunday; here 「は」 raises it as the topic, describing a habit on that day. In 「家で休みます」 the 「で」 marks the place where the action happens; if you just mean 'stay at home', say 「家にいます」.","grammar:91286:ex:words:0:zh":"Sunday","grammar:91286:ex:words:0:note":"にちようび","grammar:91286:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91286:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91286:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91286:ex:words:2:zh":"house","grammar:91286:ex:words:2:note":"いえ","grammar:91286:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91286:ex:words:3:zh":"(place of the action)","grammar:91286:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91286:ex:words:4:zh":"rest","grammar:91286:ex:words:4:note":"やすみます: the masu-form of 休む.","grammar:91286:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91286:ex:points:0":"日曜日 = Sunday; 「日曜日は」 makes Sunday the topic, meaning 'as for Sundays'","grammar:91286:ex:points:1":"The 「で」 in 家で休みます marks the place where the action happens","grammar:91286:ex2:trans":"Do you want to go out together this Sunday?","grammar:91286:ex2:structure":"〜ませんか (invitation)","grammar:91286:ex2:words:0:zh":"this time; next time","grammar:91286:ex2:words:0:note":"こんど","grammar:91286:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91286:ex2:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91286:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91286:ex2:words:2:zh":"Sunday","grammar:91286:ex2:words:2:note":"にちようび","grammar:91286:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91286:ex2:words:3:zh":"together","grammar:91286:ex2:words:3:note":"いっしょ","grammar:91286:ex2:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91286:ex2:words:4:zh":"(made adverbial)","grammar:91286:ex2:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91286:ex2:words:5:zh":"(not) go out","grammar:91286:ex2:words:5:note":"でかけません；出かける的否定","grammar:91286:ex2:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91286:ex2:words:6:zh":"(question)","grammar:91286:ex2:words:6:pos":"particle","grammar:91286:ex2:points:0":"今度の日曜日 = this coming Sunday","grammar:91286:ex2:points:1":"〜ませんか = would you like to ~? A more polite invitation than 「〜ましょう」","exam:91286:zh":"Today is Saturday, so tomorrow is Sunday.","exam:91286:why":"The day after Saturday is 「日曜日」. 「月曜日」 is Monday, one more day later."},"91287":{"example:91287:exzh":"This tea is lukewarm (not hot enough).","example:91287:exzh2":"Lukewarm beer doesn't taste good.","grammar:91287:ex:trans":"This tea is lukewarm (not hot enough).","grammar:91287:ex:structure":"〔thing〕は＋ぬるいです","grammar:91287:ex:explain":"「ぬるい」 is used when the temperature is neither one thing nor the other — tea or bath water that is not hot enough, or a drink that is not cold enough. It usually carries a negative feeling. If the temperature feels just right and pleasant, use 「温かい（あたたかい）」.","grammar:91287:ex:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91287:ex:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91287:ex:words:1:zh":"tea","grammar:91287:ex:words:1:note":"おちゃ","grammar:91287:ex:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91287:ex:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91287:ex:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91287:ex:words:3:zh":"lukewarm (not hot enough)","grammar:91287:ex:words:3:pos":"adjective","grammar:91287:ex:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91287:ex:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91287:ex:points:0":"ぬるい = not hot enough when it should be hot, not cold enough when it should be cold; it carries a complaint that the temperature is wrong","grammar:91287:ex:points:1":"ぬるい is an i-adjective; for 'warm and pleasant' use 「温かい」","grammar:91287:ex2:trans":"Beer that is not cold does not taste good.","grammar:91287:ex2:structure":"ぬるい＋〔noun〕は＋〜くないです","grammar:91287:ex2:words:0:zh":"not cold; lukewarm","grammar:91287:ex2:words:0:pos":"adjective","grammar:91287:ex2:words:1:zh":"beer","grammar:91287:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91287:ex2:words:2:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91287:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91287:ex2:words:3:zh":"does not taste good","grammar:91287:ex2:words:3:note":"; the negative of おいしい","grammar:91287:ex2:words:3:pos":"adjective","grammar:91287:ex2:words:4:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91287:ex2:words:4:pos":"copula","grammar:91287:ex2:points:0":"ぬるいビール = beer that has gone warm; a cold drink that has reached room temperature is also called 「ぬるい」","grammar:91287:ex2:points:1":"Negative of an i-adjective: おいしい → おいしくないです","exam:91287:zh":"This tea is neither hot nor cold—it's lukewarm.","exam:91287:why":"「ぬるい」 means a liquid isn't hot enough or is only slightly warm. 「涼しい」 usually describes cool weather or surroundings and isn't used for the temperature of this tea."},"91288":{"example:91288:exzh":"I write kanji in a notebook.","example:91288:exzh2":"I bought three new notebooks.","grammar:91288:ex:trans":"I write kanji in a notebook.","grammar:91288:ex:structure":"〔place〕に＋〔thing〕を書きます","grammar:91288:ex:explain":"「ノート」 is a notebook you write in, not 'notes' as in the content you write down. The place you write goes with 「に」, and what you write goes with 「を」. In Japan, practising kanji is often described as 「ノートに漢字を書く」.","grammar:91288:ex:words:0:zh":"notebook","grammar:91288:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91288:ex:words:1:zh":"(the place where you write)","grammar:91288:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91288:ex:words:2:zh":"kanji","grammar:91288:ex:words:2:note":"かんじ","grammar:91288:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91288:ex:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91288:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91288:ex:words:4:zh":"write","grammar:91288:ex:words:4:note":"かきます；書く的ます形","grammar:91288:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91288:ex:points:0":"ノート = notebook; a laptop is 「ノートパソコン」","grammar:91288:ex:points:1":"Use 「に」 for where you write: ノートに書く、紙に書く","grammar:91288:ex2:trans":"I bought three new notebooks.","grammar:91288:ex2:structure":"〔thing〕を＋〔quantity〕＋買いました","grammar:91288:ex2:words:0:zh":"new","grammar:91288:ex2:words:0:note":"あたらしい","grammar:91288:ex2:words:0:pos":"adjective","grammar:91288:ex2:words:1:zh":"notebook","grammar:91288:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91288:ex2:words:2:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91288:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91288:ex2:words:3:zh":"three (notebooks)","grammar:91288:ex2:words:3:note":"さんさつ","grammar:91288:ex2:words:3:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91288:ex2:words:4:zh":"bought","grammar:91288:ex2:words:4:note":"かいました; the past tense of 買う","grammar:91288:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91288:ex2:points:0":"ノート is counted with 「冊（さつ）」, the same as books","grammar:91288:ex2:points:1":"The quantity goes before the verb: 三冊買いました","exam:91288:zh":"To use in class, I bought a new notebook whose pages are all still blank.","exam:91288:why":"「ノート」 is a notebook for writing in, which fits the clue about blank pages. A 「辞書」 already has words and explanations printed in it."},"91289":{"example:91289:exzh":"Do you have an ashtray?","example:91289:exzh2":"There's an ashtray on the table.","grammar:91289:ex:trans":"Do you have an ashtray?","grammar:91289:ex:structure":"〔thing〕は＋ありますか","grammar:91289:ex:explain":"「灰皿」 is the dish that holds cigarette ash; when 「皿（さら）」 follows another word it is read ざら. 「〜はありますか」 is the simplest way to ask 'do you have ~?' in a shop or at someone's house.","grammar:91289:ex:words:0:zh":"ashtray","grammar:91289:ex:words:0:note":"はいざら","grammar:91289:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91289:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic, asking whether it exists)","grammar:91289:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91289:ex:words:2:zh":"there is / are","grammar:91289:ex:words:2:note":"; the masu-form of ある","grammar:91289:ex:words:2:pos":"verb","grammar:91289:ex:words:3:zh":"(question)","grammar:91289:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91289:ex:points:0":"灰皿 = ashtray (灰 + 皿, with 皿 read ざら)","grammar:91289:ex:points:1":"〜はありますか = do you have ~? Often used in shops when asking for something or checking whether something is available","grammar:91289:ex2:trans":"There is an ashtray on the table.","grammar:91289:ex2:structure":"〔place〕の上に＋〔thing〕があります","grammar:91289:ex2:words:0:zh":"desk","grammar:91289:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91289:ex2:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91289:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91289:ex2:words:2:zh":"on top of","grammar:91289:ex2:words:2:note":"うえ","grammar:91289:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91289:ex2:words:3:zh":"（place of existence）","grammar:91289:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91289:ex2:words:4:zh":"ashtray","grammar:91289:ex2:words:4:note":"はいざら","grammar:91289:ex2:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91289:ex2:words:5:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91289:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91289:ex2:words:6:zh":"there is / are","grammar:91289:ex2:words:6:note":"; the masu-form of ある","grammar:91289:ex2:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91289:ex2:points:0":"テーブルの上 = on top of the table; 「上・下・中」 after 「の」 show position","grammar:91289:ex2:points:1":"灰皿 is a thing, so it takes 「あります」","exam:91289:zh":"I put an ashtray on the table so I could knock the ash off my cigarette.","exam:91289:why":"「灰皿」 is a container used specifically for cigarette ash and butts. 「茶碗」 is a rice bowl and doesn't fit the use described here."},"91290":{"example:91290:exzh":"I sent a postcard to a friend from the place I was traveling.","example:91290:exzh2":"Please give me five postcards.","grammar:91290:ex:trans":"I sent a postcard to a friend from the place I was travelling.","grammar:91290:ex:structure":"〔place〕から＋〔person〕に＋はがきを出しました","grammar:91290:ex:explain":"「はがき」 is a postcard (written 「葉書」 in kanji); the New Year cards sent in Japan, 「年賀状」, are also a kind of はがき. To send a letter or postcard you use 「出す」. 「旅行先」 means 'the place you travelled to' — 「先」 marks the destination.","grammar:91290:ex:words:0:zh":"the place you travelled to","grammar:91290:ex:words:0:note":"りょこうさき","grammar:91290:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91290:ex:words:1:zh":"from","grammar:91290:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91290:ex:words:2:zh":"friend","grammar:91290:ex:words:2:note":"ともだち","grammar:91290:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91290:ex:words:3:zh":"(object)","grammar:91290:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91290:ex:words:4:zh":"postcard","grammar:91290:ex:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91290:ex:words:5:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91290:ex:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91290:ex:words:6:zh":"sent out","grammar:91290:ex:words:6:note":"だしました；出す的過去式","grammar:91290:ex:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91290:ex:points:0":"はがき = postcard; sending one is 「はがきを出す」","grammar:91290:ex:points:1":"〔person〕にはがきを出す: 「に」 marks who you send it to; 「から」 marks where it is sent from","grammar:91290:ex2:trans":"Five postcards, please.","grammar:91290:ex2:structure":"〔thing〕を＋〔quantity〕ください","grammar:91290:ex2:words:0:zh":"postcard","grammar:91290:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91290:ex2:words:1:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91290:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91290:ex2:words:2:zh":"five","grammar:91290:ex2:words:2:note":"ごまい","grammar:91290:ex2:words:2:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91290:ex2:words:3:zh":"please (do ～)","grammar:91290:ex2:words:3:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91290:ex2:points:0":"はがき is counted with 「枚（まい）」, as are all thin, flat things","grammar:91290:ex2:points:1":"What you say to buy postcards at a post office","exam:91290:zh":"From the place I was traveling, I sent a friend a postcard — one you can send without putting it in an envelope by writing a short message on the back.","exam:91290:why":"With a 「はがき」 you can write the message and the address directly on it and send it, with no envelope needed. A 「手紙」 usually goes in an envelope, which is a different form from what's described here."},"91291":{"example:91291:exzh":"Japanese people eat rice with chopsticks.","example:91291:exzh2":"Please give me two pairs of chopsticks.","grammar:91291:ex:trans":"Japanese people eat rice with chopsticks.","grammar:91291:ex:structure":"〔tool〕で＋〔action〕","grammar:91291:ex:explain":"「箸」 means chopsticks, read はし; it sounds the same as 「橋」 (bridge) but the pitch accent is different. To mark the tool you use, add 「で」, so 「箸で食べます」 = eat with chopsticks.","grammar:91291:ex:words:0:zh":"Japanese","grammar:91291:ex:words:0:note":"にほんじん","grammar:91291:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91291:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91291:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91291:ex:words:2:zh":"chopsticks","grammar:91291:ex:words:2:note":"はし","grammar:91291:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91291:ex:words:3:zh":"(means, tool)","grammar:91291:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91291:ex:words:4:zh":"meal","grammar:91291:ex:words:4:note":"ごはん","grammar:91291:ex:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91291:ex:words:5:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91291:ex:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91291:ex:words:6:zh":"eat","grammar:91291:ex:words:6:note":"たべます; masu-form (polite form) of 「食べる」","grammar:91291:ex:words:6:pos":"verb","grammar:91291:ex:points:1":"Add 「で」 for the tool you use: 箸で食べる","grammar:91291:ex2:trans":"Two pairs of chopsticks, please.","grammar:91291:ex2:structure":"〔thing〕を＋〔quantity〕ください","grammar:91291:ex2:words:0:zh":"chopsticks","grammar:91291:ex2:words:0:note":"はし","grammar:91291:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91291:ex2:words:1:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91291:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91291:ex2:words:2:zh":"two pairs","grammar:91291:ex2:words:2:note":"にぜん","grammar:91291:ex2:words:2:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91291:ex2:words:3:zh":"please (do ～)","grammar:91291:ex2:words:3:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91291:ex2:points:1":"The quantity goes before 「ください」","exam:91291:zh":"In Japan, people often eat rice using chopsticks, which are two thin sticks.","exam:91291:why":"「箸」 consists of two thin sticks and is used to pick up food. 「フォーク」 is tableware too, but it isn't shaped like two thin sticks."},"91292":{"example:91292:exzh":"I'm twenty years old.","example:91292:exzh2":"In Japan, you can drink alcohol from the age of twenty.","grammar:91292:ex:trans":"I am twenty.","grammar:91292:ex:structure":"〔person〕は＋〔age〕です","grammar:91292:ex:explain":"「二十歳」 has a special reading, はたち. Twenty was once the age of adulthood in Japan, so the word carries special weight. Other ages are counted with 「〜歳（さい）」, but twenty is usually read はたち.","grammar:91292:ex:words:0:zh":"I","grammar:91292:ex:words:0:note":"わたし","grammar:91292:ex:words:0:pos":"pronoun","grammar:91292:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91292:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91292:ex:words:2:zh":"twenty years old","grammar:91292:ex:words:2:note":"はたち","grammar:91292:ex:words:2:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91292:ex:words:3:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91292:ex:words:3:pos":"copula","grammar:91292:ex:points:0":"二十歳 is read はたち (an irregular reading); it can also be read にじゅっさい, but はたち is the most common","grammar:91292:ex:points:1":"For age, use 「〜歳です」","grammar:91292:ex2:trans":"In Japan you can drink alcohol from the age of twenty.","grammar:91292:ex2:structure":"〔place〕では＋〔age〕から＋〜が〔potential form〕","grammar:91292:ex2:words:0:zh":"Japan","grammar:91292:ex2:words:0:note":"にほん","grammar:91292:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91292:ex2:words:1:zh":"(scope, place)","grammar:91292:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91292:ex2:words:2:zh":"(contrastive topic)","grammar:91292:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91292:ex2:words:3:zh":"twenty years old","grammar:91292:ex2:words:3:note":"はたち","grammar:91292:ex2:words:3:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91292:ex2:words:4:zh":"from","grammar:91292:ex2:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91292:ex2:words:5:zh":"alcohol, sake","grammar:91292:ex2:words:5:note":"おさけ","grammar:91292:ex2:words:5:pos":"noun","grammar:91292:ex2:words:6:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91292:ex2:words:6:pos":"particle","grammar:91292:ex2:words:7:zh":"can drink","grammar:91292:ex2:words:7:note":"のめます；飲む的可能形","grammar:91292:ex2:words:7:pos":"verb","grammar:91292:ex2:points:0":"二十歳から = from the age of twenty; Japanese law says you must be twenty to drink or smoke","grammar:91292:ex2:points:1":"With the potential form 「飲める」 the object usually takes 「が」: お酒が飲めます","exam:91292:zh":"My older sister is nineteen, and she'll turn twenty on her next birthday.","exam:91292:why":"A nineteen-year-old who has her next birthday becomes 「二十歳」. 「二十一歳」 is an age she'll reach the year after that."},"91293":{"example:91293:exzh":"Payday is on the 20th.","example:91293:exzh2":"It took me twenty days to read this book.","grammar:91293:ex:trans":"The salary is paid on the 20th.","grammar:91293:ex:structure":"〔date〕に＋給料が出ます","grammar:91293:ex:explain":"「二十日」 is read はつか, another special reading among dates (11–19 and most dates from the 21st on use 「〜にち」). In 「給料が出る」, 「出る」 means the salary 'comes out', that is, gets paid; the salary is the subject, so it takes 「が」.","grammar:91293:ex:words:0:zh":"the 20th","grammar:91293:ex:words:0:note":"はつか","grammar:91293:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91293:ex:words:1:zh":"(point in time)","grammar:91293:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91293:ex:words:2:zh":"salary","grammar:91293:ex:words:2:note":"きゅうりょう","grammar:91293:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91293:ex:words:3:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91293:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91293:ex:words:4:zh":"is paid (salary)","grammar:91293:ex:words:4:note":"でます; the masu-form (polite form) of 出る","grammar:91293:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91293:ex:points:0":"二十日 is read はつか (an irregular reading) and means both 'the 20th' and 'twenty days'","grammar:91293:ex:points:1":"給料が出る = the salary is paid; you can also say 「給料日」 (payday)","grammar:91293:ex2:trans":"It took twenty days to read this book.","grammar:91293:ex2:structure":"〔plain-form verb〕のに＋〔time〕かかりました","grammar:91293:ex2:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91293:ex2:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91293:ex2:words:1:zh":"book","grammar:91293:ex2:words:1:note":"ほん","grammar:91293:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91293:ex2:words:2:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91293:ex2:words:2:pos":"particle","grammar:91293:ex2:words:3:zh":"read","grammar:91293:ex2:words:3:note":"よむ","grammar:91293:ex2:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91293:ex2:words:4:zh":"'s","grammar:91293:ex2:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91293:ex2:words:5:zh":"(for ~; in doing ~)","grammar:91293:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91293:ex2:words:6:zh":"twenty days","grammar:91293:ex2:words:6:note":"はつか","grammar:91293:ex2:words:6:pos":"noun","grammar:91293:ex2:words:7:zh":"took (time)","grammar:91293:ex2:words:7:note":"; the past tense of かかる","grammar:91293:ex2:words:7:pos":"verb","grammar:91293:ex2:points:0":"Here 二十日 means 'twenty days'","grammar:91293:ex2:points:1":"〜のに＋時間がかかる = it takes a certain amount of time to do ~","exam:91293:zh":"Today is April 19th, so tomorrow is April 20th.","exam:91293:why":"「二十日」 means the twentieth of the month and is the day after the 19th. 「十日」 is the 10th, which doesn't match the date in the question."},"91294":{"example:91294:exzh":"I stick a stamp on the envelope.","example:91294:exzh2":"I put a poster on the wall.","grammar:91294:ex:trans":"I stick a stamp on the envelope.","grammar:91294:ex:structure":"〔place〕に＋〔thing〕を貼ります","grammar:91294:ex:explain":"「貼る」 means to stick something on — a stamp, a poster, a sticker. Remember the position of both particles in the pattern 「〔place〕に〔thing〕を貼る」. 「切手」 means a postage stamp; do not read it literally as 'a cut hand'.","grammar:91294:ex:words:0:zh":"envelope","grammar:91294:ex:words:0:note":"ふうとう","grammar:91294:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91294:ex:words:1:zh":"(the place where it is attached)","grammar:91294:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91294:ex:words:2:zh":"stamp","grammar:91294:ex:words:2:note":"きって","grammar:91294:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91294:ex:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91294:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91294:ex:words:4:zh":"paste","grammar:91294:ex:words:4:note":"はります；貼る的ます形","grammar:91294:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91294:ex:points:0":"貼る = to stick on; typical combinations: 切手を貼る、ポスターを貼る、シールを貼る","grammar:91294:ex:points:1":"The place you stick it takes 「に」, and the thing you stick takes 「を」","grammar:91294:ex2:trans":"I put a poster on the wall.","grammar:91294:ex2:structure":"〔place〕に＋〔thing〕を貼りました","grammar:91294:ex2:words:0:zh":"wall","grammar:91294:ex2:words:0:note":"かべ","grammar:91294:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91294:ex2:words:1:zh":"(the place where it is attached)","grammar:91294:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91294:ex2:words:2:zh":"poster","grammar:91294:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91294:ex2:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91294:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91294:ex2:words:4:zh":"attached","grammar:91294:ex2:words:4:note":"はりました；貼る的過去式","grammar:91294:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91294:ex2:points:0":"壁にポスターを貼る = to put a poster on the wall","grammar:91294:ex2:points:1":"貼りました = stuck on (past)","exam:91294:zh":"Before sticking the stamp on the envelope, moisten the back of the stamp a little.","exam:91294:why":"「貼る」 means to stick on; for a stamp and an envelope you say 「切手を封筒に貼る」. 「切る」 means to cut and doesn't mean to stick."},"91295":{"example:91295:exzh":"I always eat bread in the morning.","example:91295:exzh2":"The bread at this bakery is delicious.","grammar:91295:ex:trans":"I always eat bread in the morning.","grammar:91295:ex:structure":"〔time〕は＋いつも＋〔thing〕を食べます","grammar:91295:ex:explain":"「パン」 means bread, a loanword that entered Japanese very early. 「朝は」 uses 「は」 to make the morning the topic, hinting 'in the morning it is like this' (lunch and dinner may be different). 「いつも」 expresses a habit.","grammar:91295:ex:words:0:zh":"morning","grammar:91295:ex:words:0:note":"あさ","grammar:91295:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91295:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91295:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91295:ex:words:2:zh":"always","grammar:91295:ex:words:2:pos":"adverb","grammar:91295:ex:words:3:zh":"bread","grammar:91295:ex:words:3:pos":"noun","grammar:91295:ex:words:4:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91295:ex:words:4:pos":"particle","grammar:91295:ex:words:5:zh":"eat","grammar:91295:ex:words:5:note":"たべます; masu-form (polite form) of 「食べる」","grammar:91295:ex:words:5:pos":"verb","grammar:91295:ex:points:0":"パン = bread (from Portuguese); sliced sandwich bread is 「食パン」","grammar:91295:ex:points:1":"朝は = as for the morning; 「は」 contrasts it with other times of day","grammar:91295:ex2:trans":"The bread from this bakery is delicious.","grammar:91295:ex2:structure":"〔shop〕の＋〔thing〕は＋おいしいです","grammar:91295:ex2:words:0:zh":"this","grammar:91295:ex2:words:0:pos":"pre-noun adjectival","grammar:91295:ex2:words:1:zh":"bread","grammar:91295:ex2:words:1:pos":"noun","grammar:91295:ex2:words:2:zh":"~ shop","grammar:91295:ex2:words:2:note":"や; after a noun it means a shop that sells that thing","grammar:91295:ex2:words:2:pos":"suffix","grammar:91295:ex2:words:3:zh":"'s","grammar:91295:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91295:ex2:words:4:zh":"bread","grammar:91295:ex2:words:4:pos":"noun","grammar:91295:ex2:words:5:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91295:ex2:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91295:ex2:words:6:zh":"delicious","grammar:91295:ex2:words:6:pos":"adjective","grammar:91295:ex2:words:7:zh":"is (polite form)","grammar:91295:ex2:words:7:pos":"copula","grammar:91295:ex2:points:1":"この〔shop〕の〔thing〕 = this shop's ~","exam:91295:zh":"This morning I toasted thin slices of bread, spread butter on them, and ate them with milk.","exam:91295:why":"「パン」 fits the breakfast described—sliced, toasted, and buttered. 「ご飯」 is cooked rice and can't be sliced thin like the food described here."},"91296":{"example:91296:exzh":"I wipe my hands with a handkerchief.","example:91296:exzh2":"I left a handkerchief on the train.","grammar:91296:ex:trans":"I wipe my hands with a handkerchief.","grammar:91296:ex:structure":"〔tool〕で＋〔thing〕をふきます","grammar:91296:ex:explain":"「ハンカチ」 is a handkerchief, a small item everyone carries in Japan. 「ふく」 means to wipe; in kanji it is 「拭く」, but it is often written in hiragana. The pattern 「〜で〜をふく」 = wipe ~ with ~.","grammar:91296:ex:words:0:zh":"handkerchief","grammar:91296:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91296:ex:words:1:zh":"(means, tool)","grammar:91296:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91296:ex:words:2:zh":"hand","grammar:91296:ex:words:2:note":"て","grammar:91296:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91296:ex:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91296:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91296:ex:words:4:zh":"rub","grammar:91296:ex:words:4:note":"; the masu-form of ふく","grammar:91296:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91296:ex:points:0":"ハンカチ = handkerchief; people in Japan usually carry one because many restrooms have no paper towels","grammar:91296:ex:points:1":"Add 「で」 for the tool you use; ふく for wiping is written 「拭く」","grammar:91296:ex2:trans":"I left a handkerchief on the train.","grammar:91296:ex2:structure":"〔place〕に＋〔thing〕を忘れました","grammar:91296:ex2:words:0:zh":"train","grammar:91296:ex2:words:0:note":"でんしゃ","grammar:91296:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91296:ex2:words:1:zh":"(the place where it was left)","grammar:91296:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91296:ex2:words:2:zh":"handkerchief","grammar:91296:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91296:ex2:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91296:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91296:ex2:words:4:zh":"forgot (to take it with me)","grammar:91296:ex2:words:4:note":"わすれました；忘れる的過去式","grammar:91296:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91296:ex2:points:0":"ハンカチを忘れる = to forget and leave a handkerchief behind","grammar:91296:ex2:points:1":"Use 「に」 for the place where you left something: 電車に忘れる","exam:91296:zh":"After washing my hands, I took a small cloth handkerchief out of my pocket and wiped my hands dry.","exam:91296:why":"A 「ハンカチ」 is a handkerchief you can keep in a pocket and use to wipe your hands. A 「シャツ」 is also made of cloth, but it isn't what you use to wipe your hands in this situation."},"91297":{"example:91297:exzh":"What did you have for dinner?","example:91297:exzh2":"I eat dinner at seven.","grammar:91297:ex:trans":"What did you have for dinner?","grammar:91297:ex:structure":"〔noun〕は＋何を食べましたか","grammar:91297:ex:explain":"「晩ご飯」 means dinner, and people also often say 「夕ご飯（ゆうごはん）」. This sentence first makes dinner the topic with 「は」, then asks 「何を食べましたか」 (what did you eat) — a very common way to ask in everyday conversation.","grammar:91297:ex:words:0:zh":"dinner","grammar:91297:ex:words:0:note":"ばんごはん","grammar:91297:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91297:ex:words:1:zh":"(topic)","grammar:91297:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91297:ex:words:2:zh":"what","grammar:91297:ex:words:2:note":"なに","grammar:91297:ex:words:2:pos":"question word","grammar:91297:ex:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91297:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91297:ex:words:4:zh":"ate","grammar:91297:ex:words:4:note":"たべました; the past form of 食べる","grammar:91297:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91297:ex:words:5:zh":"(question)","grammar:91297:ex:words:5:pos":"particle","grammar:91297:ex:points:0":"晩ご飯＝dinner; learn 朝ご飯 (breakfast), 昼ご飯 (lunch), and 晩ご飯 (dinner) as a set","grammar:91297:ex:points:1":"何を〜ましたか＝what did you …? Used to ask about something done in the past","grammar:91297:ex2:trans":"I eat dinner at seven.","grammar:91297:ex2:structure":"〔time〕に＋〔noun〕を食べます","grammar:91297:ex2:words:0:zh":"seven o'clock","grammar:91297:ex2:words:0:note":"しちじ","grammar:91297:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91297:ex2:words:1:zh":"(point in time)","grammar:91297:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91297:ex2:words:2:zh":"dinner","grammar:91297:ex2:words:2:note":"ばんごはん","grammar:91297:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91297:ex2:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91297:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91297:ex2:words:4:zh":"eat","grammar:91297:ex2:words:4:note":"たべます; masu-form (polite form) of 「食べる」","grammar:91297:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91297:ex2:points:0":"晩ご飯を食べる＝eat dinner","grammar:91297:ex2:points:1":"七時に: add 「に」 to a specific time; 七時 is read しちじ"},"91298":{"example:91298:exzh":"I go to Okinawa by plane.","example:91298:exzh2":"I bought a plane ticket.","grammar:91298:ex:trans":"I go to Okinawa by plane.","grammar:91298:ex:structure":"〔means of transport〕で＋〔place〕へ行きます","grammar:91298:ex:explain":"「飛行機」 means airplane (flight + machine). To say how you get somewhere, use 「〔means of transport〕で行く」; if you just mean the action of getting on board, use 「飛行機に乗る」. Okinawa is an island in southern Japan, and many people fly there.","grammar:91298:ex:words:0:zh":"plane","grammar:91298:ex:words:0:note":"ひこうき","grammar:91298:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91298:ex:words:1:zh":"(means of transport)","grammar:91298:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91298:ex:words:2:zh":"Okinawa","grammar:91298:ex:words:2:note":"おきなわ","grammar:91298:ex:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91298:ex:words:3:zh":"(direction)","grammar:91298:ex:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91298:ex:words:4:zh":"go","grammar:91298:ex:words:4:note":"いきます; masu-form of 行く","grammar:91298:ex:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91298:ex:points:0":"飛行機＝airplane; 「飛行機で行く」＝go by plane, 「飛行機に乗る」＝board a plane","grammar:91298:ex:points:1":"The means of transport takes 「で」; the destination takes 「へ」 or 「に」","grammar:91298:ex2:trans":"I bought a plane ticket.","grammar:91298:ex2:structure":"〔noun〕の切符を買いました","grammar:91298:ex2:words:0:zh":"plane","grammar:91298:ex2:words:0:note":"ひこうき","grammar:91298:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91298:ex2:words:1:zh":"'s","grammar:91298:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91298:ex2:words:2:zh":"tickets","grammar:91298:ex2:words:2:note":"きっぷ","grammar:91298:ex2:words:2:pos":"noun","grammar:91298:ex2:words:3:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91298:ex2:words:3:pos":"particle","grammar:91298:ex2:words:4:zh":"bought","grammar:91298:ex2:words:4:note":"かいました; the past tense of 買う","grammar:91298:ex2:words:4:pos":"verb","grammar:91298:ex2:points:0":"飛行機の切符＝plane ticket; people also often say 「航空券（こうくうけん）」","grammar:91298:ex2:points:1":"切符＝ticket (train tickets, etc.); train tickets are also 「切符」","exam:91298:zh":"I got on a plane at the airport and saw the town from up in the sky.","exam:91298:why":"The vehicle you board at an airport and can look down at the town from is a 「飛行機」. A 「船」 travels on water, which doesn't match the clue about the sky."},"91299":{"example:91299:exzh":"Please give me one apple.","example:91299:exzh2":"I have one question.","grammar:91299:ex:trans":"One apple, please.","grammar:91299:ex:structure":"〔thing〕を＋一つ＋ください","grammar:91299:ex:explain":"「一つ」 is read 「ひとつ」 and is the first of the native Japanese counting words, which can count most things. The quantity goes before 「ください」, so 「りんごを一つください」 means one apple, please.","grammar:91299:ex:words:0:zh":"apple","grammar:91299:ex:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91299:ex:words:1:zh":"(object marker)","grammar:91299:ex:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91299:ex:words:2:zh":"one","grammar:91299:ex:words:2:note":"ひとつ","grammar:91299:ex:words:2:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91299:ex:words:3:zh":"please (do ～)","grammar:91299:ex:words:3:pos":"auxiliary verb","grammar:91299:ex:points:0":"一つ＝one; native Japanese numbers: ひとつ、ふたつ、みっつ……","grammar:91299:ex:points:1":"Most useful when ordering or shopping: 「これを一つください」","grammar:91299:ex2:trans":"I have one question.","grammar:91299:ex2:structure":"〔noun〕が＋一つあります","grammar:91299:ex2:words:0:zh":"question","grammar:91299:ex2:words:0:note":"しつもん","grammar:91299:ex2:words:0:pos":"noun","grammar:91299:ex2:words:1:zh":"(nominative)","grammar:91299:ex2:words:1:pos":"particle","grammar:91299:ex2:words:2:zh":"one","grammar:91299:ex2:words:2:note":"ひとつ","grammar:91299:ex2:words:2:pos":"numeral with counter","grammar:91299:ex2:words:3:zh":"there is / are","grammar:91299:ex2:words:3:note":"; the masu-form of ある","grammar:91299:ex2:words:3:pos":"verb","grammar:91299:ex2:points:0":"一つ can also count abstract things: 質問が一つ, お願いが一つ","grammar:91299:ex2:points:1":"質問＝question; 「問題」 in Japanese usually means an exam question or a problem/trouble","exam:91299:zh":"There were three apples in the box, and after eating two, one is left.","exam:91299:why":"「一つ」 means one, and three minus two leaves one. 「二つ」 is how many you ate, not how many are left."}};